window.FRENCH_PRACTICE_CONTENT = {"chapters":[{"number":1,"slug":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives","title":"Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives","documents":[{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/README.md","title":"Chapter 1 Learning Pack: Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-learning-pack-nouns-articles-and-adjectives\">Chapter 1 Learning Pack: Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion course for Chapter 1 of <em>Easy French Step-by-Step</em>. It teaches the chapter's concepts without reproducing the book.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Recognize masculine and feminine nouns.</li>\n<li>Choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</li>\n<li>Choose <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</li>\n<li>Form common noun plurals.</li>\n<li>Make descriptive adjectives agree in gender and number.</li>\n<li>Place common adjectives before or after the noun.</li>\n<li>Use colors and nationalities accurately.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"best-learning-order\">Best learning order</h2>\n<p>Use the materials in this order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one short lesson in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons/</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Read the side-by-side English meaning, then say every French example aloud.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching section in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">practice/writing-drills.md</code></span> by hand.</li>\n<li>Close the lesson and answer from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching exercises in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">practice/exercises.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Correct your work with <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">practice/answer-key.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Record errors in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review/error-log.md</code></span>.</li>\n<li>Follow the review schedule in <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review/7-day-plan.md</code></span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The existing <span class=\"reference-link\"><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">audio-script.md</code></span>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tts-narration.txt</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chapter-01-audio-script.docx</code> provide listening and pronunciation practice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"does-writing-help-you-learn-french-faster\">Does writing help you learn French faster?</h2>\n<p>Yes, especially when writing is combined with <strong>retrieval and correction</strong>. Copying a paragraph repeatedly is less effective than trying to produce a phrase from memory and then checking it.</p>\n<p>The most useful cycle is:</p>\n<blockquote>See it → say it → cover it → write it from memory → check it → rewrite only the correction</blockquote>\n<p>Handwriting can slow you down enough to notice accents, gender, endings, and word order. However, writing alone will not train listening or pronunciation. A balanced beginner session should include:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Activity</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Learn one small rule</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Say examples aloud</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Write from memory</td>\n<td>10 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complete exercises</td>\n<td>10 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Review mistakes</td>\n<td>5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-target\">Mastery target</h2>\n<p>Move to Chapter 2 when you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>score at least <strong>85%</strong> on the mixed review without looking at notes;</li>\n<li>explain why an article or adjective form is correct;</li>\n<li>write five original noun phrases with correct agreement;</li>\n<li>read those phrases aloud comfortably.</li>\n</ul>","search":"# chapter 1 learning pack: nouns, articles, and adjectives this is an original companion course for chapter 1 of *easy french step-by-step*. it teaches the chapter's concepts without reproducing the book. ## what you will learn 1. recognize masculine and feminine nouns. 2. choose `le`, `la`, `l'`, and `les`. 3. choose `un`, `une`, and `des`. 4. form common noun plurals. 5. make descriptive adjectives agree in gender and number. 6. place common adjectives before or after the noun. 7. use colors and nationalities accurately. ## best learning order use the materials in this order: 1. read one short lesson in [`lessons/`](lessons/). 2. read the side-by-side english meaning, then say every french example aloud. 3. complete the matching section in [`practice/writing-drills.md`](practice/writing-drills.md) by hand. 4. close the lesson and answer from memory. 5. complete the matching exercises in [`practice/exercises.md`](practice/exercises.md). 6. correct your work with [`practice/answer-key.md`](practice/answer-key.md). 7. record errors in [`review/error-log.md`](review/error-log.md). 8. follow the review schedule in [`review/7-day-plan.md`](review/7-day-plan.md). the existing [`audio-script.md`](audio-script.md), `tts-narration.txt`, and `chapter-01-audio-script.docx` provide listening and pronunciation practice. ## does writing help you learn french faster? yes, especially when writing is combined with **retrieval and correction**. copying a paragraph repeatedly is less effective than trying to produce a phrase from memory and then checking it. the most useful cycle is: > see it -> say it -> cover it -> write it from memory -> check it -> rewrite only the correction handwriting can slow you down enough to notice accents, gender, endings, and word order. however, writing alone will not train listening or pronunciation. a balanced beginner session should include: | activity | time | |---|---:| | learn one small rule | 5 minutes | | say examples aloud | 5 minutes | | write from memory | 10 minutes | | complete exercises | 10 minutes | | review mistakes | 5 minutes | ## mastery target move to chapter 2 when you can: - score at least **85%** on the mixed review without looking at notes; - explain why an article or adjective form is correct; - write five original noun phrases with correct agreement; - read those phrases aloud comfortably. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/practice/writing-drills.md","title":"Handwriting and Active-Recall Drills","type":"Writing","html":"<h1 id=\"handwriting-and-active-recall-drills\">Handwriting and Active-Recall Drills</h1>\n<p>Use paper if possible. Do not copy while looking. Study the model, cover it, write it from memory, then compare.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-1-article-noun-chunks\">Drill 1: Article + noun chunks</h2>\n<p>Write each chunk three times. On the third repetition, cover the model.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quartier</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la cuisine</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'appartement</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m.</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'université</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f.</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les fenêtres</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un voisin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voisine</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then write the English meaning beside each phrase without using a dictionary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-2-four-corner-agreement\">Drill 2: Four-corner agreement</h2>\n<p>For each adjective, build all four forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"model\">Model</h3>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit jardin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite chambre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petits jardins</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites chambres</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"your-turn\">Your turn</h3>\n<p>Use these adjectives:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Choose your own masculine and feminine nouns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-3-singular-to-plural-transformations\">Drill 3: Singular-to-plural transformations</h2>\n<p>Write the singular phrase, cover it, and produce the plural:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chaise rouge</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel étudiant</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le beau tableau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la petite rue</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal actif</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"drill-4-dictation\">Drill 4: Dictation</h2>\n<p>Have your computer or pronunciation extension read these aloud. Do not look while writing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande maison blanche</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants français</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux quartier tranquille</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nouvelles voitures rouges</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une amie canadienne</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Check articles, accents, adjective position, gender, and plural endings separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-5-memory-sentences\">Drill 5: Memory sentences</h2>\n<p>Study each sentence for fifteen seconds. Hide it and write it from memory:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La petite bibliothèque est dans une rue tranquille.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les nouveaux étudiants ont des cahiers bleus.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Une voisine française habite dans le vieil immeuble.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Circle every article. Underline every noun. Draw an arrow from each adjective to its noun.</p>\n<h2 id=\"drill-6-personal-production\">Drill 6: Personal production</h2>\n<p>Write six original sentences:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe one room.</li>\n<li>Describe one person.</li>\n<li>Describe two objects.</li>\n<li>Use a color.</li>\n<li>Use a nationality.</li>\n<li>Use one adjective before the noun and another after it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Read the sentences aloud and correct them before adding new vocabulary.</p>","search":"# handwriting and active-recall drills use paper if possible. do not copy while looking. study the model, cover it, write it from memory, then compare. ## drill 1: article + noun chunks write each chunk three times. on the third repetition, cover the model. 1. `le quartier` 2. `la cuisine` 3. `l'appartement` (`m.`) 4. `l'université` (`f.`) 5. `les fenêtres` 6. `un voisin` 7. `une voisine` 8. `des étudiants` then write the english meaning beside each phrase without using a dictionary. ## drill 2: four-corner agreement for each adjective, build all four forms. ### model `petit` 1. `un petit jardin` 2. `une petite chambre` 3. `des petits jardins` 4. `des petites chambres` ### your turn use these adjectives: 1. `grand` 2. `sportif` 3. `heureux` 4. `canadien` choose your own masculine and feminine nouns. ## drill 3: singular-to-plural transformations write the singular phrase, cover it, and produce the plural: 1. `une chaise rouge` 2. `un nouvel étudiant` 3. `le beau tableau` 4. `la petite rue` 5. `un animal actif` ## drill 4: dictation have your computer or pronunciation extension read these aloud. do not look while writing: 1. `une grande maison blanche` 2. `des étudiants français` 3. `un vieux quartier tranquille` 4. `les nouvelles voitures rouges` 5. `une amie canadienne` check articles, accents, adjective position, gender, and plural endings separately. ## drill 5: memory sentences study each sentence for fifteen seconds. hide it and write it from memory: 1. `la petite bibliothèque est dans une rue tranquille.` 2. `les nouveaux étudiants ont des cahiers bleus.` 3. `une voisine française habite dans le vieil immeuble.` circle every article. underline every noun. draw an arrow from each adjective to its noun. ## drill 6: personal production write six original sentences: 1. describe one room. 2. describe one person. 3. describe two objects. 4. use a color. 5. use a nationality. 6. use one adjective before the noun and another after it. read the sentences aloud and correct them before adding new vocabulary. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/practice/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 1 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-exercises\">Chapter 1 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Do these without notes. Mark uncertain answers with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">?</code> rather than guessing silently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-choose-the-definite-article\">A. Choose the definite article</h2>\n<p>Fill in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>___ restaurant</li>\n<li>___ gare</li>\n<li>___ école</li>\n<li>___ hôtels</li>\n<li>___ problème</li>\n<li>___ solution</li>\n<li>___ arbres</li>\n<li>___ histoire</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-choose-the-indefinite-article\">B. Choose the indefinite article</h2>\n<p>Fill in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>___ exercice</li>\n<li>___ question</li>\n<li>___ amis</li>\n<li>___ téléphone</li>\n<li>___ adresse</li>\n<li>___ voitures</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-make-the-phrase-plural\">C. Make the phrase plural</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite fenêtre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un tableau moderne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouvel appartement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voix française</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal heureux</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-make-the-adjective-agree\">D. Make the adjective agree</h2>\n<p>Use the adjective in parentheses.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>une fille ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code>)</li>\n<li>des maisons ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanc</code>)</li>\n<li>un homme ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code>)</li>\n<li>des étudiantes ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code>)</li>\n<li>une rue ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>)</li>\n<li>des garçons ___ (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code>)</li>\n<li>une ___ voisine (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>)</li>\n<li>un ___ hôtel (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code>)</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-put-the-words-in-a-natural-order\">E. Put the words in a natural order</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouge / une / voiture</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement / un / beau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficiles / des / exercices</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ville / grande / une</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">françaises / étudiantes / des</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"f-correct-the-mistake\">F. Correct the mistake</h2>\n<p>Each phrase contains one error.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le maison</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une garçon</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voiture</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une sac noire</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un belle jardin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chambre blancs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouveau ami</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"g-translate-into-french\">G. Translate into French</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>a small house</li>\n<li>the blue books</li>\n<li>a French student (female)</li>\n<li>some old neighborhoods</li>\n<li>the new hotel</li>\n<li>some happy Canadian women</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"h-guided-writing\">H. Guided writing</h2>\n<p>Write a four-sentence description of an apartment. Include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at least four nouns with articles;</li>\n<li>one singular and one plural noun;</li>\n<li>two colors;</li>\n<li>one adjective before a noun;</li>\n<li>two adjectives after nouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"i-mixed-mastery-check\">I. Mixed mastery check</h2>\n<p>Choose the correct option.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ université</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le / la / l'</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une femme ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien / canadienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ___ maisons</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grand / grande / grandes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ___ immeuble</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau / nouvel / nouvelle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noir / noire / noirs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ animaux</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un / une / des</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une leçon ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile / difficiles</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Anne sont ___</code> — <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse / heureux / heureuses</code></li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 1 exercises do these without notes. mark uncertain answers with `?` rather than guessing silently. ## a. choose the definite article fill in `le`, `la`, `l'`, or `les`. 1. ___ restaurant 2. ___ gare 3. ___ école 4. ___ hôtels 5. ___ problème 6. ___ solution 7. ___ arbres 8. ___ histoire ## b. choose the indefinite article fill in `un`, `une`, or `des`. 1. ___ exercice 2. ___ question 3. ___ amis 4. ___ téléphone 5. ___ adresse 6. ___ voitures ## c. make the phrase plural 1. `une petite fenêtre` 2. `un tableau moderne` 3. `le nouvel appartement` 4. `la voix française` 5. `un animal heureux` ## d. make the adjective agree use the adjective in parentheses. 1. une fille ___ (`sportif`) 2. des maisons ___ (`blanc`) 3. un homme ___ (`heureux`) 4. des étudiantes ___ (`canadien`) 5. une rue ___ (`calme`) 6. des garçons ___ (`actif`) 7. une ___ voisine (`nouveau`) 8. un ___ hôtel (`vieux`) ## e. put the words in a natural order 1. `rouge / une / voiture` 2. `appartement / un / beau` 3. `difficiles / des / exercices` 4. `ville / grande / une` 5. `françaises / étudiantes / des` ## f. correct the mistake each phrase contains one error. 1. `le maison` 2. `une garçon` 3. `des voiture` 4. `une sac noire` 5. `un belle jardin` 6. `des étudiantes canadien` 7. `une chambre blancs` 8. `un nouveau ami` ## g. translate into french 1. a small house 2. the blue books 3. a french student (female) 4. some old neighborhoods 5. the new hotel 6. some happy canadian women ## h. guided writing write a four-sentence description of an apartment. include: - at least four nouns with articles; - one singular and one plural noun; - two colors; - one adjective before a noun; - two adjectives after nouns. ## i. mixed mastery check choose the correct option. 1. `___ université` — `le / la / l'` 2. `une femme ___` — `canadien / canadienne` 3. `des ___ maisons` — `grand / grande / grandes` 4. `un ___ immeuble` — `nouveau / nouvel / nouvelle` 5. `des sacs ___` — `noir / noire / noirs` 6. `___ animaux` — `un / une / des` 7. `une leçon ___` — `difficile / difficiles` 8. `paul et anne sont ___` — `heureuse / heureux / heureuses` "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/practice/answer-key.md","title":"Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"answer-key\">Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-retrieval-checks\">Lesson retrieval checks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-1\">Lesson 1</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le musée</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la question</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'hôtel</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m.</code>)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les étudiants</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-2\">Lesson 2</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des photos</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des bureaux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les journaux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voix</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-3\">Lesson 3</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Masculine</th>\n<th>Feminine</th>\n<th>Feminine plural</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportives</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuses</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italienne</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italiennes</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calmes</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"lesson-4\">Lesson 4</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noire</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante canadienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des maisons blanches</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-a\">Exercise A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-b\">Exercise B</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-c\">Exercise C</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites fenêtres</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tableaux modernes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les nouveaux appartements</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voix françaises</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux heureux</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-d\">Exercise D</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanches</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadiennes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actifs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-e\">Exercise E</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture rouge</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des exercices difficiles</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes françaises</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-f\">Exercise F</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un garçon</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voitures</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac noir</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau jardin</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chambre blanche</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel ami</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-g\">Exercise G</h2>\n<p>Reasonable answers:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres bleus</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante française</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de vieux quartiers</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouvel hôtel</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des Canadiennes heureuses</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>In item 4, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> commonly becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code> before a plural adjective placed before the noun.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exercise-i\">Exercise I</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">grandes</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noirs</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"scoring\">Scoring</h2>\n<p>For Sections A-G and I, award one point per item: <strong>46 points total</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>42-46: ready for the writing task and delayed review</li>\n<li>36-41: review only the missed rule, then retry missed items</li>\n<li>28-35: repeat the relevant lessons and handwriting drill</li>\n<li>Below 28: divide the chapter into smaller two-day blocks</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not count a corrected answer as mastered until you can answer it correctly the following day.</p>","search":"# answer key ## lesson retrieval checks ### lesson 1 1. `le musée` 2. `la question` 3. `l'hôtel` (`m.`) 4. `les étudiants` ### lesson 2 1. `des photos` 2. `des bureaux` 3. `les journaux` 4. `les voix` ### lesson 3 | masculine | feminine | feminine plural | |---|---|---| | `sportif` | `sportive` | `sportives` | | `heureux` | `heureuse` | `heureuses` | | `italien` | `italienne` | `italiennes` | | `calme` | `calme` | `calmes` | ### lesson 4 1. `une voiture noire` 2. `un bel appartement` 3. `une étudiante canadienne` 4. `des maisons blanches` ## exercise a 1. `le` 2. `la` 3. `l'` 4. `les` 5. `le` 6. `la` 7. `les` 8. `l'` ## exercise b 1. `un` 2. `une` 3. `des` 4. `un` 5. `une` 6. `des` ## exercise c 1. `des petites fenêtres` 2. `des tableaux modernes` 3. `les nouveaux appartements` 4. `les voix françaises` 5. `des animaux heureux` ## exercise d 1. `sportive` 2. `blanches` 3. `heureux` 4. `canadiennes` 5. `calme` 6. `actifs` 7. `nouvelle` 8. `vieil` ## exercise e 1. `une voiture rouge` 2. `un bel appartement` 3. `des exercices difficiles` 4. `une grande ville` 5. `des étudiantes françaises` ## exercise f 1. `la maison` 2. `un garçon` 3. `des voitures` 4. `un sac noir` 5. `un beau jardin` 6. `des étudiantes canadiennes` 7. `une chambre blanche` 8. `un nouvel ami` ## exercise g reasonable answers: 1. `une petite maison` 2. `les livres bleus` 3. `une étudiante française` 4. `de vieux quartiers` 5. `le nouvel hôtel` 6. `des canadiennes heureuses` in item 4, `des` commonly becomes `de` before a plural adjective placed before the noun. ## exercise i 1. `l'` 2. `canadienne` 3. `grandes` 4. `nouvel` 5. `noirs` 6. `des` 7. `difficile` 8. `heureux` ## scoring for sections a-g and i, award one point per item: **46 points total**. - 42-46: ready for the writing task and delayed review - 36-41: review only the missed rule, then retry missed items - 28-35: repeat the relevant lessons and handwriting drill - below 28: divide the chapter into smaller two-day blocks do not count a corrected answer as mastered until you can answer it correctly the following day. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/review/7-day-plan.md","title":"Seven-Day Study Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"seven-day-study-plan\">Seven-Day Study Plan</h1>\n<p>Aim for 30-40 focused minutes per day. Stop before concentration drops.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-1-gender-and-definite-articles\">Day 1: Gender and definite articles</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read Lesson 1.</li>\n<li>Say every example twice.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drill 1.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercise A.</li>\n<li>Record errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-2-indefinite-articles-and-plurals\">Day 2: Indefinite articles and plurals</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Recall yesterday's four definite articles before opening notes.</li>\n<li>Read Lesson 2.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drill 3.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercises B and C.</li>\n<li>Review only yesterday's errors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-3-adjective-agreement\">Day 3: Adjective agreement</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the four forms of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> from memory.</li>\n<li>Read Lesson 3.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drill 2.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercise D.</li>\n<li>Read answers aloud after correction.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-4-position-colors-and-nationalities\">Day 4: Position, colors, and nationalities</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Recall three adjectives that usually precede nouns.</li>\n<li>Read Lesson 4.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercises E and F.</li>\n<li>Do Writing Drill 4 as dictation.</li>\n<li>Add errors to the log.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-5-production-day\">Day 5: Production day</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete Exercise G.</li>\n<li>Complete Writing Drills 5 and 6.</li>\n<li>Complete Exercise H.</li>\n<li>Read your paragraph aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Correct only after finishing the full paragraph.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-6-listening-and-pronunciation\">Day 6: Listening and pronunciation</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">audio-script.md</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tts-narration.txt</code>.</li>\n<li>Shadow ten French phrases: listen, pause, and imitate rhythm.</li>\n<li>Write five heard phrases as dictation.</li>\n<li>Compare your spelling and agreement.</li>\n<li>Repeat difficult phrases slowly, then at natural speed.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"day-7-closed-book-mastery-check\">Day 7: Closed-book mastery check</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Complete Exercise I.</li>\n<li>Redo every item previously entered in the error log.</li>\n<li>Write five original sentences.</li>\n<li>Explain two corrections aloud in English.</li>\n<li>Schedule reviews for Day 10, Day 14, and Day 30.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"delayed-reviews\">Delayed reviews</h2>\n<p>At each delayed review:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write the article table from memory.</li>\n<li>Transform two singular phrases into plural.</li>\n<li>Produce all four forms of one adjective.</li>\n<li>Write two original descriptive sentences.</li>\n<li>Redo only errors and uncertain items.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# seven-day study plan aim for 30-40 focused minutes per day. stop before concentration drops. ## day 1: gender and definite articles 1. read lesson 1. 2. say every example twice. 3. complete writing drill 1. 4. complete exercise a. 5. record errors. ## day 2: indefinite articles and plurals 1. recall yesterday's four definite articles before opening notes. 2. read lesson 2. 3. complete writing drill 3. 4. complete exercises b and c. 5. review only yesterday's errors. ## day 3: adjective agreement 1. write the four forms of `petit` from memory. 2. read lesson 3. 3. complete writing drill 2. 4. complete exercise d. 5. read answers aloud after correction. ## day 4: position, colors, and nationalities 1. recall three adjectives that usually precede nouns. 2. read lesson 4. 3. complete exercises e and f. 4. do writing drill 4 as dictation. 5. add errors to the log. ## day 5: production day 1. complete exercise g. 2. complete writing drills 5 and 6. 3. complete exercise h. 4. read your paragraph aloud twice. 5. correct only after finishing the full paragraph. ## day 6: listening and pronunciation 1. use `audio-script.md` or `tts-narration.txt`. 2. shadow ten french phrases: listen, pause, and imitate rhythm. 3. write five heard phrases as dictation. 4. compare your spelling and agreement. 5. repeat difficult phrases slowly, then at natural speed. ## day 7: closed-book mastery check 1. complete exercise i. 2. redo every item previously entered in the error log. 3. write five original sentences. 4. explain two corrections aloud in english. 5. schedule reviews for day 10, day 14, and day 30. ## delayed reviews at each delayed review: 1. write the article table from memory. 2. transform two singular phrases into plural. 3. produce all four forms of one adjective. 4. write two original descriptive sentences. 5. redo only errors and uncertain items. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/audio-script.md","title":"Chapter 1 Audio Lesson: French Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives","type":"Audio","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-1-audio-lesson-french-nouns-articles-and-adjectives\">Chapter 1 Audio Lesson: French Nouns, Articles, and Adjectives</h1>\n<p><strong>Format:</strong> Text-to-speech teaching script <strong>Audience:</strong> Complete beginners <strong>Suggested duration:</strong> 25–35 minutes <strong>Use:</strong> Companion lesson for <em>Easy French Step-by-Step</em>, Chapter 1</p>\n<h2 id=\"production-notes\">Production notes</h2>\n<p>This is an original companion lesson. It teaches the same foundational topics as Chapter 1 but does not read or reproduce the textbook. The narration should be calm and deliberate, with a short pause after every French phrase and a longer pause wherever <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">[PAUSE]</code> appears.</p>\n<p>Recommended voice settings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Native or highly fluent French pronunciation for French examples</li>\n<li>Slightly slower-than-normal pace for new vocabulary</li>\n<li>English explanation followed by French example</li>\n<li>Repeat every French phrase twice</li>\n<li>Leave 3–5 seconds after prompts so the learner can answer</li>\n<li>Use a different voice or tone for “Your turn”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whenever a French example appears by itself, the English meaning is now written immediately after an em dash. Practice prompts intentionally remain untranslated until after the pause so the learner can test comprehension.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"opening\">Opening</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Welcome to your first French grammar and listening lesson.</p>\n<p>Today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. We will learn six connected ideas:</p>\n<p>First, French nouns have grammatical gender.</p>\n<p>Second, nouns can be singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Third, French uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.”</p>\n<p>Fourth, articles must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>Fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>And sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning.</p>\n<p>Do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. Your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns.</p>\n<p>When you hear “Your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Let’s begin.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-1-what-is-a-noun\">Part 1: What is a noun?</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>A noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea.</p>\n<p>In English, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. In French, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>This grammatical gender is a property of the French word. It does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le téléphone</code> — the telephone.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code> — the car.</p>\n<p>The book is masculine in French. The table is feminine. The telephone is masculine. The car is feminine.</p>\n<p>These labels are part of French vocabulary. Learn the noun together with its article whenever possible.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livre</code>. Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voiture</code>. Learn <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code>.</p>\n<p>Say them with me.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code> — the car.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voiture</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le téléphone</code> — the telephone.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le téléphone</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Again: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code>.</p>\n<p>This habit will help you remember gender naturally.</p>\n<p>Think of the article as part of the noun's identity, not as a separate decoration. In English, you can often learn “table” by itself. In French, the useful unit is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code>. When you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number.</p>\n<p>This also makes listening easier. Even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> gives you an immediate grammatical clue. The more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-2-the-definite-article-the\">Part 2: The definite article: “the”</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>The definite article is the French equivalent of “the.”</p>\n<p>For a singular masculine noun, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p>For a singular feminine noun, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p>For a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> — the books.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les tables</code> — the tables.</p>\n<p>There is also a shorter form, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code>, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.”</p>\n<p>This shortening is called elision. French avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. Instead of saying <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la amie</code>, French removes the final vowel of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> and writes and pronounces <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’amie</code>.</p>\n<p>The important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun's gender. You still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’ami</code> — the friend, masculine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’amie</code> — the friend, feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’hôtel</code> — the hotel.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’idée</code> — the idea.</p>\n<p>Notice that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. You must memorize the gender separately.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’ami</code> is masculine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’amie</code> is feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’hôtel</code> is masculine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’idée</code> is feminine.</p>\n<p>Say the four forms.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la table</code> — the table.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> — the books.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’idée</code> — the idea.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<h3 id=\"quick-recognition-practice\">Quick recognition practice</h3>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>I will say a noun phrase. Identify the article and the number.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la maison</code> — the house.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is feminine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les maisons</code> — the houses.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is plural. The noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’université</code> — the university.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is singular. The noun is feminine, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> hides the gender in pronunciation.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le projet</code> — the project.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>Notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun's gender. Do not guess gender from the English translation. French gender belongs to the French word.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-3-how-to-learn-noun-gender\">Part 3: How to learn noun gender</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>The safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article.</p>\n<p>There are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-tion</code> are feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la décision</code> — the decision.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la conversation</code> — the conversation.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code> are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le document</code> — the document.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le changement</code> — the change.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-isme</code> are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le réalisme</code> — realism.</p>\n<p>However, French has exceptions. Patterns are clues, not guarantees.</p>\n<p>The noun <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’eau</code>, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect.</p>\n<p>When a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un étudiant</code> — a male student or a student described with the masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante</code> — a female student.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ami</code> — a male friend.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une amie</code> — a female friend.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. The article tells you the gender:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le journaliste</code> — the male journalist, or the masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la journaliste</code> — the female journalist.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le professeur</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la professeure</code> are both used in modern French, depending on region and style.</p>\n<p>The key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun.</p>\n<p>When you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. A card that says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livre = book</code> trains incomplete knowledge. A better card says <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre = the book</code>, and ideally includes a short sentence such as <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre est intéressant</code> — The book is interesting.</p>\n<p>Gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. When a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. Accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-4-the-indefinite-article-a-an-and-some\">Part 4: The indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some”</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>French has three common indefinite articles.</p>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> with a singular masculine noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un livre</code> — a book.</p>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> with a singular feminine noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une table</code> — a table.</p>\n<p>Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> with plural nouns of either gender.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des livres</code> — some books, or books.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tables</code> — some tables, or tables.</p>\n<p>In natural English, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> is often not translated as “some.” The meaning depends on context.</p>\n<p>Compare the three ideas:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ordinateur</code> introduces one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’ordinateur</code> refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ordinateurs</code> refers to more than one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p>English often leaves the article out when translating plural French phrases. That does not mean the French article is optional. In French, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase.</p>\n<p>Listen:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’ai un ordinateur.</code> — I have a computer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’ai une question.</code> — I have a question.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’ai des idées.</code> — I have ideas.</p>\n<p>The article must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. Before speaking, ask yourself two questions: Is the noun masculine or feminine? Is it singular or plural? The answer to those two questions determines the article.</p>\n<p>Your turn. Say the correct article.</p>\n<p>A masculine project: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ projet</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet</code>.</p>\n<p>A feminine question: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ question</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une question</code>.</p>\n<p>Plural ideas: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ idées</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des idées</code>.</p>\n<p>Plural computers: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">___ ordinateurs</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The answer is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ordinateurs</code>.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-5-singular-and-plural-nouns\">Part 5: Singular and plural nouns</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Most French nouns form the plural by adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> in writing.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des projets</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une idée</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des idées</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le client</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les clients</code>.</p>\n<p>Usually, the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is not pronounced.</p>\n<p>This creates an important difference between written and spoken French. On the page, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">client</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">clients</code> look different. In speech, the noun may sound almost identical. The listener often relies on the article—<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> versus <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>—to recognize the number.</p>\n<p>The difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un client</code> — a client.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des clients</code> — clients or some clients.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le client</code> — the client.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les clients</code> — the clients.</p>\n<p>Some nouns already end in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-z</code> in the singular. Their spelling stays the same in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un choix</code> — a choice.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des choix</code> — choices.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un prix</code> — a price.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des prix</code> — prices.</p>\n<p>The article and context show the number.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have special plural spellings:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bureau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des bureaux</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un travail</code> can become <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des travaux</code> when referring to tasks or works.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux</code>.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. Learn each new plural with its vocabulary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"liaison-with-plural-articles\">Liaison with plural articles</h3>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>When <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word.</p>\n<p>The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is pronounced like a <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code>.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> sounds like “lay zah-mee.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des enfants</code> sounds like “day zahn-fahn.”</p>\n<p>This linking is called liaison.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> — the friends.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des enfants</code> — children or some children.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les étudiants</code> — the students.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> of the noun itself is generally silent. The linking sound comes from the article.</p>\n<p>Listen for the boundary between the words. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code>, do not pronounce the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> of <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">amis</code> as a separate sound. The <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">z</code> sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. This is one reason French can sound as though words run together.</p>\n<p>For now, prioritize recognition over production. First learn to hear that <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les amis</code> is plural. Later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech.</p>\n<p>Do not force liaison everywhere. First become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-6-adjectives-describe-nouns\">Part 6: Adjectives describe nouns</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>An adjective gives information about a noun.</p>\n<p>It can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics.</p>\n<p>In French, the adjective usually comes after the noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet intéressant</code> — an interesting project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une idée originale</code> — an original idea.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un problème difficile</code> — a difficult problem.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une solution simple</code> — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>The adjective can also follow the verb <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">être</code>, meaning “to be.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le projet est intéressant.</code> — The project is interesting.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La solution est simple.</code> — The solution is simple.</p>\n<p>The most important rule is agreement.</p>\n<p>The adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>In English, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” French makes the relationship visible in writing. The ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>When you build a French noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. The adjective is not agreeing with the English translation; it is agreeing with the French noun.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-7-masculine-and-feminine-adjective-forms\">Part 7: Masculine and feminine adjective forms</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>For many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> to the masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un client intelligent</code> — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une cliente intelligente</code> — an intelligent female client.</p>\n<p>The adjective changes from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intelligent</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intelligente</code>.</p>\n<p>Another example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un employé fatigué</code> — a tired employee, masculine form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une employée fatiguée</code> — a tired employee, feminine form.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intelligent, intelligente</code> — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué, fatiguée</code> — tired, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Some adjectives already end in a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> in the masculine form. They do not change spelling in the feminine.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue calme</code> — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une collègue calme</code> — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un enfant drôle</code> — a funny child.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une enfant drôle</code> — a funny child.</p>\n<p>The adjective is the same in writing. The noun or article gives more information.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that a word ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> is automatically feminine. The ending can be identical in both genders. For example, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code> stays <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code>, but the complete phrases <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue calme</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une collègue calme</code> still show different gender through the article and noun.</p>\n<p>This is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. You are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives have special feminine forms:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">active</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieuse</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportive</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">belle</code>.</p>\n<p>Do not try to derive every form from English. Learn the pair together.</p>\n<p>Some changes are predictable, such as adding <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. Treat irregular forms as families: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif, active</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux, heureuse</code>; <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau, belle</code>. Say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-8-adjective-plurals\">Part 8: Adjective plurals</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>The regular plural of an adjective usually adds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> in writing.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un projet intéressant</code> — an interesting project.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des projets intéressants</code> — interesting projects.</p>\n<p>For feminine plural:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une solution simple</code> — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des solutions simples</code> — simple solutions.</p>\n<p>Notice that an adjective ending in a silent <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">e</code> may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code>.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code> do not change their spelling in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un homme sérieux</code> — a serious man.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des hommes sérieux</code> — serious men.</p>\n<p>The written adjective remains <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code>.</p>\n<p>A useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. In <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des hommes sérieux</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> signals plural, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">hommes</code> is the plural noun, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sérieux</code> is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. Even when the adjective's spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active.</p>\n<p>If a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written French normally uses the masculine plural form.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un frère et une sœur intelligents</code> — an intelligent brother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun.</p>\n<p>For an entirely feminine group:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une mère et une sœur intelligentes</code> — an intelligent mother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is feminine plural.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-9-colors\">Part 9: Colors</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un écran noir</code> — a black screen.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noire</code> — a black car.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des écrans noirs</code> — black screens.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des voitures noires</code> — black cars.</p>\n<p>Many colors follow the regular pattern:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleu, bleue</code> — blue.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vert, verte</code> — green.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">gris, grise</code> — gray.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">blanc, blanche</code> — white.</p>\n<p>Some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">orange</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">marron</code> are commonly invariable.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac orange</code> — an orange bag.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chemise orange</code> — an orange shirt.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs orange</code> — orange bags.</p>\n<p>When a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J’aime le bleu.</code> — I like blue.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le rouge est vif.</code> — Red is vivid.</p>\n<p>For now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns.</p>\n<p>Color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. Look around you and describe objects you can see. Start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-10-adjectives-before-the-noun\">Part 10: Adjectives before the noun</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Examples include words related to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>size;</li>\n<li>age;</li>\n<li>beauty;</li>\n<li>goodness;</li>\n<li>quantity or order;</li>\n<li>and certain familiar descriptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Listen to these phrases:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code> — a large city.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit problème</code> — a small problem.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bon résultat</code> — a good result.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une jeune équipe</code> — a young team.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux bâtiment</code> — an old building.</p>\n<p>The adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it.</p>\n<p>Adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. Whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. Compare <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit projet</code> and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite tâche</code>: the adjective moves with the noun's gender.</p>\n<p>At the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. Do not try to memorize a long abstract list. Repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit projet</code> — a small project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite tâche</code> — a small task.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de petits projets</code> — some small projects.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de petites tâches</code> — some small tasks.</p>\n<p>Three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “beautiful” can be:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code> before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">belle</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau quartier</code> — a beautiful neighborhood.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code> — a beautiful apartment.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une belle maison</code> — a beautiful house.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “new” can be:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelle</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouveau projet</code> — a new project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel emploi</code> — a new job.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une nouvelle idée</code> — a new idea.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “old” can be:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code> before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieille</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieux livre</code> — an old book.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieil ordinateur</code> — an old computer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une vieille voiture</code> — an old car.</p>\n<p>Practice the pattern:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau, bel, belle</code> — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle</code> — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux, vieil, vieille</code> — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<h3 id=\"meaning-can-change-with-position\">Meaning can change with position</h3>\n<p>Sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. French speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. When you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase.</p>\n<p>For example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters.</p>\n<p>Similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. Just notice that adjective position can matter.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-11-nationalities-and-languages\">Part 11: Nationalities and languages</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns.</p>\n<p>As adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ingénieur canadien</code> — a Canadian male engineer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une ingénieure canadienne</code> — a Canadian female engineer.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ingénieurs canadiens</code> — Canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des ingénieures canadiennes</code> — Canadian female engineers.</p>\n<p>Nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in French:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un Canadien</code> — a Canadian man.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une Canadienne</code> — a Canadian woman.</p>\n<p>But nationality adjectives are not capitalized:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue canadien</code> — a Canadian colleague.</p>\n<p>Language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le français</code> — French, the language.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’anglais</code> — English, the language.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’espagnol</code> — Spanish, the language.</p>\n<p>Say these phrases:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> — I speak French.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle apprend l’anglais.</code> — She is learning English.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions l’espagnol.</code> — We study Spanish.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-12-guided-review\">Part 12: Guided review</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Let’s review the complete system.</p>\n<p>Step one: identify the noun.</p>\n<p>Step two: identify its gender.</p>\n<p>Step three: identify whether it is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Step four: choose the article.</p>\n<p>Step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree.</p>\n<p>This five-step routine is the main skill you are building. Do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. Work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending.</p>\n<p>With practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. At the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them.</p>\n<p>Let’s do examples together.</p>\n<p>Example one:</p>\n<p>“A small house.”</p>\n<p>House is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code>, a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Small is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>, which becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Because the noun is singular and indefinite, use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code> — a small house.</p>\n<p>Notice the reasoning: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">maison</code> is feminine, so we need <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code>. The adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petite</code> to agree with the feminine noun. Because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example two:</p>\n<p>“The new project.”</p>\n<p>Project is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>New is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le nouveau projet</code> — the new project.</p>\n<p>Here, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">projet</code> is masculine singular, so we use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>. The noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>, not <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>. The adjective comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example three:</p>\n<p>“A new apartment.”</p>\n<p>Apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound.</p>\n<p>The masculine form of “new” becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel appartement</code> — a new apartment.</p>\n<p>The noun is still masculine singular, so the article is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>. However, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">appartement</code> begins with a vowel sound. French uses <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code> before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example four:</p>\n<p>“Some interesting books.”</p>\n<p>Books are masculine plural.</p>\n<p>Interesting becomes plural masculine.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des livres intéressants</code> — interesting books.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">livres</code> is masculine plural, so the article is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code>. The adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">intéressants</code>.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Example five:</p>\n<p>“The green cars.”</p>\n<p>Cars are feminine plural.</p>\n<p>Green becomes feminine plural.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voitures vertes</code> — the green cars.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">voitures</code> is feminine plural, so we use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code>. The color follows the noun and becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vertes</code>, with both the feminine and plural endings.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-13-your-turn\">Part 13: Your turn</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Pause after each prompt and produce the French phrase.</p>\n<p>One: “a difficult problem.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un problème difficile</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine singular, so use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code>. The adjective <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code> follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular.</p>\n<p>Two: “a beautiful city.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une belle ville</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine singular, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> changes to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">belle</code>. This common adjective normally comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Three: “some new ideas.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de nouvelles idées</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine plural, so <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> becomes <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvelles</code>. Because this adjective comes before a plural noun, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> commonly shortens to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de</code>.</p>\n<p>Four: “the old hotels.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les vieux hôtels</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code>. The special singular form <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code> is not used here because the phrase is plural.</p>\n<p>Five: “a black computer.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un ordinateur noir</code>.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ordinateur</code> is masculine singular. The color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noir</code>.</p>\n<p>Six: “some orange bags.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs orange</code>.</p>\n<p>The noun is plural, but <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">orange</code> is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending.</p>\n<p>Seven: “the serious engineers.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les ingénieurs sérieux</code>.</p>\n<p>Eight: “a Canadian woman.”</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>Suggested answer: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une Canadienne canadienne</code> is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. A more natural phrase is <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une ingénieure canadienne</code>, meaning “a Canadian female engineer.”</p>\n<p>This example shows why vocabulary and context matter. Do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-14-listening-discrimination\">Part 14: Listening discrimination</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Listen for the article. I will say two phrases. Decide whether the noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le projet</code> — the project.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les projets</code> — the projects.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une idée</code> — an idea.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des idées</code> — ideas.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p>Now listen for adjective agreement.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un client satisfait</code> — a satisfied client.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une cliente satisfaite</code> — a satisfied female client.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un collègue sérieux</code> — a serious colleague.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des collègues sérieux</code> — serious colleagues.</p>\n<p>[PAUSE]</p>\n<p>The adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural.</p>\n<p>---</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-15-end-of-lesson-summary\">Part 15: End-of-lesson summary</h2>\n<p><strong>NARRATOR:</strong></p>\n<p>Here are the essential rules from today.</p>\n<p>Every French noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>Learn nouns together with their articles.</p>\n<p>The definite articles are:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code> for masculine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code> for feminine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code> before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code> for all plural nouns.</p>\n<p>The indefinite articles are:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code> for masculine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code> for feminine singular.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code> for plural nouns.</p>\n<p>Most plural nouns add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> in writing, but the final <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">s</code> is usually silent.</p>\n<p>Adjectives usually follow the noun.</p>\n<p>Adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>Many feminine adjectives add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code>.</p>\n<p>Many plural adjectives add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>.</p>\n<p>Some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order.</p>\n<p>The forms <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> change before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>Finally, practice aloud. French grammar is not only a written system. You need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound.</p>\n<p>For your next review, do three things:</p>\n<p>First, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l’</code>.</p>\n<p>Second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases.</p>\n<p>Third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un écran noir</code> — a black screen.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une tasse blanche</code> — a white cup.</p>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit bureau</code> — a small office or desk.</p>\n<p>Speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often.</p>\n<p>That is the end of this lesson. À bientôt!</p>","search":"# chapter 1 audio lesson: french nouns, articles, and adjectives **format:** text-to-speech teaching script **audience:** complete beginners **suggested duration:** 25–35 minutes **use:** companion lesson for *easy french step-by-step*, chapter 1 ## production notes this is an original companion lesson. it teaches the same foundational topics as chapter 1 but does not read or reproduce the textbook. the narration should be calm and deliberate, with a short pause after every french phrase and a longer pause wherever `[pause]` appears. recommended voice settings: - native or highly fluent french pronunciation for french examples - slightly slower-than-normal pace for new vocabulary - english explanation followed by french example - repeat every french phrase twice - leave 3–5 seconds after prompts so the learner can answer - use a different voice or tone for “your turn” whenever a french example appears by itself, the english meaning is now written immediately after an em dash. practice prompts intentionally remain untranslated until after the pause so the learner can test comprehension. --- ## opening **narrator:** welcome to your first french grammar and listening lesson. today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. we will learn six connected ideas: first, french nouns have grammatical gender. second, nouns can be singular or plural. third, french uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.” fourth, articles must agree with the noun. fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number. and sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning. do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns. when you hear “your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud. [pause] let’s begin. --- ## part 1: what is a noun? **narrator:** a noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea. in english, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. in french, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine. this grammatical gender is a property of the french word. it does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing. for example: `le livre` — the book. `la table` — the table. `le téléphone` — the telephone. `la voiture` — the car. the book is masculine in french. the table is feminine. the telephone is masculine. the car is feminine. these labels are part of french vocabulary. learn the noun together with its article whenever possible. do not learn only `livre`. learn `le livre`. do not learn only `voiture`. learn `la voiture`. say them with me. `le livre` — the book. [pause] again: `le livre`. `la voiture` — the car. [pause] again: `la voiture`. `le téléphone` — the telephone. [pause] again: `le téléphone`. `la table` — the table. [pause] again: `la table`. this habit will help you remember gender naturally. think of the article as part of the noun's identity, not as a separate decoration. in english, you can often learn “table” by itself. in french, the useful unit is `la table`. when you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number. this also makes listening easier. even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of `le`, `la`, or `l’` gives you an immediate grammatical clue. the more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes. --- ## part 2: the definite article: “the” **narrator:** the definite article is the french equivalent of “the.” for a singular masculine noun, use `le`. `le livre` — the book. for a singular feminine noun, use `la`. `la table` — the table. for a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use `les`. `les livres` — the books. `les tables` — the tables. there is also a shorter form, `l’`, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.” this shortening is called elision. french avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. instead of saying `la amie`, french removes the final vowel of `la` and writes and pronounces `l’amie`. the important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun's gender. you still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine. for example: `l’ami` — the friend, masculine. `l’amie` — the friend, feminine. `l’hôtel` — the hotel. `l’idée` — the idea. notice that `l’` does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. you must memorize the gender separately. `l’ami` is masculine. `l’amie` is feminine. `l’hôtel` is masculine. `l’idée` is feminine. say the four forms. `le livre` — the book. [pause] `la table` — the table. [pause] `les livres` — the books. [pause] `l’idée` — the idea. [pause] ### quick recognition practice **narrator:** i will say a noun phrase. identify the article and the number. `la maison` — the house. [pause] the answer is feminine singular. `les maisons` — the houses. [pause] the answer is plural. the noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine. `l’université` — the university. [pause] the answer is singular. the noun is feminine, but `l’` hides the gender in pronunciation. `le projet` — the project. [pause] the answer is masculine singular. notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun's gender. do not guess gender from the english translation. french gender belongs to the french word. --- ## part 3: how to learn noun gender **narrator:** the safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article. there are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules. many nouns ending in `-tion` are feminine. for example: `la décision` — the decision. `la conversation` — the conversation. many nouns ending in `-ment` are masculine. for example: `le document` — the document. `le changement` — the change. many nouns ending in `-isme` are masculine. for example: `le réalisme` — realism. however, french has exceptions. patterns are clues, not guarantees. the noun `l’eau`, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect. when a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms: `un étudiant` — a male student or a student described with the masculine form. `une étudiante` — a female student. `un ami` — a male friend. `une amie` — a female friend. some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. the article tells you the gender: `le journaliste` — the male journalist, or the masculine form. `la journaliste` — the female journalist. `le professeur` and `la professeure` are both used in modern french, depending on region and style. the key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun. when you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. a card that says `livre = book` trains incomplete knowledge. a better card says `le livre = the book`, and ideally includes a short sentence such as `le livre est intéressant` — the book is interesting. gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. when a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun. --- ## part 4: the indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some” **narrator:** french has three common indefinite articles. use `un` with a singular masculine noun. `un livre` — a book. use `une` with a singular feminine noun. `une table` — a table. use `des` with plural nouns of either gender. `des livres` — some books, or books. `des tables` — some tables, or tables. in natural english, `des` is often not translated as “some.” the meaning depends on context. compare the three ideas: `un ordinateur` introduces one non-specific computer. `l’ordinateur` refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context. `des ordinateurs` refers to more than one non-specific computer. english often leaves the article out when translating plural french phrases. that does not mean the french article is optional. in french, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase. listen: `j’ai un ordinateur.` — i have a computer. `j’ai une question.` — i have a question. `j’ai des idées.` — i have ideas. the article must agree with the noun. this is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. before speaking, ask yourself two questions: is the noun masculine or feminine? is it singular or plural? the answer to those two questions determines the article. your turn. say the correct article. a masculine project: `___ projet`. [pause] the answer is `un projet`. a feminine question: `___ question`. [pause] the answer is `une question`. plural ideas: `___ idées`. [pause] the answer is `des idées`. plural computers: `___ ordinateurs`. [pause] the answer is `des ordinateurs`. --- ## part 5: singular and plural nouns **narrator:** most french nouns form the plural by adding `-s` in writing. `un projet` becomes `des projets`. `une idée` becomes `des idées`. `le client` becomes `les clients`. usually, the final `s` is not pronounced. this creates an important difference between written and spoken french. on the page, `client` and `clients` look different. in speech, the noun may sound almost identical. the listener often relies on the article—`le` versus `les`, or `un` versus `des`—to recognize the number. the difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article: `un client` — a client. `des clients` — clients or some clients. `le client` — the client. `les clients` — the clients. some nouns already end in `-s`, `-x`, or `-z` in the singular. their spelling stays the same in the plural. for example: `un choix` — a choice. `des choix` — choices. `un prix` — a price. `des prix` — prices. the article and context show the number. some nouns have special plural spellings: `un bureau` becomes `des bureaux`. `un travail` can become `des travaux` when referring to tasks or works. `un animal` becomes `des animaux`. do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. learn each new plural with its vocabulary. ### liaison with plural articles **narrator:** when `les` or `des` comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word. the `s` is pronounced like a `z`. for example: `les amis` sounds like “lay zah-mee.” `des enfants` sounds like “day zahn-fahn.” this linking is called liaison. repeat: `les amis` — the friends. [pause] `des enfants` — children or some children. [pause] `les étudiants` — the students. [pause] the final `s` of the noun itself is generally silent. the linking sound comes from the article. listen for the boundary between the words. in `les amis`, do not pronounce the final `s` of `amis` as a separate sound. the `z` sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. this is one reason french can sound as though words run together. for now, prioritize recognition over production. first learn to hear that `les amis` is plural. later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech. do not force liaison everywhere. first become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases. --- ## part 6: adjectives describe nouns **narrator:** an adjective gives information about a noun. it can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics. in french, the adjective usually comes after the noun. `un projet intéressant` — an interesting project. `une idée originale` — an original idea. `un problème difficile` — a difficult problem. `une solution simple` — a simple solution. the adjective can also follow the verb `être`, meaning “to be.” `le projet est intéressant.` — the project is interesting. `la solution est simple.` — the solution is simple. the most important rule is agreement. the adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number. in english, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” french makes the relationship visible in writing. the ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural. when you build a french noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. the adjective is not agreeing with the english translation; it is agreeing with the french noun. --- ## part 7: masculine and feminine adjective forms **narrator:** for many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding `-e` to the masculine form. `un client intelligent` — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase. `une cliente intelligente` — an intelligent female client. the adjective changes from `intelligent` to `intelligente`. another example: `un employé fatigué` — a tired employee, masculine form. `une employée fatiguée` — a tired employee, feminine form. repeat: `intelligent, intelligente` — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms. [pause] `fatigué, fatiguée` — tired, masculine and feminine forms. [pause] some adjectives already end in a silent `e` in the masculine form. they do not change spelling in the feminine. `un collègue calme` — a calm colleague. `une collègue calme` — a calm colleague. `un enfant drôle` — a funny child. `une enfant drôle` — a funny child. the adjective is the same in writing. the noun or article gives more information. do not assume that a word ending in `-e` is automatically feminine. the ending can be identical in both genders. for example, `calme` stays `calme`, but the complete phrases `un collègue calme` and `une collègue calme` still show different gender through the article and noun. this is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. you are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern. some adjectives have special feminine forms: `actif` becomes `active`. `heureux` becomes `heureuse`. `sérieux` becomes `sérieuse`. `sportif` becomes `sportive`. `beau` becomes `belle`. do not try to derive every form from english. learn the pair together. some changes are predictable, such as adding `-e`, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. treat irregular forms as families: `actif, active`; `heureux, heureuse`; `beau, belle`. say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase. --- ## part 8: adjective plurals **narrator:** the regular plural of an adjective usually adds `-s` in writing. `un projet intéressant` — an interesting project. becomes: `des projets intéressants` — interesting projects. for feminine plural: `une solution simple` — a simple solution. becomes: `des solutions simples` — simple solutions. notice that an adjective ending in a silent `e` may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds `s`. some adjectives ending in `-s` or `-x` do not change their spelling in the plural. for example: `un homme sérieux` — a serious man. `des hommes sérieux` — serious men. the written adjective remains `sérieux`. a useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. in `des hommes sérieux`, `des` signals plural, `hommes` is the plural noun, and `sérieux` is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. even when the adjective's spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active. if a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written french normally uses the masculine plural form. for example: `un frère et une sœur intelligents` — an intelligent brother and sister. the adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun. for an entirely feminine group: `une mère et une sœur intelligentes` — an intelligent mother and sister. the adjective is feminine plural. --- ## part 9: colors **narrator:** color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun. `un écran noir` — a black screen. `une voiture noire` — a black car. `des écrans noirs` — black screens. `des voitures noires` — black cars. many colors follow the regular pattern: `bleu, bleue` — blue. `vert, verte` — green. `gris, grise` — gray. `blanc, blanche` — white. some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives. for example: `orange` and `marron` are commonly invariable. `un sac orange` — an orange bag. `une chemise orange` — an orange shirt. `des sacs orange` — orange bags. when a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine: `j’aime le bleu.` — i like blue. `le rouge est vif.` — red is vivid. for now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns. color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. look around you and describe objects you can see. start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending. --- ## part 10: adjectives before the noun **narrator:** most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun. examples include words related to: - size; - age; - beauty; - goodness; - quantity or order; - and certain familiar descriptions. listen to these phrases: `une grande ville` — a large city. `un petit problème` — a small problem. `un bon résultat` — a good result. `une jeune équipe` — a young team. `un vieux bâtiment` — an old building. the adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it. adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. compare `un petit projet` and `une petite tâche`: the adjective moves with the noun's gender. at the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. do not try to memorize a long abstract list. repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun. `un petit projet` — a small project. `une petite tâche` — a small task. `de petits projets` — some small projects. `de petites tâches` — some small tasks. three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.” the adjective meaning “beautiful” can be: `beau` before a consonant sound. `bel` before a vowel sound or silent “h.” `belle` with a feminine noun. examples: `un beau quartier` — a beautiful neighborhood. `un bel appartement` — a beautiful apartment. `une belle maison` — a beautiful house. the adjective meaning “new” can be: `nouveau` before a consonant sound. `nouvel` before a vowel sound or silent “h.” `nouvelle` with a feminine noun. examples: `un nouveau projet` — a new project. `un nouvel emploi` — a new job. `une nouvelle idée` — a new idea. the adjective meaning “old” can be: `vieux` before a consonant sound. `vieil` before a vowel sound or silent “h.” `vieille` with a feminine noun. examples: `un vieux livre` — an old book. `un vieil ordinateur` — an old computer. `une vieille voiture` — an old car. practice the pattern: `beau, bel, belle` — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. [pause] `nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle` — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. [pause] `vieux, vieil, vieille` — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. [pause] ### meaning can change with position sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun. this is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. french speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. when you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase. for example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters. similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy. do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. just notice that adjective position can matter. --- ## part 11: nationalities and languages **narrator:** nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns. as adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe: `un ingénieur canadien` — a canadian male engineer. `une ingénieure canadienne` — a canadian female engineer. `des ingénieurs canadiens` — canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers. `des ingénieures canadiennes` — canadian female engineers. nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in french: `un canadien` — a canadian man. `une canadienne` — a canadian woman. but nationality adjectives are not capitalized: `un collègue canadien` — a canadian colleague. language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized: `le français` — french, the language. `l’anglais` — english, the language. `l’espagnol` — spanish, the language. say these phrases: `je parle français.` — i speak french. `elle apprend l’anglais.` — she is learning english. `nous étudions l’espagnol.` — we study spanish. --- ## part 12: guided review **narrator:** let’s review the complete system. step one: identify the noun. step two: identify its gender. step three: identify whether it is singular or plural. step four: choose the article. step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree. this five-step routine is the main skill you are building. do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending. with practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. at the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them. let’s do examples together. example one: “a small house.” house is `maison`, a feminine noun. small is `petit`, which becomes `petite` with a feminine noun. because the noun is singular and indefinite, use `une`. the phrase is: `une petite maison` — a small house. notice the reasoning: `maison` is feminine, so we need `une`. the adjective `petit` becomes `petite` to agree with the feminine noun. because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is `une petite maison`. [pause] example two: “the new project.” project is masculine singular. new is `nouveau` before a consonant sound. the phrase is: `le nouveau projet` — the new project. here, `projet` is masculine singular, so we use `le`. the noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is `nouveau`, not `nouvel`. the adjective comes before the noun. [pause] example three: “a new apartment.” apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound. the masculine form of “new” becomes `nouvel`. the phrase is: `un nouvel appartement` — a new apartment. the noun is still masculine singular, so the article is `un`. however, `appartement` begins with a vowel sound. french uses `nouvel` before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly. [pause] example four: “some interesting books.” books are masculine plural. interesting becomes plural masculine. the phrase is: `des livres intéressants` — interesting books. `livres` is masculine plural, so the article is `des`. the adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: `intéressants`. [pause] example five: “the green cars.” cars are feminine plural. green becomes feminine plural. the phrase is: `les voitures vertes` — the green cars. `voitures` is feminine plural, so we use `les`. the color follows the noun and becomes `vertes`, with both the feminine and plural endings. [pause] --- ## part 13: your turn **narrator:** pause after each prompt and produce the french phrase. one: “a difficult problem.” [pause] suggested answer: `un problème difficile`. the noun is masculine singular, so use `un`. the adjective `difficile` follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular. two: “a beautiful city.” [pause] suggested answer: `une belle ville`. the noun is feminine singular, so `beau` changes to `belle`. this common adjective normally comes before the noun. three: “some new ideas.” [pause] suggested answer: `de nouvelles idées`. the noun is feminine plural, so `nouveau` becomes `nouvelles`. because this adjective comes before a plural noun, `des` commonly shortens to `de`. four: “the old hotels.” [pause] suggested answer: `les vieux hôtels`. the noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is `vieux`. the special singular form `vieil` is not used here because the phrase is plural. five: “a black computer.” [pause] suggested answer: `un ordinateur noir`. `ordinateur` is masculine singular. the color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: `noir`. six: “some orange bags.” [pause] suggested answer: `des sacs orange`. the noun is plural, but `orange` is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending. seven: “the serious engineers.” [pause] suggested answer: `les ingénieurs sérieux`. eight: “a canadian woman.” [pause] suggested answer: `une canadienne canadienne` is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. a more natural phrase is `une ingénieure canadienne`, meaning “a canadian female engineer.” this example shows why vocabulary and context matter. do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available. --- ## part 14: listening discrimination **narrator:** listen for the article. i will say two phrases. decide whether the noun is singular or plural. `le projet` — the project. `les projets` — the projects. [pause] the first is singular. the second is plural. `une idée` — an idea. `des idées` — ideas. [pause] the first is singular. the second is plural. now listen for adjective agreement. `un client satisfait` — a satisfied client. `une cliente satisfaite` — a satisfied female client. [pause] the second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form. `un collègue sérieux` — a serious colleague. `des collègues sérieux` — serious colleagues. [pause] the adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural. --- ## part 15: end-of-lesson summary **narrator:** here are the essential rules from today. every french noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine. learn nouns together with their articles. the definite articles are: `le` for masculine singular. `la` for feminine singular. `l’` before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words. `les` for all plural nouns. the indefinite articles are: `un` for masculine singular. `une` for feminine singular. `des` for plural nouns. most plural nouns add `-s` in writing, but the final `s` is usually silent. adjectives usually follow the noun. adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number. many feminine adjectives add `-e`. many plural adjectives add `-s`. some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order. the forms `beau`, `nouveau`, and `vieux` change before a vowel sound or silent “h.” finally, practice aloud. french grammar is not only a written system. you need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound. for your next review, do three things: first, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with `le`, `la`, or `l’`. second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases. third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective. for example: `un écran noir` — a black screen. `une tasse blanche` — a white cup. `un petit bureau` — a small office or desk. speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often. that is the end of this lesson. à bientôt! "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/tts-narration.txt","title":"Tts Narration","type":"Audio","html":"<p>Opening</p>\n<p>Welcome to your first French grammar and listening lesson.</p>\n<p>Today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. We will learn six connected ideas:</p>\n<p>First, French nouns have grammatical gender.</p>\n<p>Second, nouns can be singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Third, French uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.”</p>\n<p>Fourth, articles must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>Fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>And sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning.</p>\n<p>Do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. Your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns.</p>\n<p>When you hear “Your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Let’s begin.</p>\n<p>Part 1: What is a noun?</p>\n<p>A noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea.</p>\n<p>In English, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. In French, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>This grammatical gender is a property of the French word. It does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>le téléphone — the telephone.</p>\n<p>la voiture — the car.</p>\n<p>The book is masculine in French. The table is feminine. The telephone is masculine. The car is feminine.</p>\n<p>These labels are part of French vocabulary. Learn the noun together with its article whenever possible.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only livre. Learn le livre.</p>\n<p>Do not learn only voiture. Learn la voiture.</p>\n<p>Say them with me.</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: le livre.</p>\n<p>la voiture — the car.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: la voiture.</p>\n<p>le téléphone — the telephone.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: le téléphone.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Again: la table.</p>\n<p>This habit will help you remember gender naturally.</p>\n<p>Think of the article as part of the noun’s identity, not as a separate decoration. In English, you can often learn “table” by itself. In French, the useful unit is la table. When you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number.</p>\n<p>This also makes listening easier. Even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of le, la, or l’ gives you an immediate grammatical clue. The more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes.</p>\n<p>Part 2: The definite article: “the”</p>\n<p>The definite article is the French equivalent of “the.”</p>\n<p>For a singular masculine noun, use le.</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>For a singular feminine noun, use la.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>For a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use les.</p>\n<p>les livres — the books.</p>\n<p>les tables — the tables.</p>\n<p>There is also a shorter form, l’, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.”</p>\n<p>This shortening is called elision. French avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. Instead of saying la amie, French removes the final vowel of la and writes and pronounces l’amie.</p>\n<p>The important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun’s gender. You still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>l’ami — the friend, masculine.</p>\n<p>l’amie — the friend, feminine.</p>\n<p>l’hôtel — the hotel.</p>\n<p>l’idée — the idea.</p>\n<p>Notice that l’ does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. You must memorize the gender separately.</p>\n<p>l’ami is masculine.</p>\n<p>l’amie is feminine.</p>\n<p>l’hôtel is masculine.</p>\n<p>l’idée is feminine.</p>\n<p>Say the four forms.</p>\n<p>le livre — the book.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>la table — the table.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>les livres — the books.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>l’idée — the idea.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Quick recognition practice</p>\n<p>I will say a noun phrase. Identify the article and the number.</p>\n<p>la maison — the house.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is feminine singular.</p>\n<p>les maisons — the houses.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is plural. The noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>l’université — the university.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is singular. The noun is feminine, but l’ hides the gender in pronunciation.</p>\n<p>le projet — the project.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>Notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun’s gender. Do not guess gender from the English translation. French gender belongs to the French word.</p>\n<p>Part 3: How to learn noun gender</p>\n<p>The safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article.</p>\n<p>There are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in -tion are feminine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>la décision — the decision.</p>\n<p>la conversation — the conversation.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in -ment are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>le document — the document.</p>\n<p>le changement — the change.</p>\n<p>Many nouns ending in -isme are masculine.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>le réalisme — realism.</p>\n<p>However, French has exceptions. Patterns are clues, not guarantees.</p>\n<p>The noun l’eau, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect.</p>\n<p>When a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms:</p>\n<p>un étudiant — a male student or a student described with the masculine form.</p>\n<p>une étudiante — a female student.</p>\n<p>un ami — a male friend.</p>\n<p>une amie — a female friend.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. The article tells you the gender:</p>\n<p>le journaliste — the male journalist, or the masculine form.</p>\n<p>la journaliste — the female journalist.</p>\n<p>le professeur and la professeure are both used in modern French, depending on region and style.</p>\n<p>The key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun.</p>\n<p>When you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. A card that says livre = book trains incomplete knowledge. A better card says le livre = the book, and ideally includes a short sentence such as Le livre est intéressant — The book is interesting.</p>\n<p>Gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. When a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. Accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun.</p>\n<p>Part 4: The indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some”</p>\n<p>French has three common indefinite articles.</p>\n<p>Use un with a singular masculine noun.</p>\n<p>un livre — a book.</p>\n<p>Use une with a singular feminine noun.</p>\n<p>une table — a table.</p>\n<p>Use des with plural nouns of either gender.</p>\n<p>des livres — some books, or books.</p>\n<p>des tables — some tables, or tables.</p>\n<p>In natural English, des is often not translated as “some.” The meaning depends on context.</p>\n<p>Compare the three ideas:</p>\n<p>un ordinateur introduces one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p>l’ordinateur refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context.</p>\n<p>des ordinateurs refers to more than one non-specific computer.</p>\n<p>English often leaves the article out when translating plural French phrases. That does not mean the French article is optional. In French, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase.</p>\n<p>Listen:</p>\n<p>J’ai un ordinateur. — I have a computer.</p>\n<p>J’ai une question. — I have a question.</p>\n<p>J’ai des idées. — I have ideas.</p>\n<p>The article must agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. Before speaking, ask yourself two questions: Is the noun masculine or feminine? Is it singular or plural? The answer to those two questions determines the article.</p>\n<p>Your turn. Say the correct article aloud.</p>\n<p>A masculine project: ___ projet.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is un projet.</p>\n<p>A feminine question: ___ question.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is une question.</p>\n<p>Plural ideas: ___ idées.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is des idées.</p>\n<p>Plural computers: ___ ordinateurs.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is des ordinateurs.</p>\n<p>Part 5: Singular and plural nouns</p>\n<p>Most French nouns form the plural by adding -s in writing.</p>\n<p>un projet becomes des projets.</p>\n<p>une idée becomes des idées.</p>\n<p>le client becomes les clients.</p>\n<p>Usually, the final s is not pronounced.</p>\n<p>This creates an important difference between written and spoken French. On the page, client and clients look different. In speech, the noun may sound almost identical. The listener often relies on the article—le versus les, or un versus des—to recognize the number.</p>\n<p>The difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article:</p>\n<p>un client — a client.</p>\n<p>des clients — clients or some clients.</p>\n<p>le client — the client.</p>\n<p>les clients — the clients.</p>\n<p>Some nouns already end in -s, -x, or -z in the singular. Their spelling stays the same in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un choix — a choice.</p>\n<p>des choix — choices.</p>\n<p>un prix — a price.</p>\n<p>des prix — prices.</p>\n<p>The article and context show the number.</p>\n<p>Some nouns have special plural spellings:</p>\n<p>un bureau becomes des bureaux.</p>\n<p>un travail can become des travaux when referring to tasks or works.</p>\n<p>un animal becomes des animaux.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. Learn each new plural with its vocabulary.</p>\n<p>Liaison with plural articles</p>\n<p>When les or des comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word.</p>\n<p>The s is pronounced like a z.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>les amis sounds like “lay zah-mee.”</p>\n<p>des enfants sounds like “day zahn-fahn.”</p>\n<p>This linking is called liaison.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p>les amis — the friends.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>des enfants — children or some children.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>les étudiants — the students.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The final s of the noun itself is generally silent. The linking sound comes from the article.</p>\n<p>Listen for the boundary between the words. In les amis, do not pronounce the final s of amis as a separate sound. The z sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. This is one reason French can sound as though words run together.</p>\n<p>For now, prioritize recognition over production. First learn to hear that les amis is plural. Later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech.</p>\n<p>Do not force liaison everywhere. First become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases.</p>\n<p>Part 6: Adjectives describe nouns</p>\n<p>An adjective gives information about a noun.</p>\n<p>It can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics.</p>\n<p>In French, the adjective usually comes after the noun.</p>\n<p>un projet intéressant — an interesting project.</p>\n<p>une idée originale — an original idea.</p>\n<p>un problème difficile — a difficult problem.</p>\n<p>une solution simple — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>The adjective can also follow the verb être, meaning “to be.”</p>\n<p>Le projet est intéressant. — The project is interesting.</p>\n<p>La solution est simple. — The solution is simple.</p>\n<p>The most important rule is agreement.</p>\n<p>The adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>In English, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” French makes the relationship visible in writing. The ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>When you build a French noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. The adjective is not agreeing with the English translation; it is agreeing with the French noun.</p>\n<p>Part 7: Masculine and feminine adjective forms</p>\n<p>For many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding -e to the masculine form.</p>\n<p>un client intelligent — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase.</p>\n<p>une cliente intelligente — an intelligent female client.</p>\n<p>The adjective changes from intelligent to intelligente.</p>\n<p>Another example:</p>\n<p>un employé fatigué — a tired employee, masculine form.</p>\n<p>une employée fatiguée — a tired employee, feminine form.</p>\n<p>Repeat:</p>\n<p>intelligent, intelligente — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>fatigué, fatiguée — tired, masculine and feminine forms.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives already end in a silent e in the masculine form. They do not change spelling in the feminine.</p>\n<p>un collègue calme — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p>une collègue calme — a calm colleague.</p>\n<p>un enfant drôle — a funny child.</p>\n<p>une enfant drôle — a funny child.</p>\n<p>The adjective is the same in writing. The noun or article gives more information.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that a word ending in -e is automatically feminine. The ending can be identical in both genders. For example, calme stays calme, but the complete phrases un collègue calme and une collègue calme still show different gender through the article and noun.</p>\n<p>This is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. You are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives have special feminine forms:</p>\n<p>actif becomes active.</p>\n<p>heureux becomes heureuse.</p>\n<p>sérieux becomes sérieuse.</p>\n<p>sportif becomes sportive.</p>\n<p>beau becomes belle.</p>\n<p>Do not try to derive every form from English. Learn the pair together.</p>\n<p>Some changes are predictable, such as adding -e, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. Treat irregular forms as families: actif, active; heureux, heureuse; beau, belle. Say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase.</p>\n<p>Part 8: Adjective plurals</p>\n<p>The regular plural of an adjective usually adds -s in writing.</p>\n<p>un projet intéressant — an interesting project.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p>des projets intéressants — interesting projects.</p>\n<p>For feminine plural:</p>\n<p>une solution simple — a simple solution.</p>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<p>des solutions simples — simple solutions.</p>\n<p>Notice that an adjective ending in a silent e may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds s.</p>\n<p>Some adjectives ending in -s or -x do not change their spelling in the plural.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un homme sérieux — a serious man.</p>\n<p>des hommes sérieux — serious men.</p>\n<p>The written adjective remains sérieux.</p>\n<p>A useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. In des hommes sérieux, des signals plural, hommes is the plural noun, and sérieux is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. Even when the adjective’s spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active.</p>\n<p>If a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written French normally uses the masculine plural form.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un frère et une sœur intelligents — an intelligent brother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun.</p>\n<p>For an entirely feminine group:</p>\n<p>une mère et une sœur intelligentes — an intelligent mother and sister.</p>\n<p>The adjective is feminine plural.</p>\n<p>Part 9: Colors</p>\n<p>Color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun.</p>\n<p>un écran noir — a black screen.</p>\n<p>une voiture noire — a black car.</p>\n<p>des écrans noirs — black screens.</p>\n<p>des voitures noires — black cars.</p>\n<p>Many colors follow the regular pattern:</p>\n<p>bleu, bleue — blue.</p>\n<p>vert, verte — green.</p>\n<p>gris, grise — gray.</p>\n<p>blanc, blanche — white.</p>\n<p>Some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>orange and marron are commonly invariable.</p>\n<p>un sac orange — an orange bag.</p>\n<p>une chemise orange — an orange shirt.</p>\n<p>des sacs orange — orange bags.</p>\n<p>When a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine:</p>\n<p>J’aime le bleu. — I like blue.</p>\n<p>Le rouge est vif. — Red is vivid.</p>\n<p>For now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns.</p>\n<p>Color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. Look around you and describe objects you can see. Start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending.</p>\n<p>Part 10: Adjectives before the noun</p>\n<p>Most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Examples include words related to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>size;</li>\n<li>age;</li>\n<li>beauty;</li>\n<li>goodness;</li>\n<li>quantity or order;</li>\n<li>and certain familiar descriptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Listen to these phrases:</p>\n<p>une grande ville — a large city.</p>\n<p>un petit problème — a small problem.</p>\n<p>un bon résultat — a good result.</p>\n<p>une jeune équipe — a young team.</p>\n<p>un vieux bâtiment — an old building.</p>\n<p>The adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it.</p>\n<p>Adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. Whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. Compare un petit projet and une petite tâche: the adjective moves with the noun’s gender.</p>\n<p>At the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. Do not try to memorize a long abstract list. Repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun.</p>\n<p>un petit projet — a small project.</p>\n<p>une petite tâche — a small task.</p>\n<p>de petits projets — some small projects.</p>\n<p>de petites tâches — some small tasks.</p>\n<p>Three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “beautiful” can be:</p>\n<p>beau before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>bel before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>belle with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>un beau quartier — a beautiful neighborhood.</p>\n<p>un bel appartement — a beautiful apartment.</p>\n<p>une belle maison — a beautiful house.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “new” can be:</p>\n<p>nouveau before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>nouvel before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>nouvelle with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>un nouveau projet — a new project.</p>\n<p>un nouvel emploi — a new job.</p>\n<p>une nouvelle idée — a new idea.</p>\n<p>The adjective meaning “old” can be:</p>\n<p>vieux before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>vieil before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>vieille with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>un vieux livre — an old book.</p>\n<p>un vieil ordinateur — an old computer.</p>\n<p>une vieille voiture — an old car.</p>\n<p>Practice the pattern:</p>\n<p>beau, bel, belle — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>vieux, vieil, vieille — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Meaning can change with position</p>\n<p>Sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun.</p>\n<p>This is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. French speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. When you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase.</p>\n<p>For example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters.</p>\n<p>Similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy.</p>\n<p>Do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. Just notice that adjective position can matter.</p>\n<p>Part 11: Nationalities and languages</p>\n<p>Nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns.</p>\n<p>As adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe:</p>\n<p>un ingénieur canadien — a Canadian male engineer.</p>\n<p>une ingénieure canadienne — a Canadian female engineer.</p>\n<p>des ingénieurs canadiens — Canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers.</p>\n<p>des ingénieures canadiennes — Canadian female engineers.</p>\n<p>Nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in French:</p>\n<p>un Canadien — a Canadian man.</p>\n<p>une Canadienne — a Canadian woman.</p>\n<p>But nationality adjectives are not capitalized:</p>\n<p>un collègue canadien — a Canadian colleague.</p>\n<p>Language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized:</p>\n<p>le français — French, the language.</p>\n<p>l’anglais — English, the language.</p>\n<p>l’espagnol — Spanish, the language.</p>\n<p>Say these phrases:</p>\n<p>Je parle français. — I speak French.</p>\n<p>Elle apprend l’anglais. — She is learning English.</p>\n<p>Nous étudions l’espagnol. — We study Spanish.</p>\n<p>Part 12: Guided review</p>\n<p>Let’s review the complete system.</p>\n<p>Step one: identify the noun.</p>\n<p>Step two: identify its gender.</p>\n<p>Step three: identify whether it is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>Step four: choose the article.</p>\n<p>Step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree.</p>\n<p>This five-step routine is the main skill you are building. Do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. Work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending.</p>\n<p>With practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. At the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them.</p>\n<p>Let’s do examples together.</p>\n<p>Example one:</p>\n<p>“A small house.”</p>\n<p>House is maison, a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Small is petit, which becomes petite with a feminine noun.</p>\n<p>Because the noun is singular and indefinite, use une.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>une petite maison — a small house.</p>\n<p>Notice the reasoning: maison is feminine, so we need une. The adjective petit becomes petite to agree with the feminine noun. Because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is une petite maison.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example two:</p>\n<p>“The new project.”</p>\n<p>Project is masculine singular.</p>\n<p>New is nouveau before a consonant sound.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>le nouveau projet — the new project.</p>\n<p>Here, projet is masculine singular, so we use le. The noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is nouveau, not nouvel. The adjective comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example three:</p>\n<p>“A new apartment.”</p>\n<p>Apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound.</p>\n<p>The masculine form of “new” becomes nouvel.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>un nouvel appartement — a new apartment.</p>\n<p>The noun is still masculine singular, so the article is un. However, appartement begins with a vowel sound. French uses nouvel before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example four:</p>\n<p>“Some interesting books.”</p>\n<p>Books are masculine plural.</p>\n<p>Interesting becomes plural masculine.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>des livres intéressants — interesting books.</p>\n<p>livres is masculine plural, so the article is des. The adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: intéressants.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Example five:</p>\n<p>“The green cars.”</p>\n<p>Cars are feminine plural.</p>\n<p>Green becomes feminine plural.</p>\n<p>The phrase is:</p>\n<p>les voitures vertes — the green cars.</p>\n<p>voitures is feminine plural, so we use les. The color follows the noun and becomes vertes, with both the feminine and plural endings.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>Part 13: Your turn</p>\n<p>Pause after each prompt and produce the French phrase aloud.</p>\n<p>One: “a difficult problem.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is un problème difficile.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine singular, so use un. The adjective difficile follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular.</p>\n<p>Two: “a beautiful city.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is une belle ville.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine singular, so beau changes to belle. This common adjective normally comes before the noun.</p>\n<p>Three: “some new ideas.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is de nouvelles idées.</p>\n<p>The noun is feminine plural, so nouveau becomes nouvelles. Because this adjective comes before a plural noun, des commonly shortens to de.</p>\n<p>Four: “the old hotels.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is les vieux hôtels.</p>\n<p>The noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is vieux. The special singular form vieil is not used here because the phrase is plural.</p>\n<p>Five: “a black computer.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is un ordinateur noir.</p>\n<p>ordinateur is masculine singular. The color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: noir.</p>\n<p>Six: “some orange bags.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is des sacs orange.</p>\n<p>The noun is plural, but orange is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending.</p>\n<p>Seven: “the serious engineers.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is les ingénieurs sérieux.</p>\n<p>Eight: “a Canadian woman.”</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The answer is une Canadienne canadienne is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. A more natural phrase is une ingénieure canadienne, meaning “a Canadian female engineer.”</p>\n<p>This example shows why vocabulary and context matter. Do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available.</p>\n<p>Part 14: Listening discrimination</p>\n<p>Listen for the article. I will say two phrases. Decide whether the noun is singular or plural.</p>\n<p>le projet — the project.</p>\n<p>les projets — the projects.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p>une idée — an idea.</p>\n<p>des idées — ideas.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The first is singular. The second is plural.</p>\n<p>Now listen for adjective agreement.</p>\n<p>un client satisfait — a satisfied client.</p>\n<p>une cliente satisfaite — a satisfied female client.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form.</p>\n<p>un collègue sérieux — a serious colleague.</p>\n<p>des collègues sérieux — serious colleagues.</p>\n<p>Pause now and answer aloud.</p>\n<p>The adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural.</p>\n<p>Part 15: End-of-lesson summary</p>\n<p>Here are the essential rules from today.</p>\n<p>Every French noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine.</p>\n<p>Learn nouns together with their articles.</p>\n<p>The definite articles are:</p>\n<p>le for masculine singular.</p>\n<p>la for feminine singular.</p>\n<p>l’ before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words.</p>\n<p>les for all plural nouns.</p>\n<p>The indefinite articles are:</p>\n<p>un for masculine singular.</p>\n<p>une for feminine singular.</p>\n<p>des for plural nouns.</p>\n<p>Most plural nouns add -s in writing, but the final s is usually silent.</p>\n<p>Adjectives usually follow the noun.</p>\n<p>Adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number.</p>\n<p>Many feminine adjectives add -e.</p>\n<p>Many plural adjectives add -s.</p>\n<p>Some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order.</p>\n<p>The forms beau, nouveau, and vieux change before a vowel sound or silent “h.”</p>\n<p>Finally, practice aloud. French grammar is not only a written system. You need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound.</p>\n<p>For your next review, do three things:</p>\n<p>First, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with le, la, or l’.</p>\n<p>Second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases.</p>\n<p>Third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<p>un écran noir — a black screen.</p>\n<p>une tasse blanche — a white cup.</p>\n<p>un petit bureau — a small office or desk.</p>\n<p>Speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often.</p>\n<p>That is the end of this lesson. À bientôt!</p>","search":"opening welcome to your first french grammar and listening lesson. today we will build the foundation for describing people, places, and things. we will learn six connected ideas: first, french nouns have grammatical gender. second, nouns can be singular or plural. third, french uses articles, such as “the,” “a,” and “some.” fourth, articles must agree with the noun. fifth, descriptive adjectives usually agree with the noun in gender and number. and sixth, adjectives can appear before or after the noun, depending on the adjective and sometimes on the meaning. do not try to memorize everything in one sitting. your goal today is to understand the system and begin recognizing its patterns. when you hear “your turn,” pause the audio and say the answer aloud. pause now and answer aloud. let’s begin. part 1: what is a noun? a noun is a word for a person, place, object, animal, or idea. in english, nouns do not normally have grammatical gender. in french, every noun is classified as either masculine or feminine. this grammatical gender is a property of the french word. it does not always correspond to the physical sex of the thing. for example: le livre — the book. la table — the table. le téléphone — the telephone. la voiture — the car. the book is masculine in french. the table is feminine. the telephone is masculine. the car is feminine. these labels are part of french vocabulary. learn the noun together with its article whenever possible. do not learn only livre. learn le livre. do not learn only voiture. learn la voiture. say them with me. le livre — the book. pause now and answer aloud. again: le livre. la voiture — the car. pause now and answer aloud. again: la voiture. le téléphone — the telephone. pause now and answer aloud. again: le téléphone. la table — the table. pause now and answer aloud. again: la table. this habit will help you remember gender naturally. think of the article as part of the noun’s identity, not as a separate decoration. in english, you can often learn “table” by itself. in french, the useful unit is la table. when you later build a sentence, the article gives you the first signal about gender and number. this also makes listening easier. even if the noun is unfamiliar, the sound of le, la, or l’ gives you an immediate grammatical clue. the more often you hear the complete phrase, the more automatic that clue becomes. part 2: the definite article: “the” the definite article is the french equivalent of “the.” for a singular masculine noun, use le. le livre — the book. for a singular feminine noun, use la. la table — the table. for a plural noun, whether masculine or feminine, use les. les livres — the books. les tables — the tables. there is also a shorter form, l’, used before a vowel sound or before most words beginning with a silent “h.” this shortening is called elision. french avoids putting two vowel sounds next to each other when the article comes before a vowel-starting word. instead of saying la amie, french removes the final vowel of la and writes and pronounces l’amie. the important point is that elision changes the sound and spelling of the article, but it does not remove the noun’s gender. you still need to remember whether the noun is masculine or feminine. for example: l’ami — the friend, masculine. l’amie — the friend, feminine. l’hôtel — the hotel. l’idée — the idea. notice that l’ does not tell you whether the noun is masculine or feminine. you must memorize the gender separately. l’ami is masculine. l’amie is feminine. l’hôtel is masculine. l’idée is feminine. say the four forms. le livre — the book. pause now and answer aloud. la table — the table. pause now and answer aloud. les livres — the books. pause now and answer aloud. l’idée — the idea. pause now and answer aloud. quick recognition practice i will say a noun phrase. identify the article and the number. la maison — the house. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is feminine singular. les maisons — the houses. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is plural. the noun could refer to a group of feminine houses, but the plural article itself does not show masculine or feminine. l’université — the university. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is singular. the noun is feminine, but l’ hides the gender in pronunciation. le projet — the project. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is masculine singular. notice the process: first identify the article, then ask whether the noun is singular or plural, and finally recall the noun’s gender. do not guess gender from the english translation. french gender belongs to the french word. part 3: how to learn noun gender the safest method is simple: memorize the noun with its article. there are patterns that can help, but they are not perfect rules. many nouns ending in -tion are feminine. for example: la décision — the decision. la conversation — the conversation. many nouns ending in -ment are masculine. for example: le document — the document. le changement — the change. many nouns ending in -isme are masculine. for example: le réalisme — realism. however, french has exceptions. patterns are clues, not guarantees. the noun l’eau, meaning “water,” is feminine even though it does not fit the pattern you might expect. when a noun refers to a person, the word may change for masculine and feminine forms: un étudiant — a male student or a student described with the masculine form. une étudiante — a female student. un ami — a male friend. une amie — a female friend. some nouns have the same spelling for a man or a woman. the article tells you the gender: le journaliste — the male journalist, or the masculine form. la journaliste — the female journalist. le professeur and la professeure are both used in modern french, depending on region and style. the key learning strategy is still the same: store the article with the noun. when you make vocabulary cards, put the article on the front of the card. a card that says livre = book trains incomplete knowledge. a better card says le livre = the book, and ideally includes a short sentence such as le livre est intéressant — the book is interesting. gender patterns are useful for making an educated guess, but they should not replace memorization. when a new word breaks a pattern, record the complete phrase and move on. accuracy grows through repeated exposure, not through trying to invent a perfect rule for every noun. part 4: the indefinite article: “a,” “an,” and “some” french has three common indefinite articles. use un with a singular masculine noun. un livre — a book. use une with a singular feminine noun. une table — a table. use des with plural nouns of either gender. des livres — some books, or books. des tables — some tables, or tables. in natural english, des is often not translated as “some.” the meaning depends on context. compare the three ideas: un ordinateur introduces one non-specific computer. l’ordinateur refers to a specific computer, or to computers as a general category depending on context. des ordinateurs refers to more than one non-specific computer. english often leaves the article out when translating plural french phrases. that does not mean the french article is optional. in french, the article is normally part of the complete noun phrase. listen: j’ai un ordinateur. — i have a computer. j’ai une question. — i have a question. j’ai des idées. — i have ideas. the article must agree with the noun. this is a decision-making exercise, not just a pronunciation exercise. before speaking, ask yourself two questions: is the noun masculine or feminine? is it singular or plural? the answer to those two questions determines the article. your turn. say the correct article aloud. a masculine project: ___ projet. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is un projet. a feminine question: ___ question. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is une question. plural ideas: ___ idées. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is des idées. plural computers: ___ ordinateurs. pause now and answer aloud. the answer is des ordinateurs. part 5: singular and plural nouns most french nouns form the plural by adding -s in writing. un projet becomes des projets. une idée becomes des idées. le client becomes les clients. usually, the final s is not pronounced. this creates an important difference between written and spoken french. on the page, client and clients look different. in speech, the noun may sound almost identical. the listener often relies on the article—le versus les, or un versus des—to recognize the number. the difference between singular and plural is often easier to hear in the article: un client — a client. des clients — clients or some clients. le client — the client. les clients — the clients. some nouns already end in -s, -x, or -z in the singular. their spelling stays the same in the plural. for example: un choix — a choice. des choix — choices. un prix — a price. des prix — prices. the article and context show the number. some nouns have special plural spellings: un bureau becomes des bureaux. un travail can become des travaux when referring to tasks or works. un animal becomes des animaux. do not worry about memorizing every irregular plural today. learn each new plural with its vocabulary. liaison with plural articles when les or des comes before a word beginning with a vowel sound, the final sound of the article may link to the next word. the s is pronounced like a z. for example: les amis sounds like “lay zah-mee.” des enfants sounds like “day zahn-fahn.” this linking is called liaison. repeat: les amis — the friends. pause now and answer aloud. des enfants — children or some children. pause now and answer aloud. les étudiants — the students. pause now and answer aloud. the final s of the noun itself is generally silent. the linking sound comes from the article. listen for the boundary between the words. in les amis, do not pronounce the final s of amis as a separate sound. the z sound belongs to the article and connects the two words smoothly. this is one reason french can sound as though words run together. for now, prioritize recognition over production. first learn to hear that les amis is plural. later, with repeated shadowing, the liaison will become natural in your own speech. do not force liaison everywhere. first become comfortable recognizing it in common phrases. part 6: adjectives describe nouns an adjective gives information about a noun. it can describe size, color, personality, quality, age, or many other characteristics. in french, the adjective usually comes after the noun. un projet intéressant — an interesting project. une idée originale — an original idea. un problème difficile — a difficult problem. une solution simple — a simple solution. the adjective can also follow the verb être, meaning “to be.” le projet est intéressant. — the project is interesting. la solution est simple. — the solution is simple. the most important rule is agreement. the adjective agrees with the noun in gender and number. in english, the adjective usually stays unchanged: “an interesting project” and “interesting projects.” french makes the relationship visible in writing. the ending of the adjective tells us whether it describes a masculine or feminine noun and whether that noun is singular or plural. when you build a french noun phrase, use this order of thought: choose the noun, choose the article, place the adjective in its normal position, and then adjust the adjective to match the noun. the adjective is not agreeing with the english translation; it is agreeing with the french noun. part 7: masculine and feminine adjective forms for many adjectives, the feminine form is created by adding -e to the masculine form. un client intelligent — an intelligent male client or a masculine phrase. une cliente intelligente — an intelligent female client. the adjective changes from intelligent to intelligente. another example: un employé fatigué — a tired employee, masculine form. une employée fatiguée — a tired employee, feminine form. repeat: intelligent, intelligente — intelligent, masculine and feminine forms. pause now and answer aloud. fatigué, fatiguée — tired, masculine and feminine forms. pause now and answer aloud. some adjectives already end in a silent e in the masculine form. they do not change spelling in the feminine. un collègue calme — a calm colleague. une collègue calme — a calm colleague. un enfant drôle — a funny child. une enfant drôle — a funny child. the adjective is the same in writing. the noun or article gives more information. do not assume that a word ending in -e is automatically feminine. the ending can be identical in both genders. for example, calme stays calme, but the complete phrases un collègue calme and une collègue calme still show different gender through the article and noun. this is another reason to study complete phrases instead of isolated adjective lists. you are training yourself to notice the whole grammatical pattern. some adjectives have special feminine forms: actif becomes active. heureux becomes heureuse. sérieux becomes sérieuse. sportif becomes sportive. beau becomes belle. do not try to derive every form from english. learn the pair together. some changes are predictable, such as adding -e, while others involve spelling or pronunciation changes. treat irregular forms as families: actif, active; heureux, heureuse; beau, belle. say both forms aloud and place each one in a short phrase. part 8: adjective plurals the regular plural of an adjective usually adds -s in writing. un projet intéressant — an interesting project. becomes: des projets intéressants — interesting projects. for feminine plural: une solution simple — a simple solution. becomes: des solutions simples — simple solutions. notice that an adjective ending in a silent e may look the same in singular masculine and feminine, but the plural usually adds s. some adjectives ending in -s or -x do not change their spelling in the plural. for example: un homme sérieux — a serious man. des hommes sérieux — serious men. the written adjective remains sérieux. a useful reading habit is to scan from left to right and ask what each ending is doing. in des hommes sérieux, des signals plural, hommes is the plural noun, and sérieux is the adjective that agrees with that plural noun. even when the adjective’s spelling does not visibly change, the grammar is still active. if a group contains both masculine and feminine nouns, standard written french normally uses the masculine plural form. for example: un frère et une sœur intelligents — an intelligent brother and sister. the adjective is masculine plural because the group includes a masculine noun. for an entirely feminine group: une mère et une sœur intelligentes — an intelligent mother and sister. the adjective is feminine plural. part 9: colors color words are adjectives, so they usually agree with the noun. un écran noir — a black screen. une voiture noire — a black car. des écrans noirs — black screens. des voitures noires — black cars. many colors follow the regular pattern: bleu, bleue — blue. vert, verte — green. gris, grise — gray. blanc, blanche — white. some color words do not change for gender or number when used as adjectives. for example: orange and marron are commonly invariable. un sac orange — an orange bag. une chemise orange — an orange shirt. des sacs orange — orange bags. when a color word is used as a noun, it is masculine: j’aime le bleu. — i like blue. le rouge est vif. — red is vivid. for now, focus on using colors as adjectives after nouns. color is an excellent way to practice agreement because the meaning is concrete. look around you and describe objects you can see. start with the noun and article, then add the color, and finally check whether the color needs a feminine or plural ending. part 10: adjectives before the noun most descriptive adjectives follow the noun, but a group of common adjectives usually comes before the noun. examples include words related to: - size; - age; - beauty; - goodness; - quantity or order; - and certain familiar descriptions. listen to these phrases: une grande ville — a large city. un petit problème — a small problem. un bon résultat — a good result. une jeune équipe — a young team. un vieux bâtiment — an old building. the adjective still agrees with the noun even when it comes before it. adjective position is not a reason to skip agreement. whether the adjective comes before or after the noun, the noun still controls its form. compare un petit projet and une petite tâche: the adjective moves with the noun’s gender. at the beginner level, learn the common position together with each adjective. do not try to memorize a long abstract list. repeated phrases will teach you which adjectives normally come before the noun. un petit projet — a small project. une petite tâche — a small task. de petits projets — some small projects. de petites tâches — some small tasks. three especially important adjectives have extra masculine forms before a vowel sound or silent “h.” the adjective meaning “beautiful” can be: beau before a consonant sound. bel before a vowel sound or silent “h.” belle with a feminine noun. examples: un beau quartier — a beautiful neighborhood. un bel appartement — a beautiful apartment. une belle maison — a beautiful house. the adjective meaning “new” can be: nouveau before a consonant sound. nouvel before a vowel sound or silent “h.” nouvelle with a feminine noun. examples: un nouveau projet — a new project. un nouvel emploi — a new job. une nouvelle idée — a new idea. the adjective meaning “old” can be: vieux before a consonant sound. vieil before a vowel sound or silent “h.” vieille with a feminine noun. examples: un vieux livre — an old book. un vieil ordinateur — an old computer. une vieille voiture — an old car. practice the pattern: beau, bel, belle — beautiful, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. pause now and answer aloud. nouveau, nouvel, nouvelle — new, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. pause now and answer aloud. vieux, vieil, vieille — old, masculine consonant form, masculine vowel form, and feminine form. pause now and answer aloud. meaning can change with position sometimes an adjective changes meaning depending on whether it comes before or after the noun. this is a meaning issue, not only a word-order issue. french speakers use position to communicate whether a description is familiar, subjective, literal, or part of a fixed expression. when you meet a new adjective, notice both its position and the meaning of the complete phrase. for example, “ancien professeur” can mean a former professor, while “professeur ancien” would suggest an old or ancient professor, although the second phrase is less common and context matters. similarly, “un pauvre homme” often means an unfortunate or pitiable man, while “un homme pauvre” means a man who is not wealthy. do not worry about mastering every meaning contrast at the beginning. just notice that adjective position can matter. part 11: nationalities and languages nationality words can function as adjectives or nouns. as adjectives, nationality words agree with the person or thing they describe: un ingénieur canadien — a canadian male engineer. une ingénieure canadienne — a canadian female engineer. des ingénieurs canadiens — canadian male engineers or a mixed-gender group of engineers. des ingénieures canadiennes — canadian female engineers. nationality nouns referring to people are normally capitalized in french: un canadien — a canadian man. une canadienne — a canadian woman. but nationality adjectives are not capitalized: un collègue canadien — a canadian colleague. language names are masculine nouns and are not capitalized: le français — french, the language. l’anglais — english, the language. l’espagnol — spanish, the language. say these phrases: je parle français. — i speak french. elle apprend l’anglais. — she is learning english. nous étudions l’espagnol. — we study spanish. part 12: guided review let’s review the complete system. step one: identify the noun. step two: identify its gender. step three: identify whether it is singular or plural. step four: choose the article. step five: if there is an adjective, make the adjective agree. this five-step routine is the main skill you are building. do not try to produce the entire phrase at once. work from the inside out: identify the noun, recall its gender, choose the article, place the adjective, and then check the adjective ending. with practice, these steps will happen almost simultaneously. at the beginning, it is completely normal to pause and reason through them. let’s do examples together. example one: “a small house.” house is maison, a feminine noun. small is petit, which becomes petite with a feminine noun. because the noun is singular and indefinite, use une. the phrase is: une petite maison — a small house. notice the reasoning: maison is feminine, so we need une. the adjective petit becomes petite to agree with the feminine noun. because this adjective normally comes before the noun, the final phrase is une petite maison. pause now and answer aloud. example two: “the new project.” project is masculine singular. new is nouveau before a consonant sound. the phrase is: le nouveau projet — the new project. here, projet is masculine singular, so we use le. the noun begins with a consonant sound, so the masculine form is nouveau, not nouvel. the adjective comes before the noun. pause now and answer aloud. example three: “a new apartment.” apartment is masculine and begins with a vowel sound. the masculine form of “new” becomes nouvel. the phrase is: un nouvel appartement — a new apartment. the noun is still masculine singular, so the article is un. however, appartement begins with a vowel sound. french uses nouvel before that sound to make the phrase flow more smoothly. pause now and answer aloud. example four: “some interesting books.” books are masculine plural. interesting becomes plural masculine. the phrase is: des livres intéressants — interesting books. livres is masculine plural, so the article is des. the adjective follows the noun and receives the masculine plural ending: intéressants. pause now and answer aloud. example five: “the green cars.” cars are feminine plural. green becomes feminine plural. the phrase is: les voitures vertes — the green cars. voitures is feminine plural, so we use les. the color follows the noun and becomes vertes, with both the feminine and plural endings. pause now and answer aloud. part 13: your turn pause after each prompt and produce the french phrase aloud. one: “a difficult problem.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is un problème difficile. the noun is masculine singular, so use un. the adjective difficile follows the noun and has the same spelling in masculine and feminine singular. two: “a beautiful city.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is une belle ville. the noun is feminine singular, so beau changes to belle. this common adjective normally comes before the noun. three: “some new ideas.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is de nouvelles idées. the noun is feminine plural, so nouveau becomes nouvelles. because this adjective comes before a plural noun, des commonly shortens to de. four: “the old hotels.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is les vieux hôtels. the noun is masculine plural, so the adjective is vieux. the special singular form vieil is not used here because the phrase is plural. five: “a black computer.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is un ordinateur noir. ordinateur is masculine singular. the color adjective follows the noun and remains masculine singular: noir. six: “some orange bags.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is des sacs orange. the noun is plural, but orange is an invariable color adjective in this use, so it does not add a plural ending. seven: “the serious engineers.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is les ingénieurs sérieux. eight: “a canadian woman.” pause now and answer aloud. the answer is une canadienne canadienne is grammatically possible but repetitive and unnatural. a more natural phrase is une ingénieure canadienne, meaning “a canadian female engineer.” this example shows why vocabulary and context matter. do not translate word by word when a more natural phrase is available. part 14: listening discrimination listen for the article. i will say two phrases. decide whether the noun is singular or plural. le projet — the project. les projets — the projects. pause now and answer aloud. the first is singular. the second is plural. une idée — an idea. des idées — ideas. pause now and answer aloud. the first is singular. the second is plural. now listen for adjective agreement. un client satisfait — a satisfied client. une cliente satisfaite — a satisfied female client. pause now and answer aloud. the second phrase has both a feminine noun form and a feminine adjective form. un collègue sérieux — a serious colleague. des collègues sérieux — serious colleagues. pause now and answer aloud. the adjective looks the same in writing, but the article and noun show the plural. part 15: end-of-lesson summary here are the essential rules from today. every french noun has a grammatical gender: masculine or feminine. learn nouns together with their articles. the definite articles are: le for masculine singular. la for feminine singular. l’ before a vowel sound or most silent “h” words. les for all plural nouns. the indefinite articles are: un for masculine singular. une for feminine singular. des for plural nouns. most plural nouns add -s in writing, but the final s is usually silent. adjectives usually follow the noun. adjectives agree with the noun in gender and number. many feminine adjectives add -e. many plural adjectives add -s. some common adjectives usually come before the noun, especially adjectives related to size, age, beauty, goodness, and order. the forms beau, nouveau, and vieux change before a vowel sound or silent “h.” finally, practice aloud. french grammar is not only a written system. you need to connect the article, noun, and adjective in sound. for your next review, do three things: first, choose ten nouns and memorize each one with le, la, or l’. second, create five singular phrases and turn them into plural phrases. third, describe five objects around you using a noun and an adjective. for example: un écran noir — a black screen. une tasse blanche — a white cup. un petit bureau — a small office or desk. speak slowly, listen carefully, and repeat often. that is the end of this lesson. à bientôt! "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/review/error-log.md","title":"Error Log","type":"Resource","html":"<h1 id=\"error-log\">Error Log</h1>\n<p>Record errors by rule rather than merely copying the correct answer.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>My answer</th>\n<th>Correct answer</th>\n<th>Rule I missed</th>\n<th>New example from memory</th>\n<th>Retest date</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"error-categories\">Error categories</h2>\n<p>Use one of these labels:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>noun gender</li>\n<li>definite article</li>\n<li>indefinite article</li>\n<li>noun plural</li>\n<li>adjective gender</li>\n<li>adjective plural</li>\n<li>adjective position</li>\n<li>special adjective form</li>\n<li>spelling/accent</li>\n<li>pronunciation/listening</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An error is closed only after you answer a new example correctly from memory on a later day.</p>","search":"# error log record errors by rule rather than merely copying the correct answer. | date | my answer | correct answer | rule i missed | new example from memory | retest date | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | | ## error categories use one of these labels: - noun gender - definite article - indefinite article - noun plural - adjective gender - adjective plural - adjective position - special adjective form - spelling/accent - pronunciation/listening an error is closed only after you answer a new example correctly from memory on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/lessons/01-gender-and-definite-articles.md","title":"Lesson 1: Gender and Definite Articles","type":"Resource","html":"<h1 id=\"lesson-1-gender-and-definite-articles\">Lesson 1: Gender and Definite Articles</h1>\n<h2 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h2>\n<p>Every French noun has grammatical gender. Learn the article with the noun:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jardin</code> — the garden</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la fenêtre</code> — the window</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'école</code> — the school</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les voisins</code> — the neighbors</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treat <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le jardin</code> as one vocabulary unit rather than memorizing <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">jardin</code> alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"definite-articles\">Definite articles</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the + masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le café</code></td>\n<td>the café</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the + feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la chaise</code></td>\n<td>the chair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the + singular before a vowel sound</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'ami</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'histoire</code></td>\n<td>the friend, the story</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the + plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les cafés</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les chaises</code></td>\n<td>the cafés, the chairs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'</code> hides the noun's gender, so record the gender separately:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'arbre</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">m.</code>) — the tree</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">l'adresse</code> (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">f.</code>) — the address</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"useful-patterns-not-guarantees\">Useful patterns, not guarantees</h2>\n<p>Some endings frequently suggest gender:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Often feminine: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-tion</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-sion</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-té</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ette</code></li>\n<li>Often masculine: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-age</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-ment</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-eau</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-isme</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>There are exceptions. Use these patterns to make a first guess, then verify.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h2>\n<p>Without looking above, write the four French forms of “the.” Then add the correct article:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>___ musée</li>\n<li>___ question</li>\n<li>___ hôtel</li>\n<li>___ étudiants</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h2>\n<p>Look around your room. Find four objects, look up each noun, and write it with its article. Say each phrase twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud twice. Then cover it and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le musée est près de la gare.</code> — The museum is near the train station.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La question est importante.</code> — The question is important.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.</code> — The hotel is downtown.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiants regardent le tableau.</code> — The students are looking at the board.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'adresse est sur le document.</code> — The address is on the document.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentence 1 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">restaurant</code>, sentence 2 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">réponse</code>, and sentence 4 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">professeurs</code>. Adjust the articles as needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write three sentences about places or objects near you. Use one masculine noun, one feminine noun, and one plural noun.</p>","search":"# lesson 1: gender and definite articles ## core idea every french noun has grammatical gender. learn the article with the noun: - `le jardin` — the garden - `la fenêtre` — the window - `l'école` — the school - `les voisins` — the neighbors treat `le jardin` as one vocabulary unit rather than memorizing `jardin` alone. ## definite articles | meaning | form | french example | english | |---|---|---|---| | the + masculine singular | `le` | `le café` | the café | | the + feminine singular | `la` | `la chaise` | the chair | | the + singular before a vowel sound | `l'` | `l'ami`, `l'histoire` | the friend, the story | | the + plural | `les` | `les cafés`, `les chaises` | the cafés, the chairs | `l'` hides the noun's gender, so record the gender separately: - `l'arbre` (`m.`) — the tree - `l'adresse` (`f.`) — the address ## useful patterns, not guarantees some endings frequently suggest gender: - often feminine: `-tion`, `-sion`, `-té`, `-ette` - often masculine: `-age`, `-ment`, `-eau`, `-isme` there are exceptions. use these patterns to make a first guess, then verify. ## retrieval check without looking above, write the four french forms of “the.” then add the correct article: 1. ___ musée 2. ___ question 3. ___ hôtel 4. ___ étudiants ## production task look around your room. find four objects, look up each noun, and write it with its article. say each phrase twice. ## sentence usage practice read each sentence aloud twice. then cover it and write it from memory. 1. `le musée est près de la gare.` — the museum is near the train station. 2. `la question est importante.` — the question is important. 3. `l'hôtel est dans le centre-ville.` — the hotel is downtown. 4. `les étudiants regardent le tableau.` — the students are looking at the board. 5. `l'adresse est sur le document.` — the address is on the document. ### change the sentence rewrite sentence 1 with `restaurant`, sentence 2 with `réponse`, and sentence 4 with `professeurs`. adjust the articles as needed. ### make it personal write three sentences about places or objects near you. use one masculine noun, one feminine noun, and one plural noun. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/lessons/02-indefinite-articles-and-plurals.md","title":"Lesson 2: Indefinite Articles and Plurals","type":"Resource","html":"<h1 id=\"lesson-2-indefinite-articles-and-plurals\">Lesson 2: Indefinite Articles and Plurals</h1>\n<h2 id=\"indefinite-articles\">Indefinite articles</h2>\n<p>Use these forms for “a,” “an,” or plural “some”:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Noun</th>\n<th>Article</th>\n<th>French example</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un cahier</code></td>\n<td>a notebook</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une lampe</code></td>\n<td>a lamp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des cahiers</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des lampes</code></td>\n<td>some notebooks, some lamps</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Compare:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le cahier</code> — the notebook</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un cahier</code> — a notebook</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les cahiers</code> — the notebooks</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des cahiers</code> — some notebooks</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"forming-plurals\">Forming plurals</h2>\n<p>The common pattern is to add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une porte</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des portes</code> — a door → some doors</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un village</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des villages</code> — a village → some villages</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common special patterns:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-eau</code> take <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un tableau</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des tableaux</code> — a painting/board → some paintings/boards</li>\n<li>Many nouns ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-al</code> change to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-aux</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un animal</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des animaux</code> — an animal → some animals</li>\n<li>A noun already ending in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-x</code>, or <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-z</code> often does not change in writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Learn exceptions as complete singular/plural pairs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pronunciation-note\">Pronunciation note</h2>\n<p>The final plural <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code> is normally silent. The article often carries the clearest number signal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le livre</code> — the book</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">les livres</code> — the books</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h2>\n<p>Change each phrase to plural:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une photo</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bureau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le journal</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">la voix</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"production-task\">Production task</h2>\n<p>Write three singular objects you own. Convert each to plural and read both versions aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read, copy once, and then reproduce each sentence from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.</code> — I have a blue notebook in my bag.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.</code> — She buys a lamp for the bedroom.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons des photos de Paris.</code> — We have some photos of Paris.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.</code> — The desks are near the windows.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il observe des animaux au parc.</code> — He watches some animals at the park.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Change each singular object to plural. Then change each plural object to singular. Remember to change both the article and the noun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four sentences beginning with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai...</code>: two with singular objects and two with plural objects.</p>","search":"# lesson 2: indefinite articles and plurals ## indefinite articles use these forms for “a,” “an,” or plural “some”: | noun | article | french example | english | |---|---|---|---| | masculine singular | `un` | `un cahier` | a notebook | | feminine singular | `une` | `une lampe` | a lamp | | plural | `des` | `des cahiers`, `des lampes` | some notebooks, some lamps | compare: - `le cahier` — the notebook - `un cahier` — a notebook - `les cahiers` — the notebooks - `des cahiers` — some notebooks ## forming plurals the common pattern is to add `-s`: - `une porte` -> `des portes` — a door -> some doors - `un village` -> `des villages` — a village -> some villages common special patterns: - many nouns ending in `-eau` take `-x`: `un tableau` -> `des tableaux` — a painting/board -> some paintings/boards - many nouns ending in `-al` change to `-aux`: `un animal` -> `des animaux` — an animal -> some animals - a noun already ending in `-s`, `-x`, or `-z` often does not change in writing. learn exceptions as complete singular/plural pairs. ## pronunciation note the final plural `-s` is normally silent. the article often carries the clearest number signal: - `le livre` — the book - `les livres` — the books ## retrieval check change each phrase to plural: 1. `une photo` 2. `un bureau` 3. `le journal` 4. `la voix` ## production task write three singular objects you own. convert each to plural and read both versions aloud. ## sentence usage practice read, copy once, and then reproduce each sentence from memory. 1. `j'ai un cahier bleu dans mon sac.` — i have a blue notebook in my bag. 2. `elle achète une lampe pour la chambre.` — she buys a lamp for the bedroom. 3. `nous avons des photos de paris.` — we have some photos of paris. 4. `les bureaux sont près des fenêtres.` — the desks are near the windows. 5. `il observe des animaux au parc.` — he watches some animals at the park. ### change the sentence change each singular object to plural. then change each plural object to singular. remember to change both the article and the noun. ### make it personal write four sentences beginning with `j'ai...`: two with singular objects and two with plural objects. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/lessons/03-adjective-agreement.md","title":"Lesson 3: Adjective Agreement","type":"Resource","html":"<h1 id=\"lesson-3-adjective-agreement\">Lesson 3: Adjective Agreement</h1>\n<h2 id=\"core-idea\">Core idea</h2>\n<p>A descriptive adjective normally agrees with the noun it describes.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Noun form</th>\n<th>Typical adjective form</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td>base form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine plural</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-s</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td>add <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-es</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Example with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">petit</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un petit appartement</code> — a small apartment</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une petite maison</code> — a small house</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petits appartements</code> — some small apartments</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des petites maisons</code> — some small houses</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-spelling-patterns\">Common spelling patterns</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Masculine</th>\n<th>Feminine</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">actif</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">active</code></td>\n<td>active</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureuse</code></td>\n<td>happy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cher</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chère</code></td>\n<td>expensive/dear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">naturel</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">naturelle</code></td>\n<td>natural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bon</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bonne</code></td>\n<td>good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code></td>\n<td>Canadian</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Some adjectives already end in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">-e</code> and do not change in the feminine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un garçon calme</code> — a calm boy</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une fille calme</code> — a calm girl</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"mixed-groups\">Mixed groups</h2>\n<p>A plural adjective describing a group containing at least one masculine noun traditionally uses the masculine plural:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont contents.</code> — Paul and Marie are happy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read the sentences aloud and identify the noun controlled by each adjective.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul est content de son résultat.</code> — Paul is happy with his result.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est contente de son résultat.</code> — Marie is happy with her result.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marc sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — Paul and Marc are happy with their results.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie et Léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.</code> — Marie and Léa are happy with their results.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul et Marie sont contents de leurs résultats.</code> — Paul and Marie are happy with their results.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.</code> — The Canadian female students are very active.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.</code> — The athletic boy speaks with an athletic girl.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-the-pattern\">Notice the pattern</h3>\n<p>Underline <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">content</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contente</code>, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contents</code>, and <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">contentes</code>. Draw an arrow from each adjective to the noun or people it describes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite sentences 1-5 with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">fatigué</code> and then with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code>. Make every gender and number change.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four sentences about people you know:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>one masculine singular;</li>\n<li>one feminine singular;</li>\n<li>one feminine plural;</li>\n<li>one mixed group.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h2>\n<p>Write the feminine form:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">sportif</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">italien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">calme</code></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then write the feminine plural form of each.</p>","search":"# lesson 3: adjective agreement ## core idea a descriptive adjective normally agrees with the noun it describes. | noun form | typical adjective form | |---|---| | masculine singular | base form | | feminine singular | add `-e` | | masculine plural | add `-s` | | feminine plural | add `-es` | example with `petit`: - `un petit appartement` — a small apartment - `une petite maison` — a small house - `des petits appartements` — some small apartments - `des petites maisons` — some small houses ## common spelling patterns | masculine | feminine | english | |---|---|---| | `actif` | `active` | active | | `heureux` | `heureuse` | happy | | `cher` | `chère` | expensive/dear | | `naturel` | `naturelle` | natural | | `bon` | `bonne` | good | | `canadien` | `canadienne` | canadian | some adjectives already end in `-e` and do not change in the feminine: - `un garçon calme` — a calm boy - `une fille calme` — a calm girl ## mixed groups a plural adjective describing a group containing at least one masculine noun traditionally uses the masculine plural: - `paul et marie sont contents.` — paul and marie are happy. ## sentence usage practice read the sentences aloud and identify the noun controlled by each adjective. 1. `paul est content de son résultat.` — paul is happy with his result. 2. `marie est contente de son résultat.` — marie is happy with her result. 3. `paul et marc sont contents de leurs résultats.` — paul and marc are happy with their results. 4. `marie et léa sont contentes de leurs résultats.` — marie and léa are happy with their results. 5. `paul et marie sont contents de leurs résultats.` — paul and marie are happy with their results. 6. `les étudiantes canadiennes sont très actives.` — the canadian female students are very active. 7. `le garçon sportif parle avec une fille sportive.` — the athletic boy speaks with an athletic girl. ### notice the pattern underline `content`, `contente`, `contents`, and `contentes`. draw an arrow from each adjective to the noun or people it describes. ### change the sentence rewrite sentences 1-5 with `fatigué` and then with `heureux`. make every gender and number change. ### make it personal write four sentences about people you know: 1. one masculine singular; 2. one feminine singular; 3. one feminine plural; 4. one mixed group. ## retrieval check write the feminine form: 1. `sportif` 2. `heureux` 3. `italien` 4. `calme` then write the feminine plural form of each. "},{"id":"chapter-01-nouns-adjectives/lessons/04-position-colors-nationalities.md","title":"Lesson 4: Adjective Position, Colors, and Nationalities","type":"Resource","html":"<h1 id=\"lesson-4-adjective-position-colors-and-nationalities\">Lesson 4: Adjective Position, Colors, and Nationalities</h1>\n<h2 id=\"position\">Position</h2>\n<p>Most descriptive adjectives follow the noun:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une rue tranquille</code> — a quiet street</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un exercice difficile</code> — a difficult exercise</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une chemise blanche</code> — a white shirt</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Several frequent adjectives commonly precede the noun, especially short descriptions involving beauty, age, goodness, and size:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un beau quartier</code> — a beautiful neighborhood</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une jeune étudiante</code> — a young female student</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bon repas</code> — a good meal</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une grande ville</code> — a large city</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Learn position as part of the phrase. Instead of memorizing only <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">difficile</code>, memorize <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un exercice difficile</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forms-before-a-vowel-sound\">Forms before a vowel sound</h2>\n<p>Some masculine singular adjectives have a special form before a vowel sound:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">beau</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bel</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un bel appartement</code> — a beautiful apartment</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouveau</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nouvel</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un nouvel ami</code> — a new friend</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieux</code> → <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vieil</code>: <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un vieil hôtel</code> — an old hotel</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"colors\">Colors</h2>\n<p>Colors usually follow the noun and agree:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un sac noir</code> — a black bag</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une veste noire</code> — a black jacket</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des sacs noirs</code> — some black bags</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des vestes noires</code> — some black jackets</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Some color expressions derived from nouns may remain unchanged. For now, master the regular high-frequency colors first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nationalities\">Nationalities</h2>\n<p>Nationality adjectives <strong>agree</strong> with the person or noun they describe. This means the adjective changes its ending to match:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>gender: masculine or feminine;</li>\n<li>number: singular or plural.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadien</code> changes like this:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Person or group</th>\n<th>French</th>\n<th>English</th>\n</tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un étudiant canadien</code></td>\n<td>a Canadian male student</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine singular</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiante canadienne</code></td>\n<td>a Canadian female student</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>masculine or mixed plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants canadiens</code></td>\n<td>Canadian male/mixed students</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>feminine plural</td>\n<td><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code></td>\n<td>Canadian female students</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Nationality adjectives are normally lowercase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un acteur français</code> — a French actor</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une actrice française</code> — a French actress</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiants canadiens</code> — some Canadian male/mixed students</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des étudiantes canadiennes</code> — some Canadian female students</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The name of a language is also lowercase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> — I speak French.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a nationality is used as a noun for a person rather than as an adjective, it is capitalized:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est canadienne.</code> — She is Canadian. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">canadienne</code> is an adjective.)</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est une Canadienne.</code> — She is a Canadian woman. (<code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Canadienne</code> is a noun.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"retrieval-check\">Retrieval check</h2>\n<p>Correct each phrase:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une voiture noir</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">un appartement beau</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">une étudiant canadien</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des maisons blanc</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Read each sentence aloud. Mark adjectives before the noun with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">B</code> and adjectives after the noun with <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">A</code>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.</code> — We live in a large Canadian city.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle porte une petite veste noire.</code> — She is wearing a small black jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un bel appartement moderne.</code> — It is a beautiful modern apartment.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.</code> — A new French student arrives today.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.</code> — The old white houses are near the river.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.</code> — I speak French with my Canadian colleagues.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Make sentences 1, 2, and 3 plural.</li>\n<li>Change <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">noire</code> to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">rouge</code>, then to <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">bleue</code>.</li>\n<li>Replace <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">français</code> in sentence 4 with a nationality that describes you or someone you know.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Describe three objects and two people. Use at least one adjective before a noun, three adjectives after nouns, one color, and one nationality.</p>","search":"# lesson 4: adjective position, colors, and nationalities ## position most descriptive adjectives follow the noun: - `une rue tranquille` — a quiet street - `un exercice difficile` — a difficult exercise - `une chemise blanche` — a white shirt several frequent adjectives commonly precede the noun, especially short descriptions involving beauty, age, goodness, and size: - `un beau quartier` — a beautiful neighborhood - `une jeune étudiante` — a young female student - `un bon repas` — a good meal - `une grande ville` — a large city learn position as part of the phrase. instead of memorizing only `difficile`, memorize `un exercice difficile`. ## forms before a vowel sound some masculine singular adjectives have a special form before a vowel sound: - `beau` -> `bel`: `un bel appartement` — a beautiful apartment - `nouveau` -> `nouvel`: `un nouvel ami` — a new friend - `vieux` -> `vieil`: `un vieil hôtel` — an old hotel ## colors colors usually follow the noun and agree: - `un sac noir` — a black bag - `une veste noire` — a black jacket - `des sacs noirs` — some black bags - `des vestes noires` — some black jackets some color expressions derived from nouns may remain unchanged. for now, master the regular high-frequency colors first. ## nationalities nationality adjectives **agree** with the person or noun they describe. this means the adjective changes its ending to match: - gender: masculine or feminine; - number: singular or plural. for example, `canadien` changes like this: | person or group | french | english | |---|---|---| | masculine singular | `un étudiant canadien` | a canadian male student | | feminine singular | `une étudiante canadienne` | a canadian female student | | masculine or mixed plural | `des étudiants canadiens` | canadian male/mixed students | | feminine plural | `des étudiantes canadiennes` | canadian female students | nationality adjectives are normally lowercase: - `un acteur français` — a french actor - `une actrice française` — a french actress - `des étudiants canadiens` — some canadian male/mixed students - `des étudiantes canadiennes` — some canadian female students the name of a language is also lowercase: - `je parle français.` — i speak french. when a nationality is used as a noun for a person rather than as an adjective, it is capitalized: - `elle est canadienne.` — she is canadian. (`canadienne` is an adjective.) - `c'est une canadienne.` — she is a canadian woman. (`canadienne` is a noun.) ## retrieval check correct each phrase: 1. `une voiture noir` 2. `un appartement beau` 3. `une étudiant canadien` 4. `des maisons blanc` ## sentence usage practice read each sentence aloud. mark adjectives before the noun with `b` and adjectives after the noun with `a`. 1. `nous habitons dans une grande ville canadienne.` — we live in a large canadian city. 2. `elle porte une petite veste noire.` — she is wearing a small black jacket. 3. `c'est un bel appartement moderne.` — it is a beautiful modern apartment. 4. `un nouvel étudiant français arrive aujourd'hui.` — a new french student arrives today. 5. `les vieilles maisons blanches sont près de la rivière.` — the old white houses are near the river. 6. `je parle français avec mes collègues canadiens.` — i speak french with my canadian colleagues. ### change the sentence 1. make sentences 1, 2, and 3 plural. 2. change `noire` to `rouge`, then to `bleue`. 3. replace `français` in sentence 4 with a nationality that describes you or someone you know. ### make it personal describe three objects and two people. use at least one adjective before a noun, three adjectives after nouns, one color, and one nationality. "}]},{"number":2,"slug":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation","title":"Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation","documents":[{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/README.md","title":"Chapter 2: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-tre-avoir-subject-pronouns-and-negation\">Chapter 2: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate être and avoir in the present.</li>\n<li>Choose the correct subject pronoun.</li>\n<li>Form basic negative sentences with ne...pas.</li>\n<li>Use il y a and common expressions with être and avoir.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Subject pronouns</li>\n<li>Être</li>\n<li>Avoir and il y a</li>\n<li>Negation</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 2: être, avoir, subject pronouns, and negation this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate être and avoir in the present. - choose the correct subject pronoun. - form basic negative sentences with ne...pas. - use il y a and common expressions with être and avoir. ## lesson sequence 1. subject pronouns 2. être 3. avoir and il y a 4. negation ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 2 Lessons: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-lessons-tre-avoir-subject-pronouns-and-negation\">Chapter 2 Lessons: Être, Avoir, Subject Pronouns, and Negation</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-subject-pronouns\">Unit 1: Subject pronouns</h2>\n<p>French verbs change with je, tu, il/elle/on, nous, vous, and ils/elles. Use vous for a plural group or polite singular address.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis ici.</code> — I am here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes prêts.</code> — You are ready.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles ont un cours.</code> — They have a class.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-tre\">Unit 2: Être</h2>\n<p>Present forms: suis, es, est, sommes, êtes, sont. Use être for identity, description, nationality, location in many basic statements, and selected expressions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes calmes.</code> — We are calm.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est canadienne.</code> — She is Canadian.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils sont à Montréal.</code> — They are in Montreal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-avoir-and-il-y-a\">Unit 3: Avoir and il y a</h2>\n<p>Present forms: ai, as, a, avons, avez, ont. French uses avoir in many expressions where English uses “to be.” Il y a means there is or there are.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code> — I am twenty years old.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu as faim.</code> — You are hungry.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une table.</code> — There is a table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-negation\">Unit 4: Negation</h2>\n<p>Place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. Before a vowel sound, ne becomes n'. With indefinite or partitive objects after a negative avoir, the article often becomes de.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas fatigué.</code> — I am not tired.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle n'a pas de voiture.</code> — She does not have a car.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de café.</code> — There is no coffee.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write six sentences introducing yourself and your home. Use être twice, avoir twice, il y a once, and one negative.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis étudiant, mais ma sœur est ingénieure.</code> — I am a student, but my sister is an engineer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons deux chambres et un petit jardin.</code> — We have two bedrooms and a small garden.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il y a une pharmacie près de la maison.</code> — There is a pharmacy near the house.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mes voisins ne sont pas bruyants.</code> — My neighbors are not noisy.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 2 lessons: être, avoir, subject pronouns, and negation use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: subject pronouns french verbs change with je, tu, il/elle/on, nous, vous, and ils/elles. use vous for a plural group or polite singular address. ### examples - `je suis ici.` — i am here. - `vous êtes prêts.` — you are ready. - `elles ont un cours.` — they have a class. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: être present forms: suis, es, est, sommes, êtes, sont. use être for identity, description, nationality, location in many basic statements, and selected expressions. ### examples - `nous sommes calmes.` — we are calm. - `elle est canadienne.` — she is canadian. - `ils sont à montréal.` — they are in montreal. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: avoir and il y a present forms: ai, as, a, avons, avez, ont. french uses avoir in many expressions where english uses “to be.” il y a means there is or there are. ### examples - `j'ai vingt ans.` — i am twenty years old. - `tu as faim.` — you are hungry. - `il y a une table.` — there is a table. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: negation place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. before a vowel sound, ne becomes n'. with indefinite or partitive objects after a negative avoir, the article often becomes de. ### examples - `je ne suis pas fatigué.` — i am not tired. - `elle n'a pas de voiture.` — she does not have a car. - `il n'y a pas de café.` — there is no coffee. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write six sentences introducing yourself and your home. use être twice, avoir twice, il y a once, and one negative. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je suis étudiant, mais ma sœur est ingénieure.` — i am a student, but my sister is an engineer. 2. `nous avons deux chambres et un petit jardin.` — we have two bedrooms and a small garden. 3. `il y a une pharmacie près de la maison.` — there is a pharmacy near the house. 4. `mes voisins ne sont pas bruyants.` — my neighbors are not noisy. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 2 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-exercises\">Chapter 2 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Je suis content.</li>\n<li>Nous avons une maison.</li>\n<li>Il y a des fenêtres.</li>\n<li>Marie et Léa sont françaises.</li>\n<li>Paul a faim.</li>\n<li>Tu es en retard.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>subject pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis ici.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>être</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes calmes.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>avoir and il y a</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai vingt ans.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>negation</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas fatigué.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write six sentences introducing yourself and your home. Use être twice, avoir twice, il y a once, and one negative.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 2 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. je suis content. 2. nous avons une maison. 3. il y a des fenêtres. 4. marie et léa sont françaises. 5. paul a faim. 6. tu es en retard. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **subject pronouns**. use `je suis ici.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **être**. use `nous sommes calmes.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **avoir and il y a**. use `j'ai vingt ans.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **negation**. use `je ne suis pas fatigué.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write six sentences introducing yourself and your home. use être twice, avoir twice, il y a once, and one negative. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 2 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-answer-key\">Chapter 2 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne suis pas content.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons pas de maison.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il n'y a pas de fenêtres.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles sont françaises.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a faim.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous êtes en retard.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subject pronouns:</strong> French verbs change with je, tu, il/elle/on, nous, vous, and ils/elles. Use vous for a plural group or polite singular address.</li>\n<li><strong>Être:</strong> Present forms: suis, es, est, sommes, êtes, sont. Use être for identity, description, nationality, location in many basic statements, and selected expressions.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoir and il y a:</strong> Present forms: ai, as, a, avons, avez, ont. French uses avoir in many expressions where English uses “to be.” Il y a means there is or there are.</li>\n<li><strong>Negation:</strong> Place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. Before a vowel sound, ne becomes n'. With indefinite or partitive objects after a negative avoir, the article often becomes de.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 2 answer key ## section a 1. `je ne suis pas content.` 2. `nous n'avons pas de maison.` 3. `il n'y a pas de fenêtres.` 4. `elles sont françaises.` 5. `il a faim.` 6. `vous êtes en retard.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **subject pronouns:** french verbs change with je, tu, il/elle/on, nous, vous, and ils/elles. use vous for a plural group or polite singular address. - **être:** present forms: suis, es, est, sommes, êtes, sont. use être for identity, description, nationality, location in many basic statements, and selected expressions. - **avoir and il y a:** present forms: ai, as, a, avons, avez, ont. french uses avoir in many expressions where english uses “to be.” il y a means there is or there are. - **negation:** place ne before the conjugated verb and pas after it. before a vowel sound, ne becomes n'. with indefinite or partitive objects after a negative avoir, the article often becomes de. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-02-etre-avoir-pronouns-negation/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 2 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-2-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 2 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Subject pronouns</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Être</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Avoir and il y a</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Negation</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 2 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **subject pronouns**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **être** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **avoir and il y a**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **negation**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":3,"slug":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions","title":"Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions","documents":[{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/README.md","title":"Chapter 3: Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-days-months-regular-er-verbs-and-questions\">Chapter 3: Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use days, months, seasons, and parts of the day.</li>\n<li>Conjugate regular -er verbs in the present.</li>\n<li>Ask yes/no and information questions.</li>\n<li>Write a simple weekly routine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Calendar language</li>\n<li>Regular -er verbs</li>\n<li>Yes/no questions</li>\n<li>Information questions</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 3: days, months, regular -er verbs, and questions this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use days, months, seasons, and parts of the day. - conjugate regular -er verbs in the present. - ask yes/no and information questions. - write a simple weekly routine. ## lesson sequence 1. calendar language 2. regular -er verbs 3. yes/no questions 4. information questions ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 3 Lessons: Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-lessons-days-months-regular-er-verbs-and-questions\">Chapter 3 Lessons: Days, Months, Regular -er Verbs, and Questions</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-calendar-language\">Unit 1: Calendar language</h2>\n<p>Days and months are normally lowercase. Use en with months and seasons in common date expressions, and le with a recurring day.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code> — It is Monday.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voyage en juillet.</code> — I travel in July.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le samedi, je travaille.</code> — On Saturdays, I work.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-regular-er-verbs\">Unit 2: Regular -er verbs</h2>\n<p>Remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. The written endings differ more than their pronunciation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> — I speak French.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous regardons un film.</code> — We are watching a movie.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils habitent ici.</code> — They live here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-yes-no-questions\">Unit 3: Yes/no questions</h2>\n<p>Use rising intonation, est-ce que, or inversion. Est-ce que is reliable in everyday speech.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain ?</code> — Are you working tomorrow?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous aimez Paris ?</code> — Do you like Paris?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parlez-vous anglais ?</code> — Do you speak English?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-information-questions\">Unit 4: Information questions</h2>\n<p>Combine question words such as où, quand, comment, pourquoi, combien, and qui with a question structure.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code> — Where do you live?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code> — When is she arriving?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi étudient-ils ?</code> — Why are they studying?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write a seven-line weekly routine. Include four -er verbs and two questions you could ask a classmate.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le lundi, je travaille à la maison.</code> — On Mondays, I work at home.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous étudions le français en septembre.</code> — We study French in September.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français ?</code> — Do you often watch French movies?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt ?</code> — Why does she arrive so early?</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 3 lessons: days, months, regular -er verbs, and questions use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: calendar language days and months are normally lowercase. use en with months and seasons in common date expressions, and le with a recurring day. ### examples - `nous sommes lundi.` — it is monday. - `je voyage en juillet.` — i travel in july. - `le samedi, je travaille.` — on saturdays, i work. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: regular -er verbs remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. the written endings differ more than their pronunciation. ### examples - `je parle français.` — i speak french. - `nous regardons un film.` — we are watching a movie. - `ils habitent ici.` — they live here. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: yes/no questions use rising intonation, est-ce que, or inversion. est-ce que is reliable in everyday speech. ### examples - `tu travailles demain ?` — are you working tomorrow? - `est-ce que vous aimez paris ?` — do you like paris? - `parlez-vous anglais ?` — do you speak english? ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: information questions combine question words such as où, quand, comment, pourquoi, combien, and qui with a question structure. ### examples - `où habitez-vous ?` — where do you live? - `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` — when is she arriving? - `pourquoi étudient-ils ?` — why are they studying? ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write a seven-line weekly routine. include four -er verbs and two questions you could ask a classmate. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `le lundi, je travaille à la maison.` — on mondays, i work at home. 2. `nous étudions le français en septembre.` — we study french in september. 3. `est-ce que vous regardez souvent des films français ?` — do you often watch french movies? 4. `pourquoi est-ce qu'elle arrive si tôt ?` — why does she arrive so early? ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 3 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-exercises\">Chapter 3 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>nous / parler</li>\n<li>ils / étudier</li>\n<li>Vous habitez à Lyon.</li>\n<li>Elle arrive mardi.</li>\n<li>Je travaille le matin.</li>\n<li>tu / commencer</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>calendar language</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes lundi.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>regular -er verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parle français.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>yes/no questions</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu travailles demain ?</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>information questions</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Où habitez-vous ?</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a seven-line weekly routine. Include four -er verbs and two questions you could ask a classmate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 3 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. nous / parler 2. ils / étudier 3. vous habitez à lyon. 4. elle arrive mardi. 5. je travaille le matin. 6. tu / commencer ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **calendar language**. use `nous sommes lundi.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **regular -er verbs**. use `je parle français.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **yes/no questions**. use `tu travailles demain ?` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **information questions**. use `où habitez-vous ?` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write a seven-line weekly routine. include four -er verbs and two questions you could ask a classmate. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 3 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-answer-key\">Chapter 3 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous parlons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils étudient</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Est-ce que vous habitez à Lyon ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Quand travaillez-vous ?</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tu commences</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Calendar language:</strong> Days and months are normally lowercase. Use en with months and seasons in common date expressions, and le with a recurring day.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular -er verbs:</strong> Remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. The written endings differ more than their pronunciation.</li>\n<li><strong>Yes/no questions:</strong> Use rising intonation, est-ce que, or inversion. Est-ce que is reliable in everyday speech.</li>\n<li><strong>Information questions:</strong> Combine question words such as où, quand, comment, pourquoi, combien, and qui with a question structure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 3 answer key ## section a 1. `nous parlons` 2. `ils étudient` 3. `est-ce que vous habitez à lyon ?` 4. `quand est-ce qu'elle arrive ?` 5. `quand travaillez-vous ?` 6. `tu commences` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **calendar language:** days and months are normally lowercase. use en with months and seasons in common date expressions, and le with a recurring day. - **regular -er verbs:** remove -er and add -e, -es, -e, -ons, -ez, -ent. the written endings differ more than their pronunciation. - **yes/no questions:** use rising intonation, est-ce que, or inversion. est-ce que is reliable in everyday speech. - **information questions:** combine question words such as où, quand, comment, pourquoi, combien, and qui with a question structure. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-03-dates-er-verbs-questions/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 3 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-3-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 3 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Calendar language</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Regular -er verbs</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Yes/no questions</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Information questions</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 3 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **calendar language**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **regular -er verbs** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **yes/no questions**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **information questions**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":4,"slug":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs","title":"Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs","documents":[{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/README.md","title":"Chapter 4: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-numbers-dates-time-and-regular-ir-verbs\">Chapter 4: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Say cardinal and ordinal numbers.</li>\n<li>Write dates and tell time.</li>\n<li>Conjugate regular -ir verbs.</li>\n<li>Use shopping and meal vocabulary in short exchanges.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Numbers and dates</li>\n<li>Time</li>\n<li>Regular -ir verbs</li>\n<li>Functional practice</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 4: numbers, dates, time, and regular -ir verbs this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - say cardinal and ordinal numbers. - write dates and tell time. - conjugate regular -ir verbs. - use shopping and meal vocabulary in short exchanges. ## lesson sequence 1. numbers and dates 2. time 3. regular -ir verbs 4. functional practice ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 4 Lessons: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-lessons-numbers-dates-time-and-regular-ir-verbs\">Chapter 4 Lessons: Numbers, Dates, Time, and Regular -ir Verbs</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-numbers-and-dates\">Unit 1: Numbers and dates</h2>\n<p>Practice numbers in meaningful chunks: prices, addresses, years, and phone numbers. Dates use cardinal numbers except premier for the first.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code> — May first</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le quinze septembre</code> — September fifteenth</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">deux mille vingt-six</code> — two thousand twenty-six</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-time\">Unit 2: Time</h2>\n<p>Use Il est... for clock time. Add du matin, de l'après-midi, or du soir when useful. Midi and minuit are special forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est huit heures.</code> — It is eight o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est midi et quart.</code> — It is a quarter past noon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est dix-neuf heures trente.</code> — It is 7:30 p.m.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-regular-ir-verbs\">Unit 3: Regular -ir verbs</h2>\n<p>For verbs like finir, remove -ir and add -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je finis tôt.</code> — I finish early.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons le menu.</code> — We choose the menu.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles réussissent.</code> — They succeed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-functional-practice\">Unit 4: Functional practice</h2>\n<p>Combine quantities, time, and -ir verbs in realistic situations such as stores, meals, and appointments.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le magasin ouvre à neuf heures.</code> — The store opens at nine o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts.</code> — We choose two desserts.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous finissez à quelle heure ?</code> — What time do you finish?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write a one-day schedule with five times, three prices, and four regular -ir verb forms.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.</code> — The class starts at 9:15.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.</code> — My birthday is November first.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.</code> — We choose two desserts for eight euros.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les magasins finissent leur journée à dix-neuf heures.</code> — The stores finish their day at 7 p.m.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 4 lessons: numbers, dates, time, and regular -ir verbs use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: numbers and dates practice numbers in meaningful chunks: prices, addresses, years, and phone numbers. dates use cardinal numbers except premier for the first. ### examples - `le premier mai` — may first - `le quinze septembre` — september fifteenth - `deux mille vingt-six` — two thousand twenty-six ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: time use il est... for clock time. add du matin, de l'après-midi, or du soir when useful. midi and minuit are special forms. ### examples - `il est huit heures.` — it is eight o'clock. - `il est midi et quart.` — it is a quarter past noon. - `il est dix-neuf heures trente.` — it is 7:30 p.m. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: regular -ir verbs for verbs like finir, remove -ir and add -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent. ### examples - `je finis tôt.` — i finish early. - `nous choisissons le menu.` — we choose the menu. - `elles réussissent.` — they succeed. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: functional practice combine quantities, time, and -ir verbs in realistic situations such as stores, meals, and appointments. ### examples - `le magasin ouvre à neuf heures.` — the store opens at nine o'clock. - `nous choisissons deux desserts.` — we choose two desserts. - `vous finissez à quelle heure ?` — what time do you finish? ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write a one-day schedule with five times, three prices, and four regular -ir verb forms. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `le cours commence à neuf heures quinze.` — the class starts at 9:15. 2. `mon anniversaire est le premier novembre.` — my birthday is november first. 3. `nous choisissons deux desserts pour huit euros.` — we choose two desserts for eight euros. 4. `les magasins finissent leur journée à dix-neuf heures.` — the stores finish their day at 7 p.m. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 4 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-exercises\">Chapter 4 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>42</li>\n<li>1 May</li>\n<li>14:30</li>\n<li>nous / finir</li>\n<li>elles / choisir</li>\n<li>vous / réussir</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>numbers and dates</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>time</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est huit heures.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>regular -ir verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je finis tôt.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>functional practice</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le magasin ouvre à neuf heures.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a one-day schedule with five times, three prices, and four regular -ir verb forms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 4 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. 42 2. 1 may 3. 14:30 4. nous / finir 5. elles / choisir 6. vous / réussir ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **numbers and dates**. use `le premier mai` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **time**. use `il est huit heures.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **regular -ir verbs**. use `je finis tôt.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **functional practice**. use `le magasin ouvre à neuf heures.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write a one-day schedule with five times, three prices, and four regular -ir verb forms. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 4 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-answer-key\">Chapter 4 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">quarante-deux</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">le premier mai</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est quatorze heures trente.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elles choisissent</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous réussissez</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Numbers and dates:</strong> Practice numbers in meaningful chunks: prices, addresses, years, and phone numbers. Dates use cardinal numbers except premier for the first.</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> Use Il est... for clock time. Add du matin, de l'après-midi, or du soir when useful. Midi and minuit are special forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular -ir verbs:</strong> For verbs like finir, remove -ir and add -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent.</li>\n<li><strong>Functional practice:</strong> Combine quantities, time, and -ir verbs in realistic situations such as stores, meals, and appointments.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 4 answer key ## section a 1. `quarante-deux` 2. `le premier mai` 3. `il est quatorze heures trente.` 4. `nous finissons` 5. `elles choisissent` 6. `vous réussissez` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **numbers and dates:** practice numbers in meaningful chunks: prices, addresses, years, and phone numbers. dates use cardinal numbers except premier for the first. - **time:** use il est... for clock time. add du matin, de l'après-midi, or du soir when useful. midi and minuit are special forms. - **regular -ir verbs:** for verbs like finir, remove -ir and add -is, -is, -it, -issons, -issez, -issent. - **functional practice:** combine quantities, time, and -ir verbs in realistic situations such as stores, meals, and appointments. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-04-numbers-time-ir-verbs/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 4 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-4-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 4 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Numbers and dates</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Time</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Regular -ir verbs</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Functional practice</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 4 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **numbers and dates**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **time** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **regular -ir verbs**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **functional practice**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":5,"slug":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes","title":"Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes","documents":[{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/README.md","title":"Chapter 5: Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-regular-re-verbs-and-er-spelling-changes\">Chapter 5: Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate regular -re verbs.</li>\n<li>Use depuis for an action continuing into the present.</li>\n<li>Handle common -er spelling-change patterns.</li>\n<li>Describe clothing and shopping choices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Regular -re verbs</li>\n<li>Depuis</li>\n<li>Spelling changes</li>\n<li>Shopping language</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 5: regular -re verbs and -er spelling changes this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate regular -re verbs. - use depuis for an action continuing into the present. - handle common -er spelling-change patterns. - describe clothing and shopping choices. ## lesson sequence 1. regular -re verbs 2. depuis 3. spelling changes 4. shopping language ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 5 Lessons: Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-lessons-regular-re-verbs-and-er-spelling-changes\">Chapter 5 Lessons: Regular -re Verbs and -er Spelling Changes</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-regular-re-verbs\">Unit 1: Regular -re verbs</h2>\n<p>Remove -re and add -s, -s, nothing, -ons, -ez, -ent. The final consonant is often heard in plural forms but not singular forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends le bus.</code> — I am waiting for the bus.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous vendons des livres.</code> — We sell books.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils répondent vite.</code> — They answer quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-depuis\">Unit 2: Depuis</h2>\n<p>Use depuis with the present tense for something that began in the past and is still continuing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis deux ans.</code> — I have lived here for two years.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle attend depuis midi.</code> — She has been waiting since noon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Depuis quand travaillez-vous ici ?</code> — How long have you worked here?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-spelling-changes\">Unit 3: Spelling changes</h2>\n<p>Some -er verbs change spelling to preserve sound or stress: commençons, mangeons, j'achète, je préfère, j'appelle, j'essaie.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons.</code> — We are beginning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons.</code> — We are eating.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une veste.</code> — She buys a jacket.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-shopping-language\">Unit 4: Shopping language</h2>\n<p>Apply verb forms to clothing, toiletries, preferences, and time expressions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère la chemise bleue.</code> — I prefer the blue shirt.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils essaient les chaussures.</code> — They try on the shoes.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La vendeuse répond à ma question.</code> — The saleswoman answers my question.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write a short store dialogue using one -re verb, three spelling-change verbs, a color, and depuis.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends mon ami devant le magasin.</code> — I am waiting for my friend in front of the store.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.</code> — We have lived here for three years.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.</code> — She buys a shirt and tries on a jacket.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.</code> — We eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 5 lessons: regular -re verbs and -er spelling changes use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: regular -re verbs remove -re and add -s, -s, nothing, -ons, -ez, -ent. the final consonant is often heard in plural forms but not singular forms. ### examples - `j'attends le bus.` — i am waiting for the bus. - `nous vendons des livres.` — we sell books. - `ils répondent vite.` — they answer quickly. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: depuis use depuis with the present tense for something that began in the past and is still continuing. ### examples - `j'habite ici depuis deux ans.` — i have lived here for two years. - `elle attend depuis midi.` — she has been waiting since noon. - `depuis quand travaillez-vous ici ?` — how long have you worked here? ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: spelling changes some -er verbs change spelling to preserve sound or stress: commençons, mangeons, j'achète, je préfère, j'appelle, j'essaie. ### examples - `nous commençons.` — we are beginning. - `nous mangeons.` — we are eating. - `elle achète une veste.` — she buys a jacket. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: shopping language apply verb forms to clothing, toiletries, preferences, and time expressions. ### examples - `je préfère la chemise bleue.` — i prefer the blue shirt. - `ils essaient les chaussures.` — they try on the shoes. - `la vendeuse répond à ma question.` — the saleswoman answers my question. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write a short store dialogue using one -re verb, three spelling-change verbs, a color, and depuis. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `j'attends mon ami devant le magasin.` — i am waiting for my friend in front of the store. 2. `nous habitons ici depuis trois ans.` — we have lived here for three years. 3. `elle achète une chemise et essaie une veste.` — she buys a shirt and tries on a jacket. 4. `nous mangeons au restaurant avant de commencer les achats.` — we eat at the restaurant before starting the shopping. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 5 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-exercises\">Chapter 5 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>nous / attendre</li>\n<li>il / répondre</li>\n<li>nous / commencer</li>\n<li>nous / manger</li>\n<li>elle / acheter</li>\n<li>J'habite ici. I started three years ago.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>regular -re verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'attends le bus.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>depuis</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis deux ans.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>spelling changes</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous commençons.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>shopping language</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je préfère la chemise bleue.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a short store dialogue using one -re verb, three spelling-change verbs, a color, and depuis.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 5 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. nous / attendre 2. il / répondre 3. nous / commencer 4. nous / manger 5. elle / acheter 6. j'habite ici. i started three years ago. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **regular -re verbs**. use `j'attends le bus.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **depuis**. use `j'habite ici depuis deux ans.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **spelling changes**. use `nous commençons.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **shopping language**. use `je préfère la chemise bleue.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write a short store dialogue using one -re verb, three spelling-change verbs, a color, and depuis. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 5 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-answer-key\">Chapter 5 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous attendons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">il répond</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous commençons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous mangeons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">elle achète</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'habite ici depuis trois ans.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regular -re verbs:</strong> Remove -re and add -s, -s, nothing, -ons, -ez, -ent. The final consonant is often heard in plural forms but not singular forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Depuis:</strong> Use depuis with the present tense for something that began in the past and is still continuing.</li>\n<li><strong>Spelling changes:</strong> Some -er verbs change spelling to preserve sound or stress: commençons, mangeons, j'achète, je préfère, j'appelle, j'essaie.</li>\n<li><strong>Shopping language:</strong> Apply verb forms to clothing, toiletries, preferences, and time expressions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 5 answer key ## section a 1. `nous attendons` 2. `il répond` 3. `nous commençons` 4. `nous mangeons` 5. `elle achète` 6. `j'habite ici depuis trois ans.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **regular -re verbs:** remove -re and add -s, -s, nothing, -ons, -ez, -ent. the final consonant is often heard in plural forms but not singular forms. - **depuis:** use depuis with the present tense for something that began in the past and is still continuing. - **spelling changes:** some -er verbs change spelling to preserve sound or stress: commençons, mangeons, j'achète, je préfère, j'appelle, j'essaie. - **shopping language:** apply verb forms to clothing, toiletries, preferences, and time expressions. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-05-re-verbs-spelling-changes/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 5 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-5-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 5 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Regular -re verbs</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Depuis</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Spelling changes</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Shopping language</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 5 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **regular -re verbs**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **depuis** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **spelling changes**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **shopping language**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":6,"slug":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire","title":"Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire","documents":[{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/README.md","title":"Chapter 6: Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-aller-the-near-future-prepositions-and-faire\">Chapter 6: Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate aller and faire.</li>\n<li>Form the near future.</li>\n<li>Use à, de, location, time, and geographical prepositions.</li>\n<li>Describe weather and travel plans.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Aller and the near future</li>\n<li>À and de</li>\n<li>Geographical names</li>\n<li>Faire</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 6: aller, the near future, prepositions, and faire this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate aller and faire. - form the near future. - use à, de, location, time, and geographical prepositions. - describe weather and travel plans. ## lesson sequence 1. aller and the near future 2. à and de 3. geographical names 4. faire ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 6 Lessons: Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-lessons-aller-the-near-future-prepositions-and-faire\">Chapter 6 Lessons: Aller, the Near Future, Prepositions, and Faire</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-aller-and-the-near-future\">Unit 1: Aller and the near future</h2>\n<p>Aller is irregular: vais, vas, va, allons, allez, vont. Follow it with an infinitive to say what is going to happen.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais partir.</code> — I am going to leave.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons visiter Québec.</code> — We are going to visit Quebec City.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vont étudier.</code> — They are going to study.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-and-de\">Unit 2: À and de</h2>\n<p>À often marks destination or location; de often marks origin, possession, or source. Both contract with le and les.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code> — to the market</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux États-Unis</code> — to/in the United States</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Canada</code> — from Canada</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">des magasins</code> — from the stores</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-geographical-names\">Unit 3: Geographical names</h2>\n<p>Use en with many feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au with many masculine countries, and aux with plural country names.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en France</code> — in/to France</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Japon</code> — in/to Japan</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux Pays-Bas</code> — in/to the Netherlands</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du Mexique</code> — from Mexico</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-faire\">Unit 4: Faire</h2>\n<p>Faire forms: fais, fais, fait, faisons, faites, font. It appears in weather, activities, and many fixed expressions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid.</code> — It is cold.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je fais du vélo.</code> — I ride a bicycle.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous faisons attention.</code> — We are being careful.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Plan a four-day trip. State destinations, origins, weather, and at least four near-future activities.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous allons visiter un village près de Québec.</code> — Tomorrow, we are going to visit a village near Quebec City.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle vient de France, mais elle habite au Canada.</code> — She comes from France, but she lives in Canada.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.</code> — It is cold, so I am going to put on my coat.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">À quelle heure allez-vous partir pour l'aéroport ?</code> — What time are you going to leave for the airport?</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 6 lessons: aller, the near future, prepositions, and faire use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: aller and the near future aller is irregular: vais, vas, va, allons, allez, vont. follow it with an infinitive to say what is going to happen. ### examples - `je vais partir.` — i am going to leave. - `nous allons visiter québec.` — we are going to visit quebec city. - `ils vont étudier.` — they are going to study. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: à and de à often marks destination or location; de often marks origin, possession, or source. both contract with le and les. ### examples - `au marché` — to the market - `aux états-unis` — to/in the united states - `du canada` — from canada - `des magasins` — from the stores ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: geographical names use en with many feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au with many masculine countries, and aux with plural country names. ### examples - `en france` — in/to france - `au japon` — in/to japan - `aux pays-bas` — in/to the netherlands - `du mexique` — from mexico ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: faire faire forms: fais, fais, fait, faisons, faites, font. it appears in weather, activities, and many fixed expressions. ### examples - `il fait froid.` — it is cold. - `je fais du vélo.` — i ride a bicycle. - `nous faisons attention.` — we are being careful. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task plan a four-day trip. state destinations, origins, weather, and at least four near-future activities. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `demain, nous allons visiter un village près de québec.` — tomorrow, we are going to visit a village near quebec city. 2. `elle vient de france, mais elle habite au canada.` — she comes from france, but she lives in canada. 3. `il fait froid, alors je vais mettre mon manteau.` — it is cold, so i am going to put on my coat. 4. `à quelle heure allez-vous partir pour l'aéroport ?` — what time are you going to leave for the airport? ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 6 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-exercises\">Chapter 6 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I am going to travel.</li>\n<li>to Canada</li>\n<li>from France</li>\n<li>to the United States</li>\n<li>nous / faire</li>\n<li>The weather is nice.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>aller and the near future</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais partir.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>à and de</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au marché</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>geographical names</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en France</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>faire</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait froid.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Plan a four-day trip. State destinations, origins, weather, and at least four near-future activities.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 6 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i am going to travel. 2. to canada 3. from france 4. to the united states 5. nous / faire 6. the weather is nice. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **aller and the near future**. use `je vais partir.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **à and de**. use `au marché` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **geographical names**. use `en france` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **faire**. use `il fait froid.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing plan a four-day trip. state destinations, origins, weather, and at least four near-future activities. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 6 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-answer-key\">Chapter 6 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vais voyager.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">au Canada</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de France</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">aux États-Unis</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous faisons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il fait beau.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aller and the near future:</strong> Aller is irregular: vais, vas, va, allons, allez, vont. Follow it with an infinitive to say what is going to happen.</li>\n<li><strong>À and de:</strong> À often marks destination or location; de often marks origin, possession, or source. Both contract with le and les.</li>\n<li><strong>Geographical names:</strong> Use en with many feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au with many masculine countries, and aux with plural country names.</li>\n<li><strong>Faire:</strong> Faire forms: fais, fais, fait, faisons, faites, font. It appears in weather, activities, and many fixed expressions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 6 answer key ## section a 1. `je vais voyager.` 2. `au canada` 3. `de france` 4. `aux états-unis` 5. `nous faisons` 6. `il fait beau.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **aller and the near future:** aller is irregular: vais, vas, va, allons, allez, vont. follow it with an infinitive to say what is going to happen. - **à and de:** à often marks destination or location; de often marks origin, possession, or source. both contract with le and les. - **geographical names:** use en with many feminine countries and masculine countries beginning with a vowel, au with many masculine countries, and aux with plural country names. - **faire:** faire forms: fais, fais, fait, faisons, faites, font. it appears in weather, activities, and many fixed expressions. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-06-aller-prepositions-faire/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 6 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-6-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 6 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Aller and the near future</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>À and de</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Geographical names</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Faire</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 6 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **aller and the near future**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **à and de** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **geographical names**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **faire**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":7,"slug":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives","title":"Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions","documents":[{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/README.md","title":"Chapter 7: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-irregular-verbs-i-and-verb-infinitive-constructions\">Chapter 7: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use high-frequency irregular verbs.</li>\n<li>Distinguish partir, sortir, and quitter.</li>\n<li>Combine conjugated verbs with infinitives.</li>\n<li>Choose no preposition, à, or de before an infinitive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Movement and arrival</li>\n<li>Communication and action</li>\n<li>Ability, desire, obligation</li>\n<li>Verb + verb patterns</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 7: irregular verbs i and verb + infinitive constructions this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use high-frequency irregular verbs. - distinguish partir, sortir, and quitter. - combine conjugated verbs with infinitives. - choose no preposition, à, or de before an infinitive. ## lesson sequence 1. movement and arrival 2. communication and action 3. ability, desire, obligation 4. verb + verb patterns ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 7 Lessons: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-lessons-irregular-verbs-i-and-verb-infinitive-constructions\">Chapter 7 Lessons: Irregular Verbs I and Verb + Infinitive Constructions</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-movement-and-arrival\">Unit 1: Movement and arrival</h2>\n<p>Partir means to leave/depart, sortir means to go out or take out, quitter takes a direct object, and venir means to come.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars demain.</code> — I am leaving tomorrow.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sort ce soir.</code> — She is going out tonight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous quittons la ville.</code> — We are leaving the city.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-communication-and-action\">Unit 2: Communication and action</h2>\n<p>Practice dire, lire, écrire, mettre, recevoir, and related forms as complete phrases.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous dites la vérité.</code> — You are telling the truth.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'écris un message.</code> — I am writing a message.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils mettent leurs manteaux.</code> — They are putting on their coats.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-ability-desire-obligation\">Unit 3: Ability, desire, obligation</h2>\n<p>Pouvoir, vouloir, and devoir are followed directly by an infinitive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux venir.</code> — I can come.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons apprendre.</code> — We want to learn.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu dois travailler.</code> — You must work.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-verb-verb-patterns\">Unit 4: Verb + verb patterns</h2>\n<p>Some verbs take no preposition, some take à, and others take de before an infinitive. Memorize the whole frame.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle aime lire.</code> — She likes to read.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il commence à écrire.</code> — He is beginning to write.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous essayons de comprendre.</code> — We are trying to understand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write eight decisions for the coming month using pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, commencer à, and essayer de.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.</code> — I want to read more, but I must finish my work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous pouvons sortir après avoir écrit le message.</code> — We can go out after writing the message.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à apprendre le français et essaie de parler chaque jour.</code> — She is beginning to learn French and tries to speak every day.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils quittent le bureau et partent à six heures.</code> — They leave the office and depart at six o'clock.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 7 lessons: irregular verbs i and verb + infinitive constructions use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: movement and arrival partir means to leave/depart, sortir means to go out or take out, quitter takes a direct object, and venir means to come. ### examples - `je pars demain.` — i am leaving tomorrow. - `elle sort ce soir.` — she is going out tonight. - `nous quittons la ville.` — we are leaving the city. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: communication and action practice dire, lire, écrire, mettre, recevoir, and related forms as complete phrases. ### examples - `vous dites la vérité.` — you are telling the truth. - `j'écris un message.` — i am writing a message. - `ils mettent leurs manteaux.` — they are putting on their coats. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: ability, desire, obligation pouvoir, vouloir, and devoir are followed directly by an infinitive. ### examples - `je peux venir.` — i can come. - `nous voulons apprendre.` — we want to learn. - `tu dois travailler.` — you must work. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: verb + verb patterns some verbs take no preposition, some take à, and others take de before an infinitive. memorize the whole frame. ### examples - `elle aime lire.` — she likes to read. - `il commence à écrire.` — he is beginning to write. - `nous essayons de comprendre.` — we are trying to understand. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write eight decisions for the coming month using pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, commencer à, and essayer de. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je veux lire davantage, mais je dois finir mon travail.` — i want to read more, but i must finish my work. 2. `nous pouvons sortir après avoir écrit le message.` — we can go out after writing the message. 3. `elle commence à apprendre le français et essaie de parler chaque jour.` — she is beginning to learn french and tries to speak every day. 4. `ils quittent le bureau et partent à six heures.` — they leave the office and depart at six o'clock. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 7 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-exercises\">Chapter 7 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I can read.</li>\n<li>We want to leave.</li>\n<li>She begins to write.</li>\n<li>They try to understand.</li>\n<li>I leave the house.</li>\n<li>We go out tonight.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>movement and arrival</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je pars demain.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>communication and action</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous dites la vérité.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>ability, desire, obligation</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux venir.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>verb + verb patterns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle aime lire.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write eight decisions for the coming month using pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, commencer à, and essayer de.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 7 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i can read. 2. we want to leave. 3. she begins to write. 4. they try to understand. 5. i leave the house. 6. we go out tonight. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **movement and arrival**. use `je pars demain.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **communication and action**. use `vous dites la vérité.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **ability, desire, obligation**. use `je peux venir.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **verb + verb patterns**. use `elle aime lire.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write eight decisions for the coming month using pouvoir, vouloir, devoir, commencer à, and essayer de. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 7 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-answer-key\">Chapter 7 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je peux lire.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voulons partir.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle commence à écrire.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils essaient de comprendre.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je quitte la maison.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sortons ce soir.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Movement and arrival:</strong> Partir means to leave/depart, sortir means to go out or take out, quitter takes a direct object, and venir means to come.</li>\n<li><strong>Communication and action:</strong> Practice dire, lire, écrire, mettre, recevoir, and related forms as complete phrases.</li>\n<li><strong>Ability, desire, obligation:</strong> Pouvoir, vouloir, and devoir are followed directly by an infinitive.</li>\n<li><strong>Verb + verb patterns:</strong> Some verbs take no preposition, some take à, and others take de before an infinitive. Memorize the whole frame.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 7 answer key ## section a 1. `je peux lire.` 2. `nous voulons partir.` 3. `elle commence à écrire.` 4. `ils essaient de comprendre.` 5. `je quitte la maison.` 6. `nous sortons ce soir.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **movement and arrival:** partir means to leave/depart, sortir means to go out or take out, quitter takes a direct object, and venir means to come. - **communication and action:** practice dire, lire, écrire, mettre, recevoir, and related forms as complete phrases. - **ability, desire, obligation:** pouvoir, vouloir, and devoir are followed directly by an infinitive. - **verb + verb patterns:** some verbs take no preposition, some take à, and others take de before an infinitive. memorize the whole frame. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-07-irregular-verbs-infinitives/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 7 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-7-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 7 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Movement and arrival</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Communication and action</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Ability, desire, obligation</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Verb + verb patterns</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 7 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **movement and arrival**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **communication and action** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **ability, desire, obligation**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **verb + verb patterns**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":8,"slug":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns","title":"Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns","documents":[{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/README.md","title":"Chapter 8: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-irregular-verbs-ii-and-relative-pronouns\">Chapter 8: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Distinguish savoir and connaître.</li>\n<li>Use additional irregular verb families.</li>\n<li>Join ideas with qui, que, où, and dont.</li>\n<li>Use lequel after selected prepositions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Savoir versus connaître</li>\n<li>Irregular verb families</li>\n<li>Qui, que, and où</li>\n<li>Dont and lequel</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 8: irregular verbs ii and relative pronouns this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - distinguish savoir and connaître. - use additional irregular verb families. - join ideas with qui, que, où, and dont. - use lequel after selected prepositions. ## lesson sequence 1. savoir versus connaître 2. irregular verb families 3. qui, que, and où 4. dont and lequel ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 8 Lessons: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-lessons-irregular-verbs-ii-and-relative-pronouns\">Chapter 8 Lessons: Irregular Verbs II and Relative Pronouns</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-savoir-versus-conna-tre\">Unit 1: Savoir versus connaître</h2>\n<p>Savoir is used for facts, information, and knowing how; connaître is used for people, places, and familiarity.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code> — I know the answer.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle sait conduire.</code> — She knows how to drive.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous connaissons Paris.</code> — We know Paris.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-irregular-verb-families\">Unit 2: Irregular verb families</h2>\n<p>Learn voir/croire, courir/rire, offrir, conduire, suivre/vivre, and craindre through frequent first-person and plural forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois le problème.</code> — I see the problem.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous conduisons.</code> — We are driving.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils vivent ici.</code> — They live here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-qui-que-and-o\">Unit 3: Qui, que, and où</h2>\n<p>Qui is the subject of the relative clause; que is its direct object; où refers to a place or time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La femme qui parle...</code> — The woman who is speaking...</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre que je lis...</code> — The book that I am reading...</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La ville où j'habite...</code> — The city where I live...</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-dont-and-lequel\">Unit 4: Dont and lequel</h2>\n<p>Dont replaces de + noun. Forms of lequel commonly follow prepositions and agree with their antecedent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'ami dont je parle...</code> — The friend whom I am talking about...</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La table sur laquelle...</code> — The table on which...</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le projet auquel je pense...</code> — The project that I am thinking about...</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Describe a profession or person in six sentences. Include savoir, connaître, and four different relative pronouns.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.</code> — I know the woman who runs this company.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.</code> — Here is the book that my teacher recommends.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.</code> — Montreal is the city where they want to live.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.</code> — It is a profession that she often talks about.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 8 lessons: irregular verbs ii and relative pronouns use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: savoir versus connaître savoir is used for facts, information, and knowing how; connaître is used for people, places, and familiarity. ### examples - `je sais la réponse.` — i know the answer. - `elle sait conduire.` — she knows how to drive. - `nous connaissons paris.` — we know paris. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: irregular verb families learn voir/croire, courir/rire, offrir, conduire, suivre/vivre, and craindre through frequent first-person and plural forms. ### examples - `je vois le problème.` — i see the problem. - `nous conduisons.` — we are driving. - `ils vivent ici.` — they live here. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: qui, que, and où qui is the subject of the relative clause; que is its direct object; où refers to a place or time. ### examples - `la femme qui parle...` — the woman who is speaking... - `le livre que je lis...` — the book that i am reading... - `la ville où j'habite...` — the city where i live... ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: dont and lequel dont replaces de + noun. forms of lequel commonly follow prepositions and agree with their antecedent. ### examples - `l'ami dont je parle...` — the friend whom i am talking about... - `la table sur laquelle...` — the table on which... - `le projet auquel je pense...` — the project that i am thinking about... ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task describe a profession or person in six sentences. include savoir, connaître, and four different relative pronouns. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je connais la femme qui dirige cette entreprise.` — i know the woman who runs this company. 2. `voici le livre que mon professeur recommande.` — here is the book that my teacher recommends. 3. `montréal est la ville où ils veulent vivre.` — montreal is the city where they want to live. 4. `c'est un métier dont elle parle souvent.` — it is a profession that she often talks about. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 8 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-exercises\">Chapter 8 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I know Marie.</li>\n<li>I know the answer.</li>\n<li>The man speaks. He is my teacher.</li>\n<li>I read the book. The book is useful.</li>\n<li>This is the city. I live there.</li>\n<li>Here is the friend. I speak about him.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>savoir versus connaître</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>irregular verb families</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je vois le problème.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>qui, que, and où</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">La femme qui parle...</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>dont and lequel</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'ami dont je parle...</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Describe a profession or person in six sentences. Include savoir, connaître, and four different relative pronouns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 8 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i know marie. 2. i know the answer. 3. the man speaks. he is my teacher. 4. i read the book. the book is useful. 5. this is the city. i live there. 6. here is the friend. i speak about him. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **savoir versus connaître**. use `je sais la réponse.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **irregular verb families**. use `je vois le problème.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **qui, que, and où**. use `la femme qui parle...` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **dont and lequel**. use `l'ami dont je parle...` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing describe a profession or person in six sentences. include savoir, connaître, and four different relative pronouns. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 8 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-answer-key\">Chapter 8 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je connais Marie.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je sais la réponse.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">L'homme qui parle est mon professeur.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Le livre que je lis est utile.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">C'est la ville où j'habite.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Voici l'ami dont je parle.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Savoir versus connaître:</strong> Savoir is used for facts, information, and knowing how; connaître is used for people, places, and familiarity.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular verb families:</strong> Learn voir/croire, courir/rire, offrir, conduire, suivre/vivre, and craindre through frequent first-person and plural forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Qui, que, and où:</strong> Qui is the subject of the relative clause; que is its direct object; où refers to a place or time.</li>\n<li><strong>Dont and lequel:</strong> Dont replaces de + noun. Forms of lequel commonly follow prepositions and agree with their antecedent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 8 answer key ## section a 1. `je connais marie.` 2. `je sais la réponse.` 3. `l'homme qui parle est mon professeur.` 4. `le livre que je lis est utile.` 5. `c'est la ville où j'habite.` 6. `voici l'ami dont je parle.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **savoir versus connaître:** savoir is used for facts, information, and knowing how; connaître is used for people, places, and familiarity. - **irregular verb families:** learn voir/croire, courir/rire, offrir, conduire, suivre/vivre, and craindre through frequent first-person and plural forms. - **qui, que, and où:** qui is the subject of the relative clause; que is its direct object; où refers to a place or time. - **dont and lequel:** dont replaces de + noun. forms of lequel commonly follow prepositions and agree with their antecedent. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-08-irregular-verbs-relative-pronouns/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 8 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-8-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 8 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Savoir versus connaître</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Irregular verb families</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Qui, que, and où</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Dont and lequel</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 8 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **savoir versus connaître**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **irregular verb families** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **qui, que, and où**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **dont and lequel**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":9,"slug":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns","title":"Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns","documents":[{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/README.md","title":"Chapter 9: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-prendre-boire-partitive-articles-and-object-pronouns\">Chapter 9: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate prendre-family verbs and boire.</li>\n<li>Use du, de la, de l', and des.</li>\n<li>Replace direct and indirect objects.</li>\n<li>Use y and en.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Prendre and boire</li>\n<li>Partitive articles</li>\n<li>Direct and indirect objects</li>\n<li>Y and en</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 9: prendre, boire, partitive articles, and object pronouns this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate prendre-family verbs and boire. - use du, de la, de l', and des. - replace direct and indirect objects. - use y and en. ## lesson sequence 1. prendre and boire 2. partitive articles 3. direct and indirect objects 4. y and en ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 9 Lessons: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-lessons-prendre-boire-partitive-articles-and-object-pronouns\">Chapter 9 Lessons: Prendre, Boire, Partitive Articles, and Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-prendre-and-boire\">Unit 1: Prendre and boire</h2>\n<p>Practice these verbs in meals, transportation, and daily choices.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le métro.</code> — I take the subway.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous prenons un café.</code> — We have a coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle boit de l'eau.</code> — She drinks water.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-partitive-articles\">Unit 2: Partitive articles</h2>\n<p>Use du, de la, de l', and des for an unspecified quantity. After many negatives and quantity expressions, use de.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code> — some bread</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de la soupe</code> — some soup</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">de l'eau</code> — some water</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de lait.</code> — I do not drink milk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-direct-and-indirect-objects\">Unit 3: Direct and indirect objects</h2>\n<p>Place object pronouns before the conjugated verb: le, la, les for direct objects; lui and leur for many à + person objects.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le vois.</code> — I see him/it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lui parlons.</code> — We speak to him/her.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle les invite.</code> — She invites them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-y-and-en\">Unit 4: Y and en</h2>\n<p>Y often replaces à + thing or a place. En often replaces de + thing or a quantity phrase.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'y vais.</code> — I am going there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle en parle.</code> — She talks about it.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous en achetons deux.</code> — We buy two of them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write a grocery or café dialogue using two partitives, two direct objects, one indirect object, y, and en.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends du pain et je le mets sur la table.</code> — I take some bread and put it on the table.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.</code> — She speaks to her neighbors and offers them coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous allons au marché le samedi et nous y achetons des légumes.</code> — We go to the market on Saturdays and buy vegetables there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il veut trois pommes, alors il en prend trois.</code> — He wants three apples, so he takes three of them.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 9 lessons: prendre, boire, partitive articles, and object pronouns use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: prendre and boire practice these verbs in meals, transportation, and daily choices. ### examples - `je prends le métro.` — i take the subway. - `nous prenons un café.` — we have a coffee. - `elle boit de l'eau.` — she drinks water. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: partitive articles use du, de la, de l', and des for an unspecified quantity. after many negatives and quantity expressions, use de. ### examples - `du pain` — some bread - `de la soupe` — some soup - `de l'eau` — some water - `je ne bois pas de lait.` — i do not drink milk. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: direct and indirect objects place object pronouns before the conjugated verb: le, la, les for direct objects; lui and leur for many à + person objects. ### examples - `je le vois.` — i see him/it. - `nous lui parlons.` — we speak to him/her. - `elle les invite.` — she invites them. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: y and en y often replaces à + thing or a place. en often replaces de + thing or a quantity phrase. ### examples - `j'y vais.` — i am going there. - `elle en parle.` — she talks about it. - `nous en achetons deux.` — we buy two of them. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write a grocery or café dialogue using two partitives, two direct objects, one indirect object, y, and en. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je prends du pain et je le mets sur la table.` — i take some bread and put it on the table. 2. `elle parle à ses voisins et elle leur offre du café.` — she speaks to her neighbors and offers them coffee. 3. `nous allons au marché le samedi et nous y achetons des légumes.` — we go to the market on saturdays and buy vegetables there. 4. `il veut trois pommes, alors il en prend trois.` — he wants three apples, so he takes three of them. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 9 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-exercises\">Chapter 9 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Je mange le gâteau.</li>\n<li>Nous parlons à Marie.</li>\n<li>Ils vont au parc.</li>\n<li>Elle achète trois pommes.</li>\n<li>I drink some water.</li>\n<li>I do not drink milk.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>prendre and boire</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je prends le métro.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>partitive articles</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">du pain</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>direct and indirect objects</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le vois.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>y and en</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'y vais.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a grocery or café dialogue using two partitives, two direct objects, one indirect object, y, and en.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 9 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. je mange le gâteau. 2. nous parlons à marie. 3. ils vont au parc. 4. elle achète trois pommes. 5. i drink some water. 6. i do not drink milk. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **prendre and boire**. use `je prends le métro.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **partitive articles**. use `du pain` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **direct and indirect objects**. use `je le vois.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **y and en**. use `j'y vais.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write a grocery or café dialogue using two partitives, two direct objects, one indirect object, y, and en. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 9 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-answer-key\">Chapter 9 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le mange.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous lui parlons.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils y vont.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle en achète trois.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je bois de l'eau.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne bois pas de lait.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prendre and boire:</strong> Practice these verbs in meals, transportation, and daily choices.</li>\n<li><strong>Partitive articles:</strong> Use du, de la, de l', and des for an unspecified quantity. After many negatives and quantity expressions, use de.</li>\n<li><strong>Direct and indirect objects:</strong> Place object pronouns before the conjugated verb: le, la, les for direct objects; lui and leur for many à + person objects.</li>\n<li><strong>Y and en:</strong> Y often replaces à + thing or a place. En often replaces de + thing or a quantity phrase.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 9 answer key ## section a 1. `je le mange.` 2. `nous lui parlons.` 3. `ils y vont.` 4. `elle en achète trois.` 5. `je bois de l'eau.` 6. `je ne bois pas de lait.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **prendre and boire:** practice these verbs in meals, transportation, and daily choices. - **partitive articles:** use du, de la, de l', and des for an unspecified quantity. after many negatives and quantity expressions, use de. - **direct and indirect objects:** place object pronouns before the conjugated verb: le, la, les for direct objects; lui and leur for many à + person objects. - **y and en:** y often replaces à + thing or a place. en often replaces de + thing or a quantity phrase. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-09-partitives-object-pronouns/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 9 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-9-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 9 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Prendre and boire</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Partitive articles</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Direct and indirect objects</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Y and en</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 9 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **prendre and boire**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **partitive articles** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **direct and indirect objects**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **y and en**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":10,"slug":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs","title":"Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs","documents":[{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/README.md","title":"Chapter 10: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-and-adverbs\">Chapter 10: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use possessive and demonstrative adjectives/pronouns.</li>\n<li>Compare adjectives, nouns, and verbs.</li>\n<li>Form superlatives.</li>\n<li>Form and place common adverbs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Possessives</li>\n<li>Demonstratives</li>\n<li>Comparisons</li>\n<li>Adverbs</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 10: possessives, demonstratives, comparisons, and adverbs this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use possessive and demonstrative adjectives/pronouns. - compare adjectives, nouns, and verbs. - form superlatives. - form and place common adverbs. ## lesson sequence 1. possessives 2. demonstratives 3. comparisons 4. adverbs ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 10 Lessons: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-lessons-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-and-adverbs\">Chapter 10 Lessons: Possessives, Demonstratives, Comparisons, and Adverbs</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-possessives\">Unit 1: Possessives</h2>\n<p>Possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. Use mon before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère</code> — my brother</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ma sœur</code> — my sister</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mes parents</code> — my parents</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code> — my female friend</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-demonstratives\">Unit 2: Demonstratives</h2>\n<p>Use ce, cet, cette, and ces before nouns. Demonstrative pronouns replace nouns: celui, celle, ceux, celles.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre</code> — this/that book</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet arbre</code> — this/that tree</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cette maison</code> — this/that house</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ceux de Paul</code> — Paul's ones</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-comparisons\">Unit 3: Comparisons</h2>\n<p>Use plus...que, moins...que, and aussi...que. Compare nouns with plus de/moins de/autant de, and verbs with plus/moins/autant.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est plus rapide que moi.</code> — She is faster than I am.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a moins de temps.</code> — He has less time.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous travaillons autant.</code> — We work just as much.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-adverbs\">Unit 4: Adverbs</h2>\n<p>Many adverbs derive from the feminine adjective plus -ment. Their placement depends on the tense and length.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code> — slowly</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code> — fortunately/happily</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle parle clairement.</code> — She speaks clearly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il travaille très bien.</code> — He works very well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Compare two family members or two cities in eight sentences using possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur.</code> — My apartment is smaller than my sister's.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.</code> — This car travels less quickly than ours.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.</code> — These children speak as clearly as their parents.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Notre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble.</code> — Our family spends more time together.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 10 lessons: possessives, demonstratives, comparisons, and adverbs use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: possessives possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. use mon before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound. ### examples - `mon frère` — my brother - `ma sœur` — my sister - `mes parents` — my parents - `mon amie` — my female friend ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: demonstratives use ce, cet, cette, and ces before nouns. demonstrative pronouns replace nouns: celui, celle, ceux, celles. ### examples - `ce livre` — this/that book - `cet arbre` — this/that tree - `cette maison` — this/that house - `ceux de paul` — paul's ones ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: comparisons use plus...que, moins...que, and aussi...que. compare nouns with plus de/moins de/autant de, and verbs with plus/moins/autant. ### examples - `elle est plus rapide que moi.` — she is faster than i am. - `il a moins de temps.` — he has less time. - `nous travaillons autant.` — we work just as much. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: adverbs many adverbs derive from the feminine adjective plus -ment. their placement depends on the tense and length. ### examples - `lentement` — slowly - `heureusement` — fortunately/happily - `elle parle clairement.` — she speaks clearly. - `il travaille très bien.` — he works very well. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task compare two family members or two cities in eight sentences using possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `mon appartement est plus petit que celui de ma sœur.` — my apartment is smaller than my sister's. 2. `cette voiture roule moins rapidement que la nôtre.` — this car travels less quickly than ours. 3. `ces enfants parlent aussi clairement que leurs parents.` — these children speak as clearly as their parents. 4. `notre famille passe davantage de temps ensemble.` — our family spends more time together. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 10 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-exercises\">Chapter 10 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>my female friend</li>\n<li>this tree</li>\n<li>those houses</li>\n<li>Marie is taller than Luc.</li>\n<li>We have as much time.</li>\n<li>heureuse → adverb</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>possessives</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon frère</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>demonstratives</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ce livre</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>comparisons</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est plus rapide que moi.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>adverbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lentement</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Compare two family members or two cities in eight sentences using possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 10 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. my female friend 2. this tree 3. those houses 4. marie is taller than luc. 5. we have as much time. 6. heureuse -> adverb ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **possessives**. use `mon frère` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **demonstratives**. use `ce livre` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **comparisons**. use `elle est plus rapide que moi.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **adverbs**. use `lentement` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing compare two family members or two cities in eight sentences using possessives, demonstratives, and three comparison patterns. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 10 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-answer-key\">Chapter 10 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">mon amie</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">cet arbre</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ces maisons</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est plus grande que Luc.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons autant de temps.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">heureusement</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Possessives:</strong> Possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. Use mon before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound.</li>\n<li><strong>Demonstratives:</strong> Use ce, cet, cette, and ces before nouns. Demonstrative pronouns replace nouns: celui, celle, ceux, celles.</li>\n<li><strong>Comparisons:</strong> Use plus...que, moins...que, and aussi...que. Compare nouns with plus de/moins de/autant de, and verbs with plus/moins/autant.</li>\n<li><strong>Adverbs:</strong> Many adverbs derive from the feminine adjective plus -ment. Their placement depends on the tense and length.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 10 answer key ## section a 1. `mon amie` 2. `cet arbre` 3. `ces maisons` 4. `marie est plus grande que luc.` 5. `nous avons autant de temps.` 6. `heureusement` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **possessives:** possessive adjectives agree with the possessed noun, not the owner. use mon before a feminine noun beginning with a vowel sound. - **demonstratives:** use ce, cet, cette, and ces before nouns. demonstrative pronouns replace nouns: celui, celle, ceux, celles. - **comparisons:** use plus...que, moins...que, and aussi...que. compare nouns with plus de/moins de/autant de, and verbs with plus/moins/autant. - **adverbs:** many adverbs derive from the feminine adjective plus -ment. their placement depends on the tense and length. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-10-possessives-demonstratives-comparisons-adverbs/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 10 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-10-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 10 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Possessives</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Demonstratives</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Comparisons</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Adverbs</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 10 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **possessives**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **demonstratives** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **comparisons**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **adverbs**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":11,"slug":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative","title":"Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative","documents":[{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/README.md","title":"Chapter 11: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-stressed-pronouns-and-the-imperative\">Chapter 11: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use expanded affirmative and negative expressions.</li>\n<li>Choose stressed pronouns.</li>\n<li>Form affirmative and negative commands.</li>\n<li>Place object pronouns with commands.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Expanded negatives</li>\n<li>Stressed pronouns</li>\n<li>Imperative forms</li>\n<li>Commands with pronouns</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 11: affirmatives, negatives, stressed pronouns, and the imperative this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - use expanded affirmative and negative expressions. - choose stressed pronouns. - form affirmative and negative commands. - place object pronouns with commands. ## lesson sequence 1. expanded negatives 2. stressed pronouns 3. imperative forms 4. commands with pronouns ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 11 Lessons: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-lessons-affirmatives-negatives-stressed-pronouns-and-the-imperative\">Chapter 11 Lessons: Affirmatives, Negatives, Stressed Pronouns, and the Imperative</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-expanded-negatives\">Unit 1: Expanded negatives</h2>\n<p>Pairs include toujours/ne...jamais, déjà/ne...pas encore, quelqu'un/ne...personne, quelque chose/ne...rien, and encore/ne...plus.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois personne.</code> — I do not see anyone.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne mange jamais ici.</code> — She never eats here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous n'avons rien.</code> — We have nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-stressed-pronouns\">Unit 2: Stressed pronouns</h2>\n<p>Forms are moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles. Use them after prepositions, for emphasis, comparisons, and short answers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code> — with me</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lui, il comprend.</code> — He understands.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle travaille plus que nous.</code> — She works more than we do.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-imperative-forms\">Unit 3: Imperative forms</h2>\n<p>Commands use tu, nous, and vous forms without subject pronouns. Regular -er tu commands normally drop final -s.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle !</code> — Speak!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons !</code> — Let's finish!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Attendez !</code> — Wait!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Soyez prudents !</code> — Be careful!</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-commands-with-pronouns\">Unit 4: Commands with pronouns</h2>\n<p>In affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens and their order changes. In negative commands, they return before the verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code> — Look at it/him!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-lui le livre !</code> — Give him/her the book!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le regardez pas !</code> — Do not look at it/him!</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write house or study instructions containing four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.</code> — As for me, I do not see anyone in the garden.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne laissez rien sur la table !</code> — Do not leave anything on the table!</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.</code> — Give me the hammer and then bring it to him.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.</code> — As for them, they never work on Sundays.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 11 lessons: affirmatives, negatives, stressed pronouns, and the imperative use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: expanded negatives pairs include toujours/ne...jamais, déjà/ne...pas encore, quelqu'un/ne...personne, quelque chose/ne...rien, and encore/ne...plus. ### examples - `je ne vois personne.` — i do not see anyone. - `elle ne mange jamais ici.` — she never eats here. - `nous n'avons rien.` — we have nothing. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: stressed pronouns forms are moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles. use them after prepositions, for emphasis, comparisons, and short answers. ### examples - `avec moi` — with me - `lui, il comprend.` — he understands. - `elle travaille plus que nous.` — she works more than we do. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: imperative forms commands use tu, nous, and vous forms without subject pronouns. regular -er tu commands normally drop final -s. ### examples - `parle !` — speak! - `finissons !` — let's finish! - `attendez !` — wait! - `soyez prudents !` — be careful! ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: commands with pronouns in affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens and their order changes. in negative commands, they return before the verb. ### examples - `regarde-le !` — look at it/him! - `donnez-lui le livre !` — give him/her the book! - `ne le regardez pas !` — do not look at it/him! ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write house or study instructions containing four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `moi, je ne vois personne dans le jardin.` — as for me, i do not see anyone in the garden. 2. `ne laissez rien sur la table !` — do not leave anything on the table! 3. `donne-moi le marteau et apporte-le-lui ensuite.` — give me the hammer and then bring it to him. 4. `eux, ils ne travaillent jamais le dimanche.` — as for them, they never work on sundays. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 11 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-exercises\">Chapter 11 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>You never speak.</li>\n<li>with her</li>\n<li>You (tu) listen.</li>\n<li>We finish.</li>\n<li>Look at it.</li>\n<li>Do not give it to him.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>expanded negatives</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne vois personne.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>stressed pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec moi</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>imperative forms</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Parle !</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>commands with pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write house or study instructions containing four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 11 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. you never speak. 2. with her 3. you (tu) listen. 4. we finish. 5. look at it. 6. do not give it to him. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **expanded negatives**. use `je ne vois personne.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **stressed pronouns**. use `avec moi` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **imperative forms**. use `parle !` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **commands with pronouns**. use `regarde-le !` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write house or study instructions containing four affirmative commands, four negative commands, and three stressed pronouns. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 11 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-answer-key\">Chapter 11 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous ne parlez jamais.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">avec elle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Écoute !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Finissons !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Regarde-le !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ne le lui donne pas !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expanded negatives:</strong> Pairs include toujours/ne...jamais, déjà/ne...pas encore, quelqu'un/ne...personne, quelque chose/ne...rien, and encore/ne...plus.</li>\n<li><strong>Stressed pronouns:</strong> Forms are moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles. Use them after prepositions, for emphasis, comparisons, and short answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Imperative forms:</strong> Commands use tu, nous, and vous forms without subject pronouns. Regular -er tu commands normally drop final -s.</li>\n<li><strong>Commands with pronouns:</strong> In affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens and their order changes. In negative commands, they return before the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 11 answer key ## section a 1. `vous ne parlez jamais.` 2. `avec elle` 3. `écoute !` 4. `finissons !` 5. `regarde-le !` 6. `ne le lui donne pas !` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **expanded negatives:** pairs include toujours/ne...jamais, déjà/ne...pas encore, quelqu'un/ne...personne, quelque chose/ne...rien, and encore/ne...plus. - **stressed pronouns:** forms are moi, toi, lui, elle, nous, vous, eux, elles. use them after prepositions, for emphasis, comparisons, and short answers. - **imperative forms:** commands use tu, nous, and vous forms without subject pronouns. regular -er tu commands normally drop final -s. - **commands with pronouns:** in affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb with hyphens and their order changes. in negative commands, they return before the verb. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-11-affirmatives-negatives-imperative/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 11 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-11-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 11 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Expanded negatives</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Stressed pronouns</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Imperative forms</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Commands with pronouns</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 11 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **expanded negatives**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **stressed pronouns** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **imperative forms**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **commands with pronouns**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":12,"slug":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle","title":"Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle","documents":[{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/README.md","title":"Chapter 12: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-and-the-present-participle\">Chapter 12: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Conjugate reflexive and reciprocal verbs.</li>\n<li>Form negatives, questions, and commands with pronominal verbs.</li>\n<li>Use body-part constructions.</li>\n<li>Form and use the present participle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Reflexive verbs</li>\n<li>Questions, negatives, commands</li>\n<li>Body parts and reciprocal meaning</li>\n<li>Present participle</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 12: pronominal verbs and the present participle this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - conjugate reflexive and reciprocal verbs. - form negatives, questions, and commands with pronominal verbs. - use body-part constructions. - form and use the present participle. ## lesson sequence 1. reflexive verbs 2. questions, negatives, commands 3. body parts and reciprocal meaning 4. present participle ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 12 Lessons: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-lessons-pronominal-verbs-and-the-present-participle\">Chapter 12 Lessons: Pronominal Verbs and the Present Participle</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-reflexive-verbs\">Unit 1: Reflexive verbs</h2>\n<p>Use me, te, se, nous, vous, se before the conjugated verb when the subject acts on itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève.</code> — I get up.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous préparons.</code> — We get ready.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles se reposent.</code> — They rest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-questions-negatives-commands\">Unit 2: Questions, negatives, commands</h2>\n<p>Keep the reflexive pronoun before the verb in negatives and most questions. In affirmative commands, use toi/nous/vous after the verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne se couche pas.</code> — She does not go to bed.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Vous vous levez ?</code> — Are you getting up?</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code> — Get up!</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-body-parts-and-reciprocal-meaning\">Unit 3: Body parts and reciprocal meaning</h2>\n<p>French often uses the definite article for body parts when possession is clear. Plural subjects may produce reciprocal meaning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code> — I wash my hands.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se parlent.</code> — They speak to each other.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous écrivons.</code> — We write to each other.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-present-participle\">Unit 4: Present participle</h2>\n<p>Usually form it from the nous stem minus -ons plus -ant. It can express simultaneous action, often with en.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code> — speaking</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">finissant</code> — finishing</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en travaillant</code> — while/by working</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En marchant, elle téléphone.</code> — While walking, she makes a phone call.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write your morning routine in eight sentences and add two simultaneous actions with en + present participle.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.</code> — I get up early and get ready quickly.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.</code> — The children wash their hands before dinner.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.</code> — We talk to each other while walking to work.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">En étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.</code> — By studying every day, she makes progress.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 12 lessons: pronominal verbs and the present participle use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: reflexive verbs use me, te, se, nous, vous, se before the conjugated verb when the subject acts on itself. ### examples - `je me lève.` — i get up. - `nous nous préparons.` — we get ready. - `elles se reposent.` — they rest. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: questions, negatives, commands keep the reflexive pronoun before the verb in negatives and most questions. in affirmative commands, use toi/nous/vous after the verb. ### examples - `elle ne se couche pas.` — she does not go to bed. - `vous vous levez ?` — are you getting up? - `lève-toi !` — get up! ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: body parts and reciprocal meaning french often uses the definite article for body parts when possession is clear. plural subjects may produce reciprocal meaning. ### examples - `je me lave les mains.` — i wash my hands. - `ils se parlent.` — they speak to each other. - `nous nous écrivons.` — we write to each other. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: present participle usually form it from the nous stem minus -ons plus -ant. it can express simultaneous action, often with en. ### examples - `parlant` — speaking - `finissant` — finishing - `en travaillant` — while/by working - `en marchant, elle téléphone.` — while walking, she makes a phone call. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write your morning routine in eight sentences and add two simultaneous actions with en + present participle. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `je me lève tôt et je me prépare rapidement.` — i get up early and get ready quickly. 2. `les enfants se lavent les mains avant le dîner.` — the children wash their hands before dinner. 3. `nous nous parlons en marchant vers le travail.` — we talk to each other while walking to work. 4. `en étudiant chaque jour, elle fait des progrès.` — by studying every day, she makes progress. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 12 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-exercises\">Chapter 12 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I wake up.</li>\n<li>We do not get dressed.</li>\n<li>Get up! (tu)</li>\n<li>Wash your hands! (vous)</li>\n<li>nous parlons → participle</li>\n<li>while studying</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>reflexive verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lève.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>questions, negatives, commands</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle ne se couche pas.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>body parts and reciprocal meaning</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me lave les mains.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>present participle</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write your morning routine in eight sentences and add two simultaneous actions with en + present participle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 12 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i wake up. 2. we do not get dressed. 3. get up! (tu) 4. wash your hands! (vous) 5. nous parlons -> participle 6. while studying ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **reflexive verbs**. use `je me lève.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **questions, negatives, commands**. use `elle ne se couche pas.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **body parts and reciprocal meaning**. use `je me lave les mains.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **present participle**. use `parlant` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write your morning routine in eight sentences and add two simultaneous actions with en + present participle. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 12 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-answer-key\">Chapter 12 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je me réveille.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne nous habillons pas.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lève-toi !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Lavez-vous les mains !</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">parlant</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">en étudiant</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reflexive verbs:</strong> Use me, te, se, nous, vous, se before the conjugated verb when the subject acts on itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Questions, negatives, commands:</strong> Keep the reflexive pronoun before the verb in negatives and most questions. In affirmative commands, use toi/nous/vous after the verb.</li>\n<li><strong>Body parts and reciprocal meaning:</strong> French often uses the definite article for body parts when possession is clear. Plural subjects may produce reciprocal meaning.</li>\n<li><strong>Present participle:</strong> Usually form it from the nous stem minus -ons plus -ant. It can express simultaneous action, often with en.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 12 answer key ## section a 1. `je me réveille.` 2. `nous ne nous habillons pas.` 3. `lève-toi !` 4. `lavez-vous les mains !` 5. `parlant` 6. `en étudiant` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **reflexive verbs:** use me, te, se, nous, vous, se before the conjugated verb when the subject acts on itself. - **questions, negatives, commands:** keep the reflexive pronoun before the verb in negatives and most questions. in affirmative commands, use toi/nous/vous after the verb. - **body parts and reciprocal meaning:** french often uses the definite article for body parts when possession is clear. plural subjects may produce reciprocal meaning. - **present participle:** usually form it from the nous stem minus -ons plus -ant. it can express simultaneous action, often with en. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-12-pronominal-verbs-present-participle/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 12 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-12-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 12 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Reflexive verbs</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Questions, negatives, commands</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Body parts and reciprocal meaning</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Present participle</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 12 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **reflexive verbs**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **questions, negatives, commands** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **body parts and reciprocal meaning**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **present participle**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":13,"slug":"chapter-13-passe-compose","title":"The Passé Composé","documents":[{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/README.md","title":"Chapter 13: The Passé Composé","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-the-pass-compos\">Chapter 13: The Passé Composé</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form the passé composé with avoir and être.</li>\n<li>Use regular and common irregular past participles.</li>\n<li>Place negatives, questions, and adverbs.</li>\n<li>Handle basic past-participle agreement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Avoir + past participle</li>\n<li>Irregular participles</li>\n<li>Être verbs</li>\n<li>Placement and agreement</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 13: the passé composé this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form the passé composé with avoir and être. - use regular and common irregular past participles. - place negatives, questions, and adverbs. - handle basic past-participle agreement. ## lesson sequence 1. avoir + past participle 2. irregular participles 3. être verbs 4. placement and agreement ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 13 Lessons: The Passé Composé","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-lessons-the-pass-compos\">Chapter 13 Lessons: The Passé Composé</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-avoir-past-participle\">Unit 1: Avoir + past participle</h2>\n<p>Most verbs use present-tense avoir plus a past participle. Regular endings are -é for -er, -i for regular -ir, and -u for regular -re.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé.</code> — I spoke.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons fini.</code> — We finished.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a vendu.</code> — She sold.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-irregular-participles\">Unit 2: Irregular participles</h2>\n<p>Learn frequent forms as verb chunks: faire/fait, prendre/pris, voir/vu, lire/lu, écrire/écrit, boire/bu, avoir/eu, être/été.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a fait du sport.</code> — He exercised.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai lu le message.</code> — I read the message.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elles ont pris le train.</code> — They took the train.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-tre-verbs\">Unit 3: Être verbs</h2>\n<p>A smaller movement/change group and pronominal verbs use être. The participle generally agrees with the subject.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code> — She arrived.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils sont partis.</code> — They left.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous nous sommes levés.</code> — We got up.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-placement-and-agreement\">Unit 4: Placement and agreement</h2>\n<p>Negation surrounds the auxiliary. Short adverbs often follow it. A preceding direct-object pronoun may trigger agreement with an avoir verb.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas mangé.</code> — I did not eat.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle a bien travaillé.</code> — She worked well.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Je les ai écrites.</code> — The letters? I wrote them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write a ten-sentence account of yesterday using six avoir verbs, two être verbs, one pronominal verb, and one negative.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Hier, j'ai préparé le dîner et j'ai regardé un film.</code> — Yesterday, I prepared dinner and watched a movie.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Marie est arrivée à huit heures et elle est repartie à minuit.</code> — Marie arrived at eight and left again at midnight.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous ne nous sommes pas levés tôt ce matin.</code> — We did not get up early this morning.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les photos ? Je les ai prises pendant le voyage.</code> — The photos? I took them during the trip.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 13 lessons: the passé composé use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: avoir + past participle most verbs use present-tense avoir plus a past participle. regular endings are -é for -er, -i for regular -ir, and -u for regular -re. ### examples - `j'ai parlé.` — i spoke. - `nous avons fini.` — we finished. - `elle a vendu.` — she sold. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: irregular participles learn frequent forms as verb chunks: faire/fait, prendre/pris, voir/vu, lire/lu, écrire/écrit, boire/bu, avoir/eu, être/été. ### examples - `il a fait du sport.` — he exercised. - `j'ai lu le message.` — i read the message. - `elles ont pris le train.` — they took the train. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: être verbs a smaller movement/change group and pronominal verbs use être. the participle generally agrees with the subject. ### examples - `elle est arrivée.` — she arrived. - `ils sont partis.` — they left. - `nous nous sommes levés.` — we got up. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: placement and agreement negation surrounds the auxiliary. short adverbs often follow it. a preceding direct-object pronoun may trigger agreement with an avoir verb. ### examples - `je n'ai pas mangé.` — i did not eat. - `elle a bien travaillé.` — she worked well. - `les lettres ? je les ai écrites.` — the letters? i wrote them. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write a ten-sentence account of yesterday using six avoir verbs, two être verbs, one pronominal verb, and one negative. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `hier, j'ai préparé le dîner et j'ai regardé un film.` — yesterday, i prepared dinner and watched a movie. 2. `marie est arrivée à huit heures et elle est repartie à minuit.` — marie arrived at eight and left again at midnight. 3. `nous ne nous sommes pas levés tôt ce matin.` — we did not get up early this morning. 4. `les photos ? je les ai prises pendant le voyage.` — the photos? i took them during the trip. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 13 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-exercises\">Chapter 13 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Je parle.</li>\n<li>Nous finissons.</li>\n<li>Elle arrive.</li>\n<li>Ils se lèvent.</li>\n<li>I did not see it.</li>\n<li>The letters? I wrote them.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>avoir + past participle</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>irregular participles</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il a fait du sport.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>être verbs</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>placement and agreement</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je n'ai pas mangé.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a ten-sentence account of yesterday using six avoir verbs, two être verbs, one pronominal verb, and one negative.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 13 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. je parle. 2. nous finissons. 3. elle arrive. 4. ils se lèvent. 5. i did not see it. 6. the letters? i wrote them. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **avoir + past participle**. use `j'ai parlé.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **irregular participles**. use `il a fait du sport.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **être verbs**. use `elle est arrivée.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **placement and agreement**. use `je n'ai pas mangé.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write a ten-sentence account of yesterday using six avoir verbs, two être verbs, one pronominal verb, and one negative. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 13 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-answer-key\">Chapter 13 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'ai parlé.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avons fini.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle est arrivée.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils se sont levés.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne l'ai pas vu.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Les lettres ? Je les ai écrites.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Avoir + past participle:</strong> Most verbs use present-tense avoir plus a past participle. Regular endings are -é for -er, -i for regular -ir, and -u for regular -re.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular participles:</strong> Learn frequent forms as verb chunks: faire/fait, prendre/pris, voir/vu, lire/lu, écrire/écrit, boire/bu, avoir/eu, être/été.</li>\n<li><strong>Être verbs:</strong> A smaller movement/change group and pronominal verbs use être. The participle generally agrees with the subject.</li>\n<li><strong>Placement and agreement:</strong> Negation surrounds the auxiliary. Short adverbs often follow it. A preceding direct-object pronoun may trigger agreement with an avoir verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 13 answer key ## section a 1. `j'ai parlé.` 2. `nous avons fini.` 3. `elle est arrivée.` 4. `ils se sont levés.` 5. `je ne l'ai pas vu.` 6. `les lettres ? je les ai écrites.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **avoir + past participle:** most verbs use present-tense avoir plus a past participle. regular endings are -é for -er, -i for regular -ir, and -u for regular -re. - **irregular participles:** learn frequent forms as verb chunks: faire/fait, prendre/pris, voir/vu, lire/lu, écrire/écrit, boire/bu, avoir/eu, être/été. - **être verbs:** a smaller movement/change group and pronominal verbs use être. the participle generally agrees with the subject. - **placement and agreement:** negation surrounds the auxiliary. short adverbs often follow it. a preceding direct-object pronoun may trigger agreement with an avoir verb. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-13-passe-compose/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 13 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-13-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 13 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Avoir + past participle</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Irregular participles</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Être verbs</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Placement and agreement</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 13 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **avoir + past participle**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **irregular participles** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **être verbs**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **placement and agreement**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":14,"slug":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns","title":"The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns","documents":[{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/README.md","title":"Chapter 14: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-the-imparfait-past-narration-and-double-object-pronouns\">Chapter 14: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form and use the imparfait.</li>\n<li>Contrast background and completed events.</li>\n<li>Combine passé composé and imparfait.</li>\n<li>Order double object pronouns.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forming the imparfait</li>\n<li>Choosing the past tense</li>\n<li>Past narration</li>\n<li>Double object pronouns</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 14: the imparfait, past narration, and double object pronouns this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form and use the imparfait. - contrast background and completed events. - combine passé composé and imparfait. - order double object pronouns. ## lesson sequence 1. forming the imparfait 2. choosing the past tense 3. past narration 4. double object pronouns ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 14 Lessons: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-lessons-the-imparfait-past-narration-and-double-object-pronouns\">Chapter 14 Lessons: The Imparfait, Past Narration, and Double Object Pronouns</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-forming-the-imparfait\">Unit 1: Forming the imparfait</h2>\n<p>Take the present nous form, remove -ons, and add -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. Être uses ét-.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code> — I was speaking/used to speak</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">nous finissions</code> — we were finishing/used to finish</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">vous étiez</code> — you were</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-choosing-the-past-tense\">Unit 2: Choosing the past tense</h2>\n<p>Use the imparfait for background, description, habit, and ongoing action; use the passé composé for bounded completed events.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code> — It was raining.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Lyon.</code> — We lived/used to live in Lyon.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Soudain, le téléphone a sonné.</code> — Suddenly, the telephone rang.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-past-narration\">Unit 3: Past narration</h2>\n<p>Set the scene with the imparfait and move events forward with the passé composé.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dormais quand Marie a appelé.</code> — I was sleeping when Marie called.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faisait beau, puis un orage a commencé.</code> — The weather was nice, then a storm began.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-double-object-pronouns\">Unit 4: Double object pronouns</h2>\n<p>Pronoun order is fixed before ordinary verbs. With affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb in a different order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> — I give it to him/her.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous y en mettons.</code> — We put some there.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-le-moi !</code> — Give it to me!</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write a short travel memory: four background sentences in the imparfait and four completed events in the passé composé.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.</code> — It was raining and the streets were almost empty.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.</code> — I was reading in my room when the telephone rang.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous habitions à Paris lorsque nous avons rencontré Léa.</code> — We were living in Paris when we met Léa.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.</code> — Paul wanted the tickets, so I gave them to him.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 14 lessons: the imparfait, past narration, and double object pronouns use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: forming the imparfait take the present nous form, remove -ons, and add -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. être uses ét-. ### examples - `je parlais` — i was speaking/used to speak - `nous finissions` — we were finishing/used to finish - `vous étiez` — you were ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: choosing the past tense use the imparfait for background, description, habit, and ongoing action; use the passé composé for bounded completed events. ### examples - `il pleuvait.` — it was raining. - `nous habitions à lyon.` — we lived/used to live in lyon. - `soudain, le téléphone a sonné.` — suddenly, the telephone rang. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: past narration set the scene with the imparfait and move events forward with the passé composé. ### examples - `je dormais quand marie a appelé.` — i was sleeping when marie called. - `il faisait beau, puis un orage a commencé.` — the weather was nice, then a storm began. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: double object pronouns pronoun order is fixed before ordinary verbs. with affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb in a different order. ### examples - `je le lui donne.` — i give it to him/her. - `nous y en mettons.` — we put some there. - `donnez-le-moi !` — give it to me! ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write a short travel memory: four background sentences in the imparfait and four completed events in the passé composé. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `il pleuvait et les rues étaient presque vides.` — it was raining and the streets were almost empty. 2. `je lisais dans ma chambre quand le téléphone a sonné.` — i was reading in my room when the telephone rang. 3. `nous habitions à paris lorsque nous avons rencontré léa.` — we were living in paris when we met léa. 4. `paul voulait les billets, alors je les lui ai donnés.` — paul wanted the tickets, so i gave them to him. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 14 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-exercises\">Chapter 14 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>nous parlons → imparfait, je</li>\n<li>nous finissons → imparfait, ils</li>\n<li>It was raining.</li>\n<li>I was reading when he arrived.</li>\n<li>Je donne le livre à Paul.</li>\n<li>Give it to me!</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>forming the imparfait</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>choosing the past tense</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>past narration</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je dormais quand Marie a appelé.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>double object pronouns</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write a short travel memory: four background sentences in the imparfait and four completed events in the passé composé.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 14 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. nous parlons -> imparfait, je 2. nous finissons -> imparfait, ils 3. it was raining. 4. i was reading when he arrived. 5. je donne le livre à paul. 6. give it to me! ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **forming the imparfait**. use `je parlais` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **choosing the past tense**. use `il pleuvait.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **past narration**. use `je dormais quand marie a appelé.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **double object pronouns**. use `je le lui donne.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write a short travel memory: four background sentences in the imparfait and four completed events in the passé composé. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 14 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-answer-key\">Chapter 14 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">je parlais</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">ils finissaient</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il pleuvait.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je lisais quand il est arrivé.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je le lui donne.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Donnez-le-moi !</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forming the imparfait:</strong> Take the present nous form, remove -ons, and add -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. Être uses ét-.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing the past tense:</strong> Use the imparfait for background, description, habit, and ongoing action; use the passé composé for bounded completed events.</li>\n<li><strong>Past narration:</strong> Set the scene with the imparfait and move events forward with the passé composé.</li>\n<li><strong>Double object pronouns:</strong> Pronoun order is fixed before ordinary verbs. With affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb in a different order.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 14 answer key ## section a 1. `je parlais` 2. `ils finissaient` 3. `il pleuvait.` 4. `je lisais quand il est arrivé.` 5. `je le lui donne.` 6. `donnez-le-moi !` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **forming the imparfait:** take the present nous form, remove -ons, and add -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. être uses ét-. - **choosing the past tense:** use the imparfait for background, description, habit, and ongoing action; use the passé composé for bounded completed events. - **past narration:** set the scene with the imparfait and move events forward with the passé composé. - **double object pronouns:** pronoun order is fixed before ordinary verbs. with affirmative commands, pronouns follow the verb in a different order. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-14-imparfait-past-narration-double-pronouns/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 14 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-14-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 14 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Forming the imparfait</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Choosing the past tense</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Past narration</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Double object pronouns</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 14 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **forming the imparfait**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **choosing the past tense** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **past narration**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **double object pronouns**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":15,"slug":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites","title":"Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites","documents":[{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/README.md","title":"Chapter 15: Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-future-conditional-pluperfect-and-indefinites\">Chapter 15: Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form the future and conditional.</li>\n<li>Use common irregular stems.</li>\n<li>Form the pluperfect and past conditional.</li>\n<li>Use tout and other indefinite words.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Future tense</li>\n<li>Conditional</li>\n<li>Earlier past and unreal past</li>\n<li>Tout and indefinites</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 15: future, conditional, pluperfect, and indefinites this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form the future and conditional. - use common irregular stems. - form the pluperfect and past conditional. - use tout and other indefinite words. ## lesson sequence 1. future tense 2. conditional 3. earlier past and unreal past 4. tout and indefinites ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 15 Lessons: Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-lessons-future-conditional-pluperfect-and-indefinites\">Chapter 15 Lessons: Future, Conditional, Pluperfect, and Indefinites</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-future-tense\">Unit 1: Future tense</h2>\n<p>Add -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the infinitive or irregular future stem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parlerai.</code> — I will speak.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous finirons.</code> — We will finish.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle viendra.</code> — She will come.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Ils auront.</code> — They will have.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-conditional\">Unit 2: Conditional</h2>\n<p>Use the future stem plus imparfait endings. It expresses politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café.</code> — I would like a coffee.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous voyagerions.</code> — We would travel.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais dormir.</code> — You should sleep.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-earlier-past-and-unreal-past\">Unit 3: Earlier past and unreal past</h2>\n<p>The pluperfect uses the imparfait of avoir/être plus a participle. The past conditional uses the conditional auxiliary plus a participle.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais fini.</code> — I had finished.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle était partie.</code> — She had left.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous aurions accepté.</code> — We would have accepted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-tout-and-indefinites\">Unit 4: Tout and indefinites</h2>\n<p>Tout changes form as an adjective or pronoun: tout, toute, tous, toutes. Other useful indefinite forms include chaque, plusieurs, certains, aucun, and chacun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la journée</code> — all day</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">tous les clients</code> — all the customers</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">plusieurs idées</code> — several ideas</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">chacun choisira</code> — each person will choose</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write future plans and a hypothetical alternative: five future sentences, four conditional sentences, and one past-conditional sentence.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.</code> — Tomorrow, we will go to the bank and speak to the manager.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.</code> — I would like to open an account, please.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.</code> — If I had more time, I would travel in all these countries.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.</code> — She had already finished when we arrived.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 15 lessons: future, conditional, pluperfect, and indefinites use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: future tense add -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the infinitive or irregular future stem. ### examples - `je parlerai.` — i will speak. - `nous finirons.` — we will finish. - `elle viendra.` — she will come. - `ils auront.` — they will have. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: conditional use the future stem plus imparfait endings. it expresses politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results. ### examples - `je voudrais un café.` — i would like a coffee. - `nous voyagerions.` — we would travel. - `tu devrais dormir.` — you should sleep. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: earlier past and unreal past the pluperfect uses the imparfait of avoir/être plus a participle. the past conditional uses the conditional auxiliary plus a participle. ### examples - `j'avais fini.` — i had finished. - `elle était partie.` — she had left. - `nous aurions accepté.` — we would have accepted. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: tout and indefinites tout changes form as an adjective or pronoun: tout, toute, tous, toutes. other useful indefinite forms include chaque, plusieurs, certains, aucun, and chacun. ### examples - `toute la journée` — all day - `tous les clients` — all the customers - `plusieurs idées` — several ideas - `chacun choisira` — each person will choose ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write future plans and a hypothetical alternative: five future sentences, four conditional sentences, and one past-conditional sentence. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `demain, nous irons à la banque et nous parlerons au directeur.` — tomorrow, we will go to the bank and speak to the manager. 2. `je voudrais ouvrir un compte, s'il vous plaît.` — i would like to open an account, please. 3. `si j'avais plus de temps, je voyagerais dans tous ces pays.` — if i had more time, i would travel in all these countries. 4. `elle avait déjà terminé quand nous sommes arrivés.` — she had already finished when we arrived. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 15 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-exercises\">Chapter 15 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>I will speak.</li>\n<li>She will come.</li>\n<li>I would like...</li>\n<li>You should leave.</li>\n<li>We had finished.</li>\n<li>all the houses</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>future tense</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parlerai.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>conditional</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais un café.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>earlier past and unreal past</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">J'avais fini.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>tout and indefinites</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toute la journée</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write future plans and a hypothetical alternative: five future sentences, four conditional sentences, and one past-conditional sentence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 15 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. i will speak. 2. she will come. 3. i would like... 4. you should leave. 5. we had finished. 6. all the houses ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **future tense**. use `je parlerai.` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **conditional**. use `je voudrais un café.` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **earlier past and unreal past**. use `j'avais fini.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **tout and indefinites**. use `toute la journée` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write future plans and a hypothetical alternative: five future sentences, four conditional sentences, and one past-conditional sentence. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 15 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-answer-key\">Chapter 15 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je parlerai.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Elle viendra.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je voudrais...</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Tu devrais partir.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous avions fini.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">toutes les maisons</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Future tense:</strong> Add -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the infinitive or irregular future stem.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditional:</strong> Use the future stem plus imparfait endings. It expresses politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results.</li>\n<li><strong>Earlier past and unreal past:</strong> The pluperfect uses the imparfait of avoir/être plus a participle. The past conditional uses the conditional auxiliary plus a participle.</li>\n<li><strong>Tout and indefinites:</strong> Tout changes form as an adjective or pronoun: tout, toute, tous, toutes. Other useful indefinite forms include chaque, plusieurs, certains, aucun, and chacun.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 15 answer key ## section a 1. `je parlerai.` 2. `elle viendra.` 3. `je voudrais...` 4. `tu devrais partir.` 5. `nous avions fini.` 6. `toutes les maisons` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **future tense:** add -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont to the infinitive or irregular future stem. - **conditional:** use the future stem plus imparfait endings. it expresses politeness, wishes, advice, and hypothetical results. - **earlier past and unreal past:** the pluperfect uses the imparfait of avoir/être plus a participle. the past conditional uses the conditional auxiliary plus a participle. - **tout and indefinites:** tout changes form as an adjective or pronoun: tout, toute, tous, toutes. other useful indefinite forms include chaque, plusieurs, certains, aucun, and chacun. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-15-future-conditional-indefinites/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 15 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-15-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 15 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Future tense</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Conditional</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Earlier past and unreal past</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Tout and indefinites</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 15 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **future tense**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **conditional** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **earlier past and unreal past**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **tout and indefinites**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]},{"number":16,"slug":"chapter-16-subjunctive","title":"The Present Subjunctive","documents":[{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/README.md","title":"Chapter 16: The Present Subjunctive","type":"Overview","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-the-present-subjunctive\">Chapter 16: The Present Subjunctive</h1>\n<p>This is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. It does not reproduce the textbook.</p>\n<h2 id=\"outcomes\">Outcomes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Form regular and common irregular subjunctives.</li>\n<li>Recognize triggers involving necessity and judgment.</li>\n<li>Use the subjunctive after volition, emotion, and doubt.</li>\n<li>Contrast indicative and subjunctive meaning.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lesson-sequence\">Lesson sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Forming the subjunctive</li>\n<li>Important irregulars</li>\n<li>Necessity and judgment</li>\n<li>Volition, emotion, and doubt</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"recommended-workflow\">Recommended workflow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Read one unit in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Say every French example aloud twice.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples and rewrite them from memory.</li>\n<li>Complete the matching transformation in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">exercises.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Check <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">answer-key.md</code> only after finishing.</li>\n<li>Record the rule behind each error.</li>\n<li>Follow the spaced plan in <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">review-plan.md</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"mastery-standard\">Mastery standard</h2>\n<p>Continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes.</p>","search":"# chapter 16: the present subjunctive this is an original companion learning pack based on the chapter's grammar sequence. it does not reproduce the textbook. ## outcomes - form regular and common irregular subjunctives. - recognize triggers involving necessity and judgment. - use the subjunctive after volition, emotion, and doubt. - contrast indicative and subjunctive meaning. ## lesson sequence 1. forming the subjunctive 2. important irregulars 3. necessity and judgment 4. volition, emotion, and doubt ## recommended workflow 1. read one unit in `lessons.md`. 2. say every french example aloud twice. 3. cover the examples and rewrite them from memory. 4. complete the matching transformation in `exercises.md`. 5. check `answer-key.md` only after finishing. 6. record the rule behind each error. 7. follow the spaced plan in `review-plan.md`. ## mastery standard continue when you can score at least 85%, produce six original sentences, and explain your corrections without notes. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/lessons.md","title":"Chapter 16 Lessons: The Present Subjunctive","type":"Lessons","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-lessons-the-present-subjunctive\">Chapter 16 Lessons: The Present Subjunctive</h1>\n<p>Use short sessions. Accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-1-forming-the-subjunctive\">Unit 1: Forming the subjunctive</h2>\n<p>For many verbs, start from the ils present stem, remove -ent, and add -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code> — that I speak</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous finissions</code> — that we finish</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'ils attendent</code> — that they wait</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-2-important-irregulars\">Unit 2: Important irregulars</h2>\n<p>Master être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir, and common stem-changing forms.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je sois</code> — that I be</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que tu aies</code> — that you have</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'elle aille</code> — that she go</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous fassions</code> — that we do/make</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-3-necessity-and-judgment\">Unit 3: Necessity and judgment</h2>\n<p>Impersonal expressions such as il faut que, il est important que, and il vaut mieux que commonly trigger the subjunctive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez.</code> — You must come.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il est important qu'elle comprenne.</code> — It is important that she understand.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"unit-4-volition-emotion-and-doubt\">Unit 4: Volition, emotion, and doubt</h2>\n<p>A change of subject after vouloir, préférer, être content, avoir peur, douter, or a negative opinion often leads to que + subjunctive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"examples\">Examples</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code> — I want you to succeed.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes contents qu'il soit ici.</code> — We are happy that he is here.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'elle vienne.</code> — I do not think that she is coming.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"active-writing\">Active writing</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy each example once while saying it.</li>\n<li>Cover the examples.</li>\n<li>Rewrite all three from memory.</li>\n<li>Change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences.</li>\n<li>Read your new sentences aloud.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-production-task\">Chapter production task</h2>\n<p>Write eight recommendations for a friend using il faut que, je veux que, il est important que, and je suis content que.</p>\n<p>After writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-usage-practice\">Sentence usage practice</h2>\n<p>Use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. Read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.</code> — You must arrive before nine o'clock.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.</code> — I want my friends to be able to come to the party.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Nous sommes heureux qu'elle soit ici avec nous.</code> — We are happy that she is here with us.</li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je ne pense pas qu'il connaisse la réponse.</code> — I do not think that he knows the answer.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"notice-and-mark\">Notice and mark</h3>\n<p>For each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. Underline the word or phrase that controls its form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-the-sentence\">Change the sentence</h3>\n<p>Rewrite every sentence twice:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject, person, or number;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-it-personal\">Make it personal</h3>\n<p>Write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. Read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected.</p>","search":"# chapter 16 lessons: the present subjunctive use short sessions. accuracy from memory matters more than copying volume. ## unit 1: forming the subjunctive for many verbs, start from the ils present stem, remove -ent, and add -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent. ### examples - `que je parle` — that i speak - `que nous finissions` — that we finish - `qu'ils attendent` — that they wait ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 2: important irregulars master être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir, and common stem-changing forms. ### examples - `que je sois` — that i be - `que tu aies` — that you have - `qu'elle aille` — that she go - `que nous fassions` — that we do/make ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 3: necessity and judgment impersonal expressions such as il faut que, il est important que, and il vaut mieux que commonly trigger the subjunctive. ### examples - `il faut que vous veniez.` — you must come. - `il est important qu'elle comprenne.` — it is important that she understand. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## unit 4: volition, emotion, and doubt a change of subject after vouloir, préférer, être content, avoir peur, douter, or a negative opinion often leads to que + subjunctive. ### examples - `je veux que tu réussisses.` — i want you to succeed. - `nous sommes contents qu'il soit ici.` — we are happy that he is here. - `je ne pense pas qu'elle vienne.` — i do not think that she is coming. ### active writing 1. copy each example once while saying it. 2. cover the examples. 3. rewrite all three from memory. 4. change the subject, noun, or time expression to make three new sentences. 5. read your new sentences aloud. ## chapter production task write eight recommendations for a friend using il faut que, je veux que, il est important que, and je suis content que. after writing, circle conjugated verbs, underline agreement or pronoun choices, and read the passage aloud twice. ## sentence usage practice use these complete sentences to practice the chapter structures in context. read each sentence aloud twice, cover it, and write it from memory. 1. `il faut que vous arriviez avant neuf heures.` — you must arrive before nine o'clock. 2. `je veux que mes amis puissent venir à la fête.` — i want my friends to be able to come to the party. 3. `nous sommes heureux qu'elle soit ici avec nous.` — we are happy that she is here with us. 4. `je ne pense pas qu'il connaisse la réponse.` — i do not think that he knows the answer. ### notice and mark for each sentence, circle the target verb or grammar structure. underline the word or phrase that controls its form. ### change the sentence rewrite every sentence twice: 1. change the subject, person, or number; 2. change one meaningful detail such as the place, object, time, or description. make every required change to conjugation, agreement, articles, pronouns, and word order. ### make it personal write four new sentences about your own life using the same four chapter structures. read them aloud, check them, and rewrite only the parts you corrected. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/exercises.md","title":"Chapter 16 Exercises","type":"Exercises","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-exercises\">Chapter 16 Exercises</h1>\n<p>Complete these without notes. Put a question mark beside uncertain answers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-transform-or-translate\">A. Transform or translate</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>parler, que je</li>\n<li>finir, que nous</li>\n<li>être, qu'il</li>\n<li>Il faut / vous venir</li>\n<li>Je veux / tu réussir</li>\n<li>I am happy that she is here.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"b-controlled-production\">B. Controlled production</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>forming the subjunctive</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>important irregulars</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je sois</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>necessity and judgment</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n<li>Write a new sentence demonstrating <strong>volition, emotion, and doubt</strong>. Use <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code> only as a structural model; change its content.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"c-four-way-variation\">C. Four-way variation</h2>\n<p>Choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>change the subject;</li>\n<li>make it negative;</li>\n<li>turn it into a question;</li>\n<li>change one meaningful detail.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"d-dictation-and-correction\">D. Dictation and correction</h2>\n<p>Use text-to-speech to hear six examples from <code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">lessons.md</code>. Write what you hear without looking. Correct in separate passes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>vocabulary;</li>\n<li>verb form;</li>\n<li>agreement or pronoun placement;</li>\n<li>spelling and accents.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"e-personal-writing\">E. Personal writing</h2>\n<p>Write eight recommendations for a friend using il faut que, je veux que, il est important que, and je suis content que.</p>\n<h2 id=\"f-oral-recall\">F. Oral recall</h2>\n<p>Explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple English, then give one French example from memory.</p>","search":"# chapter 16 exercises complete these without notes. put a question mark beside uncertain answers. ## a. transform or translate 1. parler, que je 2. finir, que nous 3. être, qu'il 4. il faut / vous venir 5. je veux / tu réussir 6. i am happy that she is here. ## b. controlled production 1. write a new sentence demonstrating **forming the subjunctive**. use `que je parle` only as a structural model; change its content. 2. write a new sentence demonstrating **important irregulars**. use `que je sois` only as a structural model; change its content. 3. write a new sentence demonstrating **necessity and judgment**. use `il faut que vous veniez.` only as a structural model; change its content. 4. write a new sentence demonstrating **volition, emotion, and doubt**. use `je veux que tu réussisses.` only as a structural model; change its content. ## c. four-way variation choose one sentence from each lesson unit and rewrite it four ways: 1. change the subject; 2. make it negative; 3. turn it into a question; 4. change one meaningful detail. ## d. dictation and correction use text-to-speech to hear six examples from `lessons.md`. write what you hear without looking. correct in separate passes: 1. vocabulary; 2. verb form; 3. agreement or pronoun placement; 4. spelling and accents. ## e. personal writing write eight recommendations for a friend using il faut que, je veux que, il est important que, and je suis content que. ## f. oral recall explain each of the chapter's four main rules aloud in simple english, then give one french example from memory. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/answer-key.md","title":"Chapter 16 Answer Key","type":"Answers","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-answer-key\">Chapter 16 Answer Key</h1>\n<h2 id=\"section-a\">Section A</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que je parle</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">que nous finissions</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">qu'il soit</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Il faut que vous veniez.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je veux que tu réussisses.</code></li>\n<li><code class=\"speakable\" lang=\"fr\">Je suis content qu'elle soit ici.</code></li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"section-b\">Section B</h2>\n<p>Answers vary. A correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sections-c-f\">Sections C-F</h2>\n<p>These are productive tasks. Check them using this rule summary:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Forming the subjunctive:</strong> For many verbs, start from the ils present stem, remove -ent, and add -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent.</li>\n<li><strong>Important irregulars:</strong> Master être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir, and common stem-changing forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Necessity and judgment:</strong> Impersonal expressions such as il faut que, il est important que, and il vaut mieux que commonly trigger the subjunctive.</li>\n<li><strong>Volition, emotion, and doubt:</strong> A change of subject after vouloir, préférer, être content, avoir peur, douter, or a negative opinion often leads to que + subjunctive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"self-scoring\">Self-scoring</h2>\n<p>Award one point for each Section A answer and two points for each structurally correct Section B sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>85-100%:</strong> proceed and schedule delayed review.</li>\n<li><strong>70-84%:</strong> review only missed rules and retry the next day.</li>\n<li><strong>Below 70%:</strong> repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day.</p>","search":"# chapter 16 answer key ## section a 1. `que je parle` 2. `que nous finissions` 3. `qu'il soit` 4. `il faut que vous veniez.` 5. `je veux que tu réussisses.` 6. `je suis content qu'elle soit ici.` ## section b answers vary. a correct sentence must clearly demonstrate the named structure, contain a properly conjugated verb where required, and preserve agreement and word order. ## sections c-f these are productive tasks. check them using this rule summary: - **forming the subjunctive:** for many verbs, start from the ils present stem, remove -ent, and add -e, -es, -e, -ions, -iez, -ent. - **important irregulars:** master être, avoir, aller, faire, pouvoir, savoir, vouloir, and common stem-changing forms. - **necessity and judgment:** impersonal expressions such as il faut que, il est important que, and il vaut mieux que commonly trigger the subjunctive. - **volition, emotion, and doubt:** a change of subject after vouloir, préférer, être content, avoir peur, douter, or a negative opinion often leads to que + subjunctive. ## self-scoring award one point for each section a answer and two points for each structurally correct section b sentence. - **85-100%:** proceed and schedule delayed review. - **70-84%:** review only missed rules and retry the next day. - **below 70%:** repeat the lesson in two smaller sessions. a corrected answer is not mastered until you can produce a different correct example on a later day. "},{"id":"chapter-16-subjunctive/review-plan.md","title":"Chapter 16 Seven-Day Review Plan","type":"Review","html":"<h1 id=\"chapter-16-seven-day-review-plan\">Chapter 16 Seven-Day Review Plan</h1>\n<h2 id=\"day-1\">Day 1</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Forming the subjunctive</strong>. Say examples, then write them from memory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-2\">Day 2</h2>\n<p>Recall Day 1 without notes. Study <strong>Important irregulars</strong> and complete the first three transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-3\">Day 3</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Necessity and judgment</strong>. Create four original examples and read them aloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-4\">Day 4</h2>\n<p>Study <strong>Volition, emotion, and doubt</strong>. Complete the remaining transformations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-5\">Day 5</h2>\n<p>Complete controlled production and four-way variation. Review only recorded mistakes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-6\">Day 6</h2>\n<p>Do dictation and the chapter production task. Read your writing aloud twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"day-7\">Day 7</h2>\n<p>Redo Section A closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spaced-reviews\">Spaced reviews</h2>\n<p>Repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on Days 10, 14, and 30:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>write the key forms or rule from memory;</li>\n<li>transform three sentences;</li>\n<li>write two original sentences;</li>\n<li>review only errors.</li>\n</ol>","search":"# chapter 16 seven-day review plan ## day 1 study **forming the subjunctive**. say examples, then write them from memory. ## day 2 recall day 1 without notes. study **important irregulars** and complete the first three transformations. ## day 3 study **necessity and judgment**. create four original examples and read them aloud. ## day 4 study **volition, emotion, and doubt**. complete the remaining transformations. ## day 5 complete controlled production and four-way variation. review only recorded mistakes. ## day 6 do dictation and the chapter production task. read your writing aloud twice. ## day 7 redo section a closed-book, explain all four rules aloud, and produce six new sentences. ## spaced reviews repeat a ten-minute closed-book check on days 10, 14, and 30: 1. write the key forms or rule from memory; 2. transform three sentences; 3. write two original sentences; 4. review only errors. "}]}]};
